# Kernel.World — the World, its one transition `World.step`, and `fold`

`World.admit` IS the receive path (law 1): the Host commits exactly what it returns. A log is a
list of steps (KERNEL-47): an admitted Turn, or a recorded refusal (`World.refuse`, KERNEL-44: an
authenticated, in-window refusal, charged to its member's window budget, final for its transaction
id). `World.step` is the one transition of a log; the follower and a reopening Host fold the log
with it, and nothing else changes a World.

The kernel is generic over the digest `D` and the hash (`Env`) so that theorems can state a
hash collision as an explicit disjunct, and the simulation can run toy parameters through the
same `admit`. There is one deployed `Env` (CODEC's digest and hash).

Order of the checks `checkWith` makes (each refuses by name, nothing is written before all pass):
bounds → window (an expired retry is `outsideWindow`, whether or not its entry was pruned:
KERNEL-33) → journal (an exact retry inside its window is `replayed`) → shape → touches
(ONE lookup per touched cell: its cell pin (`stale`), then its write or retirement, and a written
value cell's decoding and floor) → value (born value cells, then every denomination's balance and
floor sums: LEDGER-2) → nullifiers → rows remain (a retired parent holds no row) → ranges (each range
read's relation, width, length and listing pin (`stale`)) → rows (each row touch in order, a pinned
row's pin (`stale`) among its checks, then a retired parent's heads) → charge → created ids → the
step counter. On the Host's path `Receive.submit` has already compared every pin before deriving the
Turn (KERNEL-66, `checkPins`), so there a `stale` from admission is not reached; a log's Turn (the
follower's fold) meets these three comparisons here.
-/
import Kernel.Check
import Kernel.Turn
import Kernel.Value
import Kernel.Trie
import Codec.Raw

namespace Nanodregg.Kernel

/-- The hash and the canonical encodings the kernel digests. CODEC supplies the deployed one. -/
structure Env (D : Type) where
  /-- the hash under a domain (deployed: cSHAKE256 with the domain as customization string,
  CODEC-7), so domains are separated by the hash, not by byte prefixes -/
  hash : (domain : String) → ByteArray → D
  /-- the digest's canonical bytes (32 for the deployed digest) -/
  bytes : D → ByteArray
  turnBytes : Turn D → ByteArray
  cellBytes : Cell → ByteArray

/-- A cell id's slot in the World: a live cell with its version, or the tombstone of a retired one.
The version (KERNEL-54) counts the cell's writes: 0 at its birth, one more per write (`touchCheck`),
refused past `Bounds.counterLimit` by name (`overBound .version`). A pin names it, so a pin observed on
the fold and stale now is BEHIND for ever: no World the fold reaches holds it again, up to a collision
on the pin inputs (`stale_behind_forever`). A pin naming a FUTURE version (AHEAD) is stale now and may
match later: it is not final.
A tomb carries none: it is terminal (an id is never reborn, `checkCreates`; nothing removes an entry). -/
inductive Entry
  | live (cell : Cell) (ver : Nat)
  | tomb
  deriving DecidableEq

/-- Every refusal, by name (law 7). -/
inductive Reject (D : Type)
  | overBound (which : Bound)
  /-- the transaction is already in the journal, admitted at `height` (an exact retry: the
  transaction id is the digest of the signed call, so the same id is the same call) -/
  | replayed (height : Nat)
  /-- a cell is touched twice in one turn -/
  | duplicateTouch (cell : D)
  | duplicateNullifier (nullifier : D)
  | duplicateKey (cell : D) (key : Nat)
  | nonCanonicalCell (index : Nat)
  | outsideWindow
  /-- the cell is not what the signer saw: its entry's pin (`pinOf`, version included) differs. Never
  recorded (KERNEL-54): a pin observed on the fold and stale now (BEHIND) is stale at every World the
  fold reaches, up to a collision on the pin inputs (`stale_behind_forever`; for rows and ranges, or
  the parent is retired), so it is final without a record; a pin naming a future version (AHEAD) is
  declined too, and is not final -/
  | stale (cell : D)
  | spent (nullifier : D)
  | chargeMismatch (expected : Nat)
  | overAllowance
  | idTaken (cell : D)
  /-- a born ROM cell whose id is not its content digest (`romIdOf`): a package's identity IS its
  id (KERNEL-3), and the Turn carries ids explicitly (D5), so admit checks it -/
  | notContentId (cell : D)
  /-- a born id is also touched: a born cell did not exist to be observed -/
  | bornTouched (cell : D)
  | missingCell (cell : D)
  | romWrite (cell : D)
  | romRetire (cell : D)
  | appendOnly (cell : D) (key : Nat)
  /-- a value cell the turn leaves behind does not decode (`Value.ofCell`) -/
  | valueMalformed (cell : D)
  /-- a value cell the turn leaves behind is below minus its floor -/
  | belowFloor (cell : D)
  /-- the turn changes a denomination's balance sum -/
  | notConserved (denom : ByteArray)
  /-- the turn changes a denomination's floor sum, and does not bear the denomination -/
  | floorNotConserved (denom : ByteArray)
  /-- the transaction id holds a recorded refusal (KERNEL-44, KERNEL-47 amendment 6): a refused call
  is final within its window, so it is never admitted later (a pin returning A→B→A changes nothing);
  `code` is the recorded refusal's code -/
  | refusedFinal (code : Nat)
  /-- the member's authenticated, in-window refusals this window have reached
  `Bounds.refusalsPerWindow`: the refusal is not recorded (it is NOT a verdict on the call) -/
  | refusalBudget
  /-- an inserted row's key is taken, or a declared head exists (KERNEL-46; binding on the current World) -/
  | keyTaken (parent : D)
  /-- the relation holds `limit` rows -/
  | relationFull (parent : D)
  /-- the relation's `amt` sum would pass its `cap` (LANG-45 (ii)) -/
  | relationCapped (parent : D)
  /-- a row or range names a parent the turn does not pin -/
  | rowParentUnpinned (parent : D)
  /-- a row names a parent that is not live -/
  | orphanRow (parent : D)
  /-- a retired parent still holds rows -/
  | rowsRemain (parent : D)
  /-- a row key's width is not its relation's -/
  | keyWidth (parent : D)
  /-- no head: the relation was never declared -/
  | noRelation (parent : D)
  /-- a retract in a relation declared without retract -/
  | retractForbidden (parent : D)
  /-- a head declared outside the turn that bears its parent -/
  | declareLate (parent : D)
  /-- a range read's rows pass its `most` (refused, never truncated) -/
  | rangeTooLong (parent : D)
  /-- one row slot twice in a turn -/
  | duplicateRow (parent : D)
  /-- an act on the wrong slot kind, or a pin where none belongs or missing where one must be -/
  | rowShape (parent : D)
  /-- a retract of a row that is absent, pinned absent (KERNEL-54: its pin matched; it was `stale`) -/
  | missingRow (parent : D)
  /-- the World's authenticated, in-window refusals this window, all members together, have reached
  `Bounds.refusalsPerWindowMax` (KERNEL-55 R1): the refusal is not recorded (it is NOT a verdict on
  the call; a liveness cost on refusal finality until the window turns, never on admission) -/
  | refusalsFull
  /-- the member's admitted ticks this window, with this Turn's, would pass `Bounds.ticksPerWindow`
  (DESIGN-OBS §1.3, the meter): a rate limit on compute as `refusalBudget` is on refusals; recorded
  (class (A): the call was signed and its pins compared) and final within its window -/
  | ticksBudget (member : D)
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

/-- The refusal's name on the wire (law 7): its constructor's, arguments dropped
(`Reject.name_injective`). -/
def Reject.name {D : Type} : Reject D → String
  | .overBound _ => "overBound"
  | .replayed _ => "replayed"
  | .duplicateTouch _ => "duplicateTouch"
  | .duplicateNullifier _ => "duplicateNullifier"
  | .duplicateKey _ _ => "duplicateKey"
  | .nonCanonicalCell _ => "nonCanonicalCell"
  | .outsideWindow => "outsideWindow"
  | .stale _ => "stale"
  | .spent _ => "spent"
  | .chargeMismatch _ => "chargeMismatch"
  | .overAllowance => "overAllowance"
  | .idTaken _ => "idTaken"
  | .notContentId _ => "notContentId"
  | .bornTouched _ => "bornTouched"
  | .missingCell _ => "missingCell"
  | .romWrite _ => "romWrite"
  | .romRetire _ => "romRetire"
  | .appendOnly _ _ => "appendOnly"
  | .valueMalformed _ => "valueMalformed"
  | .belowFloor _ => "belowFloor"
  | .notConserved _ => "notConserved"
  | .floorNotConserved _ => "floorNotConserved"
  | .refusedFinal _ => "refusedFinal"
  | .refusalBudget => "refusalBudget"
  | .keyTaken _ => "keyTaken"
  | .relationFull _ => "relationFull"
  | .relationCapped _ => "relationCapped"
  | .rowParentUnpinned _ => "rowParentUnpinned"
  | .orphanRow _ => "orphanRow"
  | .rowsRemain _ => "rowsRemain"
  | .keyWidth _ => "keyWidth"
  | .noRelation _ => "noRelation"
  | .retractForbidden _ => "retractForbidden"
  | .declareLate _ => "declareLate"
  | .rangeTooLong _ => "rangeTooLong"
  | .duplicateRow _ => "duplicateRow"
  | .rowShape _ => "rowShape"
  | .missingRow _ => "missingRow"
/-
# Lang.Program — the language as KERNEL's `Receive.Program`: install check, laws, methods

`program bytesOf digestOf` instantiates `Kernel.Receive.Program D`. Every read goes through the
caller's `Pinned` view (so `receive_congr` covers LANG's reads): a package and every package in its
closure must be pinned, or the receiver refuses `unpinned`.

* `checkInstall` decodes the package (canonical bytes or a named decode refusal), loads its imports'
  closure exactly as a run does (each import pinned, decoded and RE-CHECKED by `admitContextual` at
  its own holes, depth one below the package's own load), and admits the package by that same
  admission over those interfaces (LANG-35, LANG-78).
* `run` loads the closure (each package once, depth ≤ `closureDepthMax`, bodies relocated to the
  closure's indices), decodes the state at the package's state type and the arguments at the
  method's argument type, runs `method state args req` under the call's tick budget and extracts
  its result (`resultTy` at the method's call slots: the calls, then the new state) under what is left of it; the calls are
  KERNEL `Body`s by `Lang.callsOf`.
* `judge` runs every law in force — the package's own, then each extra law package's — on
  `old → new`, each under `lawTicks`, each reading the slots at ITS state type (LANG-11). `refused r`
  is `lawDenied law (label ++ ": " ++ r)`; running out of ticks is `lawDenied law (label ++ ": exhausted")`.
-/
import Kernel.Receive.Call
import Lang.Law
import Lang.Wire.Data
import Lang.Read
import Lang.Call
import Lang.Rel
import Lang.LiveHoles

namespace Nanodregg.Lang

open Nanodregg.Kernel
open Nanodregg.Kernel.Receive

/-- How deep a closure's import graph may be. -/
def closureDepthMax : Nat := 8
/-- The longest reason a law may give, in bytes. -/
def reasonMax : Nat := Bounds.reasonMax

variable {D : Type} [DecidableEq D]

/-- A loaded closure: package ids in `lib` order, the relocated bodies, the packages. -/
structure Loaded where
  ids : List ByteArray
  lib : List Term
  pkgs : List Package

/-- The interfaces of a closure's packages, in `lib` order: the types `ref` gives them. -/
abbrev Loaded.ifaces (L : Loaded) : List Ty := L.pkgs.map (·.iface)

def reasonText (r : ByteArray) : String :=
  let r := r.extract 0 reasonMax
  (String.fromUTF8? r).getD s!"{r.size} bytes"

def faultText : Fault → String
  | .stuck => "stuck" | .overflow => "overflow" | .underflow => "underflow"
  | .divZero => "divZero" | .tooLarge => "tooLarge" | .ticks => "exhausted" | .stack => "stack"
  | .refused why => s!"refused {reasonText why}"

def decodeText (e : Codec.Refusal) : String := s!"decode {repr e}"

section
variable (bytesOf : D → ByteArray) (digestOf : ByteArray → Option D) (rc : ReceiptCheck)

/-- The package in a pinned package cell. The cell must be ROM (install's birth, `Receive.install`):
what a package id reads is then what it read at install, at every World a fold reaches
(`Kernel.Proofs.Frame.fold_rom`), so a description read at one World and an invoke planned at a
later one read the same package (HOSTING-43). A package cell of another discipline is
`malformedCell`. -/
def readPackage (look : Pinned D) (id : D) : Except (Refusal D) Package := do
  let cell ← readKind look id kindPackage
  if cell.discipline ≠ .rom then throw (.malformedCell id 0)
  match cell.slots.get 0 with
  | none => throw (.malformedCell id 0)
  | some b =>
    match Wire.decodePackage b with
    | .ok p => pure p
    | .error _ => throw (.malformedCell id 0)

def idOf (b : ByteArray) : Except (Refusal D) D :=
  match digestOf b with
  | some d => .ok d
  | none => .error (.program "import is not a digest")

/-- Every digest in `ds` is a digest of the World (LANG-38: an id enters a run only as one). -/
def digestsOk (ds : List ByteArray) : Bool := ds.all fun b => (digestOf b).isSome

/-- Install's and every load's id-literal check: the checked package, or `notDigest` when a
digest literal of its body is not a digest of the World (LANG-38). -/
def checkLits (p : Package) : Except (Refusal D) Unit :=
  if digestsOk digestOf p.body.digestLits then .ok () else .error (.program "notDigest")

def indexIn (b : ByteArray) : List ByteArray → Option Nat
  | [] => none
  | x :: xs => if x = b then some 0 else (indexIn b xs).map (· + 1)

/-- The interfaces of the closure's packages at positions `gs`. -/
def ifacesAt (pkgs : List Package) : List Nat → Option (List Ty)
  | [] => some []
  | g :: gs =>
    match pkgs[g]?, ifacesAt pkgs gs with
    | some P, some r => some (P.iface :: r)
    | _, _ => none

/-- Append package `p` (imports already loaded at `gis`) to the closure, RE-CHECKING it against its
imports' interfaces (decisions/lang.md LANG-16), its body relocated to the closure's indices. -/
def admitPackage (idb : ByteArray) (p : Package) (acc : Loaded) (gis : List Nat) :
    Except (Refusal D) (Loaded × Nat) :=
  match ifacesAt acc.pkgs gis with
  | none => .error (.program "closure")
  | some ifs =>
    match p.checkWith ifs with
    | .error e => .error (.program e.wire)
    | .ok () =>
      match checkLits digestOf p with
      | .error e => .error e
      | .ok () =>
      .ok ({ ids := acc.ids ++ [idb], lib := acc.lib ++ [p.body.relocate fun i => gis.getD i 0],
             pkgs := acc.pkgs ++ [p] }, acc.ids.length)

mutual
/-- Load package `idb` and its closure into `acc` (each package once); its index in the closure. -/
def loadUsing (admit : ByteArray → Package → Loaded → List Nat → Except (Refusal D) (Loaded × Nat)) (look : Pinned D) (fuel : Nat) (idb : ByteArray) (acc : Loaded) :
    Except (Refusal D) (Loaded × Nat) :=
  match indexIn idb acc.ids with
  | some i => .ok (acc, i)
  | none =>
    match fuel with
    | 0 => .error (.program "imports too deep")
    | fuel + 1 =>
      match idOf digestOf idb with
      | .error e => .error e
      | .ok id =>
        match readPackage look id with
        | .error e => .error e
        | .ok p =>
          match loadAllUsing admit look fuel p.imports acc with
          | .error e => .error e
          | .ok (acc', gis) => admit idb p acc' gis
termination_by (fuel, 0)

def loadAllUsing (admit : ByteArray → Package → Loaded → List Nat → Except (Refusal D) (Loaded × Nat)) (look : Pinned D) (fuel : Nat) (ds : List ByteArray) (acc : Loaded) :
    Except (Refusal D) (Loaded × List Nat) :=
  match ds with
  | [] => .ok (acc, [])
  | d :: ds =>
    match loadUsing admit look fuel d acc with
    | .error e => .error e
    | .ok (acc', g) =>
      match loadAllUsing admit look fuel ds acc' with
      | .error e => .error e
/-
# Protocol.Command.Machine — what `host follow` does with a member's argv

`start` parses the argv and answers the first step; `step` takes the continuation `k` and the
result of the step it asked for and answers the next one, with follow's World after absorbing
any log the result carried. A write runs

  sign(envelope) → host(log, envelope) → [absorb: every logged step re-derived, PROTOCOL-4]
  → (page again, from the page's step count, until it is the writer's tip)
  → plan on the caught-up World → sign(callMessage u) → submit(call) → done

and a `read` stops after the catch-up; `init` needs no World (sign twice, print a join token).
The first envelope is valid for follow's own height + window, every later one for the tip the last
page stated + window. A member more than a window behind is refused `expired` with the writer's
height and re-signs ONCE a command for that height + window (`reSign`, PROTOCOL-19); a thin member's
`adopt` envelopes do the same, first for one window from height 0 (a pin names a step count).

`rotate` asks custody to `precommit` after the catch-up, plans the rotation to custody's NEXT key
(precommitting the digest of its PENDING key) and has it signed by `signNext`; custody advances on
its own settlement of the submit (CLIENT-13).

`invite INPUT` (PROTOCOL-42) catches up, checks that INPUT makes the member's own World, pins the
writer's newest base as follow verified it itself (PROTOCOL-47, `pinOf`: follow's own World at the
base's step count, the digest of its bytes; `noBase` / `baseMismatch` otherwise) and has custody
sign the invitation; it prints `invite HEX`.

`adopt INVITE KEY` (thin consent, PROTOCOL-14 A) takes its pin from an invitation `Invite.accept`
accepts under the sponsor key KEY, and pages the writer's newest base with `read`, one
signed envelope per page, `Protocol.Thin.accept` deciding each page; the download so far lives in
follow's state (`Follow.download`, memory only), and an adopted base replaces follow's World and is
handed back as `Persist.adopt` for follow to seal as its own newest base. From there every command
catches up from the base's step count like any other.

The log is a list of STEPS (KERNEL-47): admitted Turns and recorded refusals. Log positions count
steps, and follow binds on (steps, chain), as the kernel's `fold_reaches_host` does. Envelope windows
stay in heights: the page states the writer's tip in both units (PROTOCOL-41).

`sync --to STEPS:CHAIN` / `--attest` / `--check ATTEST` (PROTOCOL-74, `Protocol.Head`): the catch-up
compares follow's own fold with another member's stated position as it passes it (`Head.anchorCheck`
on each page's steps, re-derived from the argv at every step like every plan: `syncAnchor`), and
refuses `forkedWorld STEPS` by name when the chain there is another; `--attest` has custody sign
follow's own head at the tip (`attestTip`: the message is `Head.message` of follow's OWN World,
never a result's) and prints `attest HEX`; `--check` verifies the attestation's signature and World
before anything is sent and its signer's current key at the tip (`Head.signer`).

`cancel CALL` (PROTOCOL-63) catches up, checks on follow's World that the cancel records there, has
custody sign it and relays it; the writer's final answer (`refused cancelled`, or `ok`: the call was
admitted) is its word, so follow catches up AGAIN and ends by its own settlement of the call
(`cancelSettled`, PROTOCOL-71): `cancelled TXID` only when its own World records the cancel.

`k` holds only the argv, the phase and the results so far; every plan is RE-DERIVED from follow's
World at each step and the bytes it signs are compared with the ones custody signed (`planMoved`),
so `k` cannot steer what is signed (HOST-9's `resume_rederives`). A plan is offered for signature
only if follow's own World admits the Turn it derives (consent: `Protocol.Proofs.Command`).
-/
import Kernel.Receive.Rederive
import Protocol.Command.Verb
import Protocol.Envelope
import Protocol.Invite
import Protocol.Head
import Protocol.Command.Describe
import Protocol.Receipt
import Protocol.Settle
import Protocol.Attempt
import Protocol.Command.Exit
import Lang.Program

namespace Nanodregg.Protocol.Command

open Nanodregg.Codec Nanodregg.Kernel Nanodregg.Kernel.Receive

/-- What one `host follow` knows about the member it serves. -/
structure Self (D : Type) where
  /-- the World this member was invited to (its genesis origin, KERNEL-24), from the member's own
  side — its own Store's genesis, built from the invitation — never from the Host it talks to.
  Every call and envelope it signs names it. -/
  world : D
  member : D
  /-- the member's World grant (observe; enroll, if it carries it), which its envelopes name and
  its enrolments spend (KERNEL-16) -/
  grant : D
  /-- how many heights a signed call or envelope stays valid -/
  window : Nat

/-- Why a command ends without its effect. -/
inductive Refusal (D : Type)
  | parse (r : ParseRefusal)
  /-- follow's own derivation refused the call -/
  | receive (r : Receive.Refusal D)
  /-- follow's own World rejects the derived Turn -/
  | reject (r : Reject D)
  /-- the Host refused, by its name -/
  | host (name : String)
  /-- the logged step at this log position does not re-derive on follow's World: an event that does
  not read (`eventOf`), or one `Receive.rederive` refuses `eventMismatch` (a call that does not
  decode, a Turn event that does not derive, a refusal event that `Receive.judge` does not record)
  (a kernel rejection of a derived Turn is refused by the kernel's own name, `reject`; a well-formed
  cancel event that `Receive.cancel` does not record by `receive cancelForged`: `ofVerdict`) -/
  | eventMismatch (step : Nat)
  | badState
  | badResult
  /-- follow's World moved between the plan custody signed and the submit -/
  | planMoved
  /-- read: a `KEY=VALUE` that does not hold -/
  | expect (key : Nat)
  | missingCell (cell : D)
  /-- the writer's stated step count and chain after a log page are not what follow's fold reached -/
  | chainMismatch (steps : Nat)
  /-- a log page carried more steps than `Bounds.pageStepsMax`, more than one follower record holds
  (HOST-41): refused before any of it is absorbed -/
  | pageTooLong (count : Nat)
  /-- the derivation asked for more cells than `Kernel.Bounds.touchesMax` -/
  | tooManyPins
  /-- a base download refused, by `Protocol.Thin`'s name -/
  | thin (r : Thin.Refusal)
  /-- custody refused the step, by its name (`CustodyRefusal`) -/
  | custody (r : CustodyRefusal)
  /-- `read OBJ ?`: some part of the object's description has no text (`Lang.Print`) -/
  | noText
  /-- an invitation refused by `Invite.accept`'s name, or one for another World (PROTOCOL-42) -/
  | invite (r : Invite.Refusal)
  /-- the writer refused the envelope `expired` at `height` after follow's one re-signed envelope
  of this command, or stated a height that a re-signed envelope would not cover (PROTOCOL-19) -/
  | expired (height : Nat)
  /-- a call's bytes that do not read back as the call: `receipt CALL` whose bytes do not decode as
  a signed call, or step 3's signed call whose canonical bytes do not decode to itself (the World
  would judge the call they decode to, not the one follow derived) -/
  | badCall
  /-- `read OBJ REL K`: the object's package declares no relation REL (a data object declares none) -/
  | unknownRelation (rel : String)
  /-- `read OBJ REL K`: K is past the relation's declared `limit` (PROTOCOL-51) -/
  | rangePastLimit (most : Nat)
  /-- `read OBJ REL K PREFIX`: PREFIX reads at none of the relation's prefix types -/
  | badPrefix
  /-- `invoke … --pin REL KEY`: KEY does not read at the relation's key type (CLIENT-35) -/
  | badKey
  /-- `sync --to` / `--check`: follow's own fold reached the anchor's step count with ANOTHER chain
  (`Head.anchorCheck`): this member and the member whose head that is hold two histories of one
  World (PROTOCOL-74; `Proofs.Head.forked_not_on_fold`). The page is refused and nothing of it
  kept, as a `chainMismatch` page is -/
  | forkedWorld (steps : Nat)
  /-- `sync --to` / `--check`: follow was already past the position when the command began. Its
  World holds only the current chain, so the comparison on the way cannot be made: the member
  BEHIND does it, or `host check STORE --anchor` on this member's own Store (PROTOCOL-74) -/
  | ahead (steps : Nat)
  /-- a page carried a member's post of ITS head of this World (`Std.head` with `world` this World's
  origin, `Head.selfPosts`) at a position follow's own fold reached on this page with ANOTHER chain
  (PROTOCOL-75; `Proofs.Head.forkedSelfPost_forked`). The page is KEPT (it is lawful on follow's
  fold), the command ends by name, and the post is the witness: its poster signed that head
  (`Proofs.Head.body_post`) -/
  | forkedPost (steps : Nat) (member : Option D)
  /-- `sync --check`: the attestation refused by `Protocol.Head`'s name -/
  | attest (r : Head.Refusal)

/-- A step answered to the client. -/
inductive Reply
  | done (exit : UInt8) (out err : ByteArray)
  | sign (message k : ByteArray)
  | host (op : Op) (payload k : ByteArray)
  | submit (call k : ByteArray)
  | precommit (k : ByteArray)
  | signNext (message k : ByteArray)
  | signIncoming (message k : ByteArray)
  deriving Inhabited

/-- The continuation a reply carries, if any. -/
def Reply.cont : Reply → Option ByteArray
  | .done .. => none
  | .sign _ k | .host _ _ k | .submit _ k | .precommit k | .signNext _ k | .signIncoming _ k => some k

variable {D : Type} [Ord D] [DecidableEq D]

/-- The refusal's name on the wire (law 7): the constructor, arguments dropped. PROTOCOL's own
constructors are named here; a receive refusal or a kernel rejection carries its owner's name
(`Receive.Refusal.name`, `Reject.name`, each with `name_injective`), and a parse refusal the
parser's. -/
def Refusal.name : Refusal D → String
  | .parse r => r.name
  | .receive r => r.name
  | .reject r => r.name

exec
/bin/zsh -lc "ssh -n hbox 'cd /tank/dregg-build/nanodregg/wt/DOCS-AUDIT && python3 -c '\\''from pathlib import Path
paths=[Path(\"Host/Serve.lean\"),*sorted(Path(\"Hosting/Resident\").glob(\"*.lean\")),*sorted(Path(\"Codec\").glob(\"*.lean\")),*sorted(Path(\"client/src\").glob(\"*.rs\")),*sorted(Path(\"launcher/src\").glob(\"*.rs\"))]
out=[]
for p in paths:
