Codec/Deployed.lean:6
/-!
# Codec.Deployed — the one deployed `Env` and `Ctx`

The kernel is generic in its digest and hash. This module fixes them for deployment: digests are
`Codec.Digest` (32 bytes), the hash is cSHAKE256 with the domain as customization (`Hash.digest`),
turns, cells and calls are encoded by `Codec.Kernel`, and signatures are checked by RFC 8032
Ed25519 (`Ed25519.verify`). LANG supplies the `Program`. The facts the kernel's theorems take as
premises are proved for this instance in `Codec.Proofs.Deployed`.
-/

Codec/Digest.lean:1
/-! # Digest — the one digest type

A digest is exactly 32 bytes. It is never a natural number: ordering, trie index
bits and equality are read off the bytes. Hash functions produce it
(`Codec.Hash`); codecs carry it as 32 raw bytes (`Codec.Stream.digest`). -/

Codec/Ed25519.lean:3
/-!
# Ed25519 — signature verification (RFC 8032 §5.1.7), cofactored

Field elements are ten unboxed `UInt64` limbs (radix 2^25.5). The product is generated
schoolbook arithmetic, folded by `2^255 ≡ 19`, and no intermediate exceeds 2^63. Points are
extended twisted-Edwards coordinates (RFC 8032 §5.1.4). Verification computes `[S]B - [k]A` in one
double-and-add pass (Shamir's trick) and checks `[8]([S]B - [k]A) = [8]R`. It refuses a
non-canonical `S` (≥ L), a point encoding with `y ≥ p`, and a point that does not decompress. Every
loop is structural on a bit count. RFC 8032 vectors: `Codec.Proofs.Ed25519.{Test1, Test2, Tampered}`.
-/

Codec/Hash.lean:4
/-!
# Hash — Keccak-f[1600], SHA3-256, cSHAKE256 and KMAC256 (FIPS 202, NIST SP 800-185)

The one hash family. `Hash.digest domain msg` is cSHAKE256 with `domain` as the customization
string: every digest the system forms is domain-separated by construction, because SP 800-185
encodes the customization with its length (`encodeString`), so no domain is a prefix of another.
`Hash.mac key domain msg` is KMAC256 with a 32-byte key: the Host's log and base seal.

The permutation works on 25 unboxed `UInt64` lanes of a structure, one round unrolled (generated from
the FIPS 202 ρ and π tables): no array access can fail, no lane is boxed, and there is no `partial def`. Every loop is structural on a block count. The conformance
vectors (FIPS 202 and SP 800-185 samples) are theorems in `Codec.Proofs.Hash`.
-/

Codec/Kernel.lean:6
/-!
# Codec.Kernel — the canonical bytes of KERNEL's values

One codec per kernel type, built from the one Stream family. Each is generic in the digest's own
codec `dc : Stream D` (the deployed 32-byte digest, or a simulation's toy digest). Kernel types carry
plain `Nat`, `List` and `ByteArray` with bounds checked in `World.admit`. So each codec is lawful on
the values within those bounds (`Codec.Proofs.Kernel`): every admitted Turn has exactly one spelling,
and every decoded value is within bounds.

Layouts (all integers little-endian, every count a u32, every byte string `u32 len ‖ bytes`):
* `Op`: tag 0 ‖ key u32 ‖ value, or tag 1 ‖ key u32.
* `Cell`: kind u32 ‖ discipline u8 ‖ slots (count ‖ (key u32 ‖ value)*).
* `Act`: tag 0 (keep), tag 1 ‖ ops, or tag 2 (retire). `Touch`: cell ‖ seen ‖ act.
* `Turn`: txId ‖ notBefore u64 ‖ validUntil u64 ‖ touches ‖ creates (id ‖ cell each, D5) ‖ nullifiers ‖ charge u64 ‖
  event ‖ rows ‖ ranges.
* Rows (KERNEL-46/48): `RowSlot` as `rowKeyBytes` (parent ‖ rel u8 ‖ kind u8 [‖ u8 len ‖ key]); `RowLeaf` on
  the World wire with its version and floor as u64 varints (`rowLeaf`, KERNEL-54), and as `rowLeafBytes`, the
  row pin's preimage, with both as u64 (`rowLeafHashed`); `RowAct`: a tag ‖ its fields; `RowTouch`: slot ‖ option seen ‖ act; `RangeRead`: parent ‖ rel u8 ‖
  u8 len ‖ prefix ‖ most u64 ‖ seen.
* `Call`: unsigned ‖ signature. `Unsigned`: member ‖ key ‖ notBefore ‖ validUntil ‖ pins ‖ maxCharge ‖
  body. `Body`: a tag (its constructor's index) ‖ its fields.
-/

Codec/Layout.lean:4
/-! # Layout — a frame's fields, as data

A `Layout` is a list of named fields. It is data, so the protocol table can carry it, the Rust
emitter can print a reader and writer for it, and `Layout.codec` turns it into the one `Stream`
that Lean speaks. Every byte-string field shares one bound `max`, the frame bound: a field cannot
be longer than the frame that carries it. -/

Codec/Path.lean:1
/-!
# Codec.Path — a sandbox path: clean components, joined by `/`

A path relative to an operator's root is a list of components; on the wire it is ONE byte string,
the components joined by `/` (47), so the Rust side hands exactly those bytes to the sandbox and
assembles nothing (law 2). A component is CLEAN: nonempty, at most `componentMax` bytes, not `.`
or `..`, and free of the `forbidden` bytes (`/` and NUL). Over clean components the join is
injective and `split` inverts it (`Codec.Proofs.Path`), so the wire spelling is canonical.

`clean`, `join` and `split` index the bytes directly (no `List UInt8`, law 3) and are structural on
the size, so they reduce in the kernel: a `decide` over a literal path checks. The Rust emitter
prints the same rule from `componentMax`, `forbidden` and `reserved`.
-/

Codec/Prime.lean:1
/-!
# Codec.Prime — modular exponentiation, for primality certificates (CODEC-23)

`powMod b e m n` is `b ^ e % m` for `e < 2 ^ n`, by square-and-multiply. It is structural on `n`, so the
kernel reduces it on literals: a Pratt certificate for the Ed25519 field prime `2^255 − 19` checks by
`decide +kernel` (`Codec.Proofs.Prime`).
-/

Codec/Raw.lean:3
/-!
# Raw — codecs over unbounded types, canonical on a domain

KERNEL's types carry plain `Nat`, `List` and `ByteArray` and check their bounds in `World.admit`
(`checkBounds`), not in the types. These codecs write any value, but read only values in their
domain: `nat64` reads naturals below 2^64, `rawBytes max` byte strings of at most `max` bytes, and
`rawList max c` lists of at most `max` elements of `c`'s domain. They are lawful ON that domain
(`Codec.Proofs.Raw`, `LawfulOn`): every value in the domain round-trips, and every value read is in the
domain and was spelled exactly one way. Outside the domain, `put` truncates, and nothing ever reads
such a value back.
-/

Codec/Refusal.lean:1
/-! # Codec refusals

Every way a decoder refuses, by name, with the byte offset at which it refused.
A refusal is never a value: a decoder returns `Except Refusal`, and the layer that
answers a request embeds `Refusal` in its own named refusal. -/

Codec/Sha512.lean:1
/-!
# Sha512 — SHA-512 (FIPS 180-4), for Ed25519 (RFC 8032)

Eight unboxed `UInt64` working variables in a structure; the 80-word schedule of one block is a
`Vector`; every loop is structural on a block or round count. Vectors: `Codec.Proofs.Sha512`.
-/

Codec/Stream.lean:4
/-! # Stream — the one codec family

A `Stream α` writes a value by appending its bytes to a buffer and reads one
value at an offset of a `ByteArray`, returning the offset after it. It never
copies its input to decode, never forms a `List UInt8`, and never re-encodes to
check itself: canonicity (`Codec.Stream.Lawful`, in `Codec.StreamLaws`) is a
theorem about each combinator, proved once.

Every codec carries its `Shape`. A tag is the hash of a label and a shape
(`Codec.Tag`), so a codec whose structure changes gets a new tag without anyone
editing a literal.

Integers are fixed width, little-endian. Lengths and counts are `u32` and every
variable-size value carries its bound in its type (`Bytes max`, `Bounded max α`):
a value past its bound cannot be built, so it cannot be written, and a length
past the bound is refused by name before anything is read. -/

Codec/Tag.lean:4
/-!
# Tag — content-addressed codec identities

A codec's tag is the digest of a label and the codec's `Shape`: `Tag.of label c =
Hash.digest "nanodregg/codec/tag/v1" (label ‖ shape bytes)`. Change a codec's structure and its
tag changes with it. Nobody edits a version literal, no census lists the tags, and no fingerprint is
pinned. A Store stamped with the tags of the codecs that wrote it refuses to open under codecs
with other tags: an old shape refuses, it is never reinterpreted. A `refine`'s name is the one part
the shape takes on trust: the shape cannot see inside a function. Its accepted set is pinned by its
lawfulness theorem (the refine census) and its meaning by the codec vectors, which go red when a
meaning changes under an unchanged tag (CODEC-28).
-/

Codec/Tree.lean:3
/-!
# Tree — the one recursive codec

A recursive type (LANG's terms and types) is encoded as a `Tree`: a node is a tag byte, a payload
written with the ordinary codecs, and its children. The recursion lives here, once, depth-bounded:
a reader nests at most `depth` levels, and anything deeper is refused (`tooDeep`) before it is
read. A type becomes a `Stream` with `Stream.viaTree`, a refinement of `tree`. Its author proves
only that `toTree` and `ofTree` are inverse (`refine_lawful`); the byte-level canonicity is
`tree_lawful`, proved here.

Wire: `u8 tag ‖ varint |payload| ‖ payload ‖ varint |children| ‖ child*` (CODEC-13: LEB128, shortest form,
so a small node's framing is three bytes).
-/

Codec/Varint.lean:3
/-!
# Varint — LEB128 naturals, shortest form only

A natural as base-128 digits, least significant first, each byte's top bit set when another byte
follows: 0–127 is one byte, below 2^14 two. Two widths share one reader (`getVarint width limit`):
`varint`, below 2^32 in at most five bytes (lengths, counts), and `varint64`, below 2^64 in at most
ten (KERNEL-54's per-cell versions and rows floors on the World wire). A decoder refuses an overlong
spelling (a last byte of 0 after a continuation, which a shorter spelling would have omitted), a byte
past the width, and a value at or past the limit. So every value has exactly one spelling
(`Codec.Proofs.Varint`).
-/

Codec/World.lean:3
/-!
# Codec.World — the canonical bytes of a whole World (the checkpoint, HOST-14's base)

`trie ‖ journal ‖ refusals ‖ rows ‖ height u64 ‖ steps u64 ‖ chain ‖ allowance u64 ‖ origin`. The World's four maps are tries
(KERNEL-23 cells and spent nullifiers; KERNEL-41 the journal, under `journalSpec`; KERNEL-44 the
refusals, under `refusalSpec`; KERNEL-48 the rows, under `rowSpec`), and each is written
as its listing (`Trie.T.toList`, left to right by key bits) and read back by `Trie.build` over that
listing (`listingAccept`): every node hash is recomputed, none is ever read from the wire (CODEC-19), and
a listing is refused by name unless it is exactly the rebuilt trie's own (no key twice in a leaf).
The cells' listing is refused `kernel/leaf-fit` unless every slot holds the leaf of its kind, and
`kernel/trie-order` unless it is the canonical trie's; the journal's is refused
`kernel/journal-order` unless it is the canonical journal trie's (at the deployed digest: strictly
ascending by transaction id, `Codec.Deployed.journalOrder_iff`); the refusals' `kernel/refusal-order`
likewise. So every map reads back EXACTLY
(`build_toList`), decode ∘ bytes is the identity (`world_lawfulOn`), and the bytes are a function of
the contents. The World is refused `kernel/journal` unless its journal is the window's (KERNEL-39's
`journalFits`, every entry at or below the height and inside the window, one entry per height), so a
decoded base carries `World.JournalOk` (`world_decode_journalOk`); `kernel/refusals` unless its refusals
are the window's (KERNEL-47's `refusalsFits`), so it carries `World.RefusalsOk`
(`world_decode_refusalsOk`); `kernel/steps` unless its height is at most its step count; and
`kernel/rows` / `kernel/rows-headed` / `kernel/rows-parented` unless its rows fit, every head counts its
rows and every relation's parent is live (KERNEL-48).
`world.size w` is the encoded length, computed from the entries without encoding (HOST's
`worldBytesMax`).
-/

Codec/WorldSize.lean:3
/-!
# Codec.WorldSize — the measures a World's encoded size is stated in (cv 01a150a3-2523)

The named numbers HOST reads for its `worldBytesMax` (`entrySlack`, `worldFixed`, `journalBytes`,
`refusalsBytes`) and the per-entry counts the exact size names; their theorems are
`Codec.Proofs.WorldSize`'s (`world_size_eq`, `world_size_le`). Specification measures, O(World), never
computed on the request path.
-/

client/src/bin/nd-supervise.rs:1
//! `nd-supervise SOCKET [--group GID [--peer UID]] (--hide|--read|--write|--exec|--connect PATH)… --
//! /ABS/PROGRAM [ARG]…` (`--peer`: only that uid in GID connects, others closed `peerRefused`)
//!
//! Binds SOCKET (mode 0600; with `--group GID`, mode 0660 group GID, refused unless SOCKET's
//! directory passes `custody::check_shared_dir` with group GID: HOSTING-17 §7's nd-hosted follower,
//! whose driver `nd` runs as another uid in that group), starts PROGRAM confined by `sandbox` (CLIENT-9: the child sees only
//! its program, the `--read`/`--write`/`--exec` paths and the platform floor; every `--hide` path,
//! the member's workspace first, must lie outside all of them, CLIENT-8), and moves frames between
//! the socket's connections and the child's stdio (`mux`). It exits when the child does, with the
//! child's status, or with one of its own (`Protocol.Exit`, emitted: refusedAtLaunch,
//! childUnroutable, childSignaled); the service manager restarts the pair. Nothing is parsed but frame headers.
//! SIGTERM (nd-members removing a resident, a unit's stop) stops the child, so every peer reads
//! `closed unreachable` (CLIENT-30), and then ends the supervisor as SIGTERM's default action would.


client/src/bin/nd.rs:1
//! `nd ARG…` — one member command. The workspace is `$ND_WORKSPACE` (`seed`, `follow.sock`,
//! `host.sock`, `attempts/`). Every argument goes to `host follow` unread; `nd` performs the steps
//! follow returns and prints what follow says to print. The local verbs are custody, not protocol
//! (CLIENT-13): `nd --init` creates a member's keys, the next secret in `$ND_HOLDING` (a 0700
//! directory on removable media or under another account); `nd --init-hosted` creates a hosted
//! resident's one-record keys; `nd --init-observer` an observe-only follower driver's record,
//! which signs read envelopes only (CLIENT-22): a joined `--init-hosted` record sealed by
//! `nd --seal-observer` (a one-way rename); `nd --keys` prints custody's public keys (`key HEX`, `next HEX`)
//! for the operator's `host genesis`. `$ND_HOLDING` is read again only to rotate. `nd --join`: [`join`].
//! `nd --stdio WORKSPACE FOLLOW_SOCK HOST_SOCK` serves a hosted resident's commands on stdin
//! (nd-supervise's child, CLIENT-24: only STDIO_VERBS, no rotation). `$ND_FOLLOW_SOCK` / `$ND_HOST_SOCK` name the two sockets when they are not the workspace's
//! (HOSTING-17 §7: nd, as nd, drives the nd-hosted follower through its socket in /run/nd-follow).


launcher/src/bin/grain-launch.rs:1
//! `grain-launch ROOT BROKER MEMBER LABEL PIDS CONTROLLER [ARG...]`
//!
//! Starts CONTROLLER (the Lean `hosting-controller`, or the test's `hosting-drive`) confined, with
//! read access to `ROOT/worlds/LABEL/store` only, and two pipes: it reads action frames from the
//! controller's stdout and writes report frames to its stdin. Each launch is one
//! `nanodregg_sandbox::command` over the frame's plan joined onto ROOT, its `broker` sockets onto
//! BROKER (keybroker's socket root, HOSTING-17) and its `member` sockets onto MEMBER (where nd serves
//! each resident's member socket, HOSTING-20/25). The three roots must be pairwise disjoint trees,
//! or the launcher ends `rootsOverlap` before it starts anything. A launch naming a path that is not
//! its own grain's (a program, read, write or exec path outside `worlds/LABEL/grains/<grain>/`, or a
//! socket of either root other than `<grain>/<name>`) ends the run `foreignPath` (a defect in the
//! controller: another grain's socket is another grain's identity). Every child's argv is its plan's
//! paths as joined, field for field (`plan_argv`, HOSTING-27). Each child gets a PID file
//! `PIDS/<grain>.pid`, removed when the child is reaped. A kill stops the child by its own handle
//! (the PID in that file). When the controller's pipe closes, every child is killed and reaped,
//! then the launcher exits with a name on stderr. While idle it reports its clock (`tick`, every
//! `TICK_MS`, HOSTING-38). It decides nothing: a frame it cannot decode, or
//! a frame that breaks a law, ends the run by name.


launcher/src/bin/keybroker.rs:1
//! `keybroker KEYS ANCHORS PINS SOCKDIR PIDS UPSTREAM STORE OPERATOR PEER DECIDER`
//!
//! The provider-key broker's byte mover (HOSTING-12). It holds the operator's provider keys (the
//! file KEYS, read once under the custody checks of `nanodregg_sandbox::custody`) and the TLS trust
//! anchors (the PEM file ANCHORS, the operator's data: in production the installed copy of
//! `deploy/tls/anchors.pem`, read once under `custody::read_public`), the leaf-key pins (the file
//! PINS, the operator's data shaped like ANCHORS: `HOST SHA256-HEX` lines, the SHA-256 of the
//! provider's leaf certificate's SubjectPublicKeyInfo; in production the installed copy of
//! `deploy/tls/pins`, emitted from Lean's `Hosting.Pins.pins`, HOSTING-28), and serves one Unix
//! socket per resident, `SOCKDIR/<resident>/broker.sock` (HOSTING-17), for exactly the residents the
//! World holds: it asks its decider `residents` at start and every `TICK`, binds a socket for each
//! name newly answered (a resident grain's birth) and closes and removes the socket of each name no
//! longer answered (its removal) (HOSTING-26; no operator list). SOCKDIR is the operator's (nd:nd-hosted, mode 02710, every directory above
//! it StrictModes-clean); keybroker makes each resident's directory in it (02710, SOCKDIR's group by
//! the setgid bit), puts nothing there but the socket, and sets the socket 0660 (0600 when PEER is
//! keybroker's own uid: a one-uid box, where no group is needed and the sandbox's connect rule
//! accepts exactly 0600; HOSTING-17 §5, HOSTING-21). Anything else at
//! those paths ends it `sockDir` before any socket opens. On every accept the peer's credentials must
//! be uid PEER (the hosted uid) and SOCKDIR's group, or the connection is answered `peerRefused`.
//! Identity is the socket: keybroker puts a `decide` frame naming that socket's resident in front
//! of each ask, unread, and hands both to DECIDER (the Lean `hosting-broker`, started confined as
//! `DECIDER T0 SLOTS STORE OPERATOR`: the operator clock, the comma-separated key slots the key file
//! holds (so Lean refuses `keyMissing` before it charges anything), and the World it follows for
//! the allowances OPERATOR owns, which it may read and nothing else). A `grant` names a key slot and the
//! upstream request Lean printed; keybroker dials UPSTREAM (`addr:port`, the operator's dial
//! address), speaks TLS 1.3 (rustls with the ring provider, HOSTING-15) with SNI and certificate
//! verification for the HOST the grant names (Lean's provider table, never the dial address), then
//! checks that the verified leaf's key is one PINS names for that host (`pinMismatch` otherwise,
//! before any request byte, a host with no pin included), sends the request (HTTP/1.0, `Host:`
//! Lean's host) with the slot's key in the header Lean named, and hands the upstream's status and
//! body back to the decider (`reply`), which answers the reply TEXT Lean read from the provider's
//! JSON, the status, or `replyMalformed` (HOSTING-29): the resident never receives the provider's
//! body. A response holding the key is `keyEchoed` before Lean sees it, and a reply text holding it
//! is `keyEchoed` before the resident does (an escape can spell the key without its bytes). A TLS
//! failure is `tlsCertificate`, `tlsHostname` or `tlsProtocol`, never the upstream's text. It
//! decides nothing: every verdict is the decider's, and every way it ends is a name on stderr. One
//! ask at a time per resident (`busy`).


launcher/src/bin/nd-members.rs:1
//! `nd-members MEMBER MEMBERS STATE FOLLOW PIDS HOST_SOCK PEER SUPERVISE ND HOST STORE [--hide PATH]…
//! -- /ABS/DECIDER [ARG]…`
//!
//! Each resident's member socket, for exactly the resident grains the World holds (HOSTING-30,
//! CLIENT-29). It asks its decider `residents` at start and every `TICK` (the frames keybroker reads,
//! `residents::ask`). For each name answered whose member is provisioned it runs two existing
//! `nd-supervise` children, which bind, check peers, bound fds and name every close exactly as
//! nd-supervise does:
//! - the resident's own follower (a follower is ONE member's command machine: every call and
//!   envelope it plans names its MEMBER, so it is never shared), `FOLLOW/<name>/follow.sock` →
//!   `host follow STATE/<name>/store MEMBER GRANT`, MEMBER and GRANT read from the provisioning
//!   step's `STATE/<name>/follow` (`HEX HEX` and LF, 0600, chain-checked), confined to write that
//!   Store, with MEMBERS hidden;
//! - once that follower answers a command (after all its startup checks), `MEMBER/<name>/nd.sock` → `nd --stdio MEMBERS/<name>
//!   FOLLOW/<name>/follow.sock HOST_SOCK`, confined to write the workspace and connect [HOST_SOCK,
//!   its own follow socket] (CLIENT-24), with STATE hidden: it reads the World only through the
//!   follow socket and can neither write nor read a follower Store or a `follow` file (HOSTING-32,
//!   cv 01a1507c-7245; `Hosting.Proofs.Plan.roots_disjoint`: MEMBERS and STATE are disjoint trees).
//!   The socket is 0660 for uid PEER in MEMBER's group (`--group --peer`), or 0600 when PEER is
//!   this uid (HOSTING-21).
//!
//! Each child hides STORE and every `--hide` path; each has a PID file in PIDS. A child that ends is
//! started again at most every `RESTART_AFTER`; past `STARTS_MAX` starts in `STARTS_WINDOW` the name
//! is `memberCrashLoop`, left unbound until the answer changes. A member not yet provisioned is
//! retried every tick and named once (`memberMissing`), so provisioning just after the birth
//! binds it within a tick. A name no longer answered loses both sockets, both directories and both
//! children, each SIGTERMed to name every peer's close (CLIENT-30), reaped on later ticks, SIGKILLed
//! past `GRACE`, counted until gone; a rebirth of its name waits. A resident's own trouble is a name
//! on stderr and that resident unbound, never an end (`memberMissing`, `socketDirShared`,
//! `memberCrashLoop`, `memberSpawn`, `memberStopping`, `memberStopForced`): one resident must not stop the others.
//! Its own ends are `members_exit` (Lean's `Hosting.MembersExit`); the decider's own end is passed
//! through, and a World past `RESIDENTS_MAX` ends it with the decider's code for that fact (7).


launcher/src/bin/probe.rs:1
//! The launcher tests' grain (`launcher/tests/isolation.rs`, `launcher/tests/resident.rs`). Run as
//! `<grain>/pkg/probe`, with no arguments and an empty environment (the sandbox clears both), it
//! reads `probe.targets` beside itself (lines `NAME OP PATH`), tries each in order, and appends one
//! line per target to `<grain>/vol/probe.out` as soon as it is tried, so a test can follow it:
//! `NAME ok` (a `list` prints `ok` and the sorted names, comma-separated) or `NAME denied <errno
//! name>`. The ops:
//! - `read`, `write`, `list`: the file system;
//! - `connect`: open a unix socket (a resident's broker socket, HOSTING-17);
//! - `ask`: connect to a broker socket and send one ask (model `claude-m`, 10 tokens, `hello`),
//!   then print `ok reply <text>` (the reply text the Lean decider read, HOSTING-29), `ok status
//!   <status>` or `ok refused <broker refusal>`. Every byte received is
//!   appended to `<grain>/vol/probe.recv`, so the test can check that no key reached the grain;
//! - `wait`: wait (at most `WAIT`) until the file PATH exists, so a test can sequence the grain
//!   with what it does outside (`ok`, or `denied timeout`);
//! - `nd`: `NAME nd SOCK WORD...`: connect to a member socket (`nd --stdio` behind nd-supervise,
//!   HOSTING-20) and send the WORDs as one command (the mux child wire, the client's own frame
//!   and layout code), then print `ok <exit> <stdout>` (or `<stderr>` when the exit is not OK),
//!   newlines as `|`.
//!
//! It finds itself by argv[0] (no /proc under bwrap). Exit 3 when it cannot write its own volume.
//!
//! Run in `nd`'s place by nd-members (`launcher/tests/members.rs`, HOSTING-32) it is started as
//! `probe --stdio WS FOLLOW_SOCK HOST_SOCK`: then its directory is the member workspace WS, the one
//! path that child may write: `WS/probe.targets` in, `WS/probe.out` out, so a test reads what nd's
//! own confinement lets it touch.



