- **Pattern [M].** Every teardown was forced by money or a wall and came before a verdict was in hand. Commits survived every time. Verdicts and evidence did not.

### 4.8 What the record says the drift was (one paragraph)
The expensive losses were not lost commits. Every teardown and wall kept the git refs.

What was lost was **verdicts and attention**:
- 33 lanes killed in one hour on 10-04, half of it on overage;
- two root context deaths that orphaned 62 and 15 lanes;
- a 37.7-h pause behind a wall estimated at 9 h;
- 90 finished commits held off main for ~52 h behind a `next` branch that no landing ever judged (Ember, b379#3959: "we never should have been working on a side branch!");
- 27 hand relays through the Mac for want of a deploy key;
- a ~9% (26% at peak) bookkeeping tax of pin, ledger and fixture commits.

The renewal discipline improved measurably from ce77 to b379. Successors now start from their own continuation files, and the 10-14 false wall cost zero state. The integration discipline did not improve: the parallel-lane semantic conflicts (Loaded ×2, CellTable ×2) and the serial-gate/landing wall (2–3.5 h) remain.

