at follower and relays its log asks to `host.sock`, so
   there is no new component. **The principle: the trusted side initiates to the less-trusted side,
   never the reverse.** keybroker (nd) serves sockets the hosted group connects to, and that is the one
   direction where the less-trusted side initiates. There it is the asking itself (the resident must be
   able to ask), and it is bounded by the peer check, one socket per resident, and Lean's verdict. OPS-3
   renders `nd-follow-drive` per hosted follower.

8. **SOCKDIR is outside every grain's grant, by construction** (ROOT's condition, 10-18, from CLIENT-20's
   measurement: on Linux a directory a grain may read, write or exec is already a connect grant to every
   unix socket in it, sockets made there after launch included, because bwrap filters by mount, not by
   operation; the class of `EXEC_FILTERED = false`). Three facts make it so. (i) Lean's plan names only
   component lists relative to the hosting ROOT (`plan_paths`: every allowed path lies under the grain's
   own directory `worlds/<l>/grains/<id>`), and `connect` entries relative to the separate broker root
   BROKER; Lean never names an absolute path, so no plan can grant a read on `/run` or on anything above
   or beside its grain. (ii) The Linux floor (`sandbox::FLOOR`: /usr, /lib, /lib64, /bin,
   /etc/ld.so.cache) holds no `/run`. (iii) grain-launch refuses at start, by name, a BROKER that lies
   under or contains ROOT (they must be disjoint trees: `/run/nd-broker` against the `nd-hosted` hosting
   root, e.g. `/srv/nd-hosted`; in tests, two sibling `devtools::scratch` directories). CLIENT's resolver
   adds `ConnectOverlap` (a connect directory under or containing a read/write/exec entry) and
   `SocketInGrant` (a read/write/exec tree holding a socket at launch), and the sandbox binds exactly
   `BROKER/<id>` and nothing above it. What no check sees: a grain may bind a socket in its own `vol`
   after launch; only that grain sees its `vol`, so that socket reaches nobody else.

**Alternatives.** (a) ROOT's starting default: the socket owned by the resident's uid, 0600, under the
resident's own 0700 directory. The socket's owner is whoever binds it, and the binder is keybroker; for
the inode to be the resident's, keybroker would have to BE the resident's uid (the shared-uid hole above)
or receive listening descriptors bound by the hosting side over a control socket (SCM_RIGHTS: a control
protocol, re-registration on every keybroker restart, and the same impersonation exposure as below).
Kept from it: one socket per resident in its own directory, no shared directory, refusals by name for a
wrong owner or mode. Changed: the owner is the broker and access is the hosted group at 0660. (b) Grains
run as nd: refused (key theft on escape). (c) Abstract sockets: no file permissions at all. Refused. (d)
systemd `.socket` units: static, and residents come from the World. Refused. (e) A socket-file bind:
breaks on broker restart (4). (f) keybroker in group nd-hosted and an explicit chgrp: membership gives nd
the hosted group's files for nothing; the setgid directory does it with no membership. (g) A peer UID
argument instead of the group rule: one more operator argument for the same fact SOCKDIR's group states.

**CRITIQUE.** All grains share `nd-hosted`. A grain that escapes its sandbox can connect to EVERY
resident's socket (the group grants it; only the mount namespace withheld it) and spend any resident's
allowances, and it can read every resident's custody files. It still cannot read a provider key or write
the Store. Per-grain uids (subordinate uid ranges and a privileged mapper) are the fix, and they change the
grain design, not this decision. `peerRefused` keys on the peer's gid, which a process can drop only toward
less access, but root passes nothing and a root peer is refused, which is right. On one uid (every test on
hbox) the owner/0600 branch runs and the cross-uid branch, `ConnectOwner` and `peerRefused`, cannot
be produced; those are review-guarded until a two-uid box runs them (the plant for `peerRefused` uses a
SOCKDIR whose group is a supplementary group of the test user: every ask is refused). keybroker cannot tell a SOCKDIR whose
group is its own primary group (grains run as nd: the hole of the premise) from the one-uid test, so that
misconfiguration is the install's to refuse (OPS: the tmpfiles line names `nd-hosted`), not keybroker's. The fix is cv task
01a14aad-fc83 whose trigger is the first deployment that hosts grains for more than one
principal. **The controller runs as `nd-hosted` against its OWN follower Store** (ROOT, 10-18): a `host
follow` running as `nd-hosted`, its Store under an `nd-hosted` 0700 chain, which the controller tails (law
5: the follower is the replayer). It never reads the writer's files or their key. HOST-9 agrees the
follower's socket path shape. `peerRefused`'s uid half is planted on one uid by configuring PEER as
another uid (every ask is then refused): the same comparison as a wrong-uid peer, which one uid cannot
produce. The resident's own nd (its custody,
the ASSEMBLY's second process) connects to host.sock across the same uid boundary; that is CLIENT-12's
attach design, not this.
**META.** I started from ROOT's default and kept its shape until the binder question broke it, which is
the anchoring a default invites; the reason to leave it is a concrete hole (escape = keys), not taste. I
reached for the group-plus-setgid idiom by familiarity with how mail spools and X sockets do it; the test is
whether the per-uid fix (CRITIQUE) arrives and makes the group unnecessary, and then this decision is
deleted, not kept beside it.
**Custody.** This widens who may connect to keybroker from uid nd to the `nd-hosted` group, and narrows
what a hosted process can read (no key, no Store write). ROOT APPROVED 10-18 with the
conditions folded in above (the peer uid, the named residue and its cv task, the StrictModes check of
SOCKDIR and each per-resident directory, `connectMax` = 2, the controller on its own follower Store), and
puts it to Ember to ratify as a custody change.
**Flag day.** keybroker gains PEER and its SOCKDIR layout changes (`<r>.sock` → `<r>/broker.sock`), KeybrokerExit gains
`sockDir`, Broker.Refusal gains `peerRefused` (broker_generated.rs re-emitted), the launch frame gains
`connect` (generated.rs re-emitted), grain-launch gains BROKER, the keybroker unit is re-rendered. Nothing
persisted changes.
**Built (HOSTING-6, 10-18).** keybroker's half: 45b1394. The resident plan: `Plan.connect` (relative to BROKER),
`connectOf` (an app none, a resident `[[<id>, "broker.sock"]]`), `residentUnsupported` DELETED (a resident is now
planned), the launch frame's `connect` field, `grain-launch ROOT BROKER LABEL PIDS CONTROLLER` (connect joined onto
BROKER; `rootsOverlap` when the trees nest). Theorems `Proofs.Plan.plan_connect_own`, `plan_connect_disjoint`
(premise `idHex ≠ idHex'`, which `dirOf_injective` discharges for two World ids), `plan_connect_clean`, the tooth
`planned_resident_encodes`; `launch_fields` names `connect`. Physical: `launcher/tests/isolation.rs`
`a_resident_reaches_its_own_broker_socket_and_nothing_else` (VACUITY-READER-H4's hole 1: the connect grant is the
ONLY identity barrier between residents, since all share the hosted uid and group). Lean plans a resident through
`hosting-drive --resident`. Over a broker root that holds two residents' listening sockets and a file, the resident
connects to its own socket. Another resident's socket and the root's file are ENOENT. Its own directory lists exactly
`broker.sock`, and the broker root lists only its own directory, which is bwrap's mount scaffolding. CONTROL:
unconfined, the probe reaches both sockets and the file. Plants (run, each red): `policy` granting the whole broker
root (the reader's surviving mutation; the sandbox refuses a directory as a connect entry); connect joined onto ROOT;
a resident planned with no connect entry. A socket path must fit sun_path (107 bytes), and a resident's directory is 64
hex digits, so SOCKDIR must be at most 30 bytes long. `/run/nd-broker` is 14; the test uses a short scratch root.
**Residue (reader, hole 2).** keybroker binds the names the operator typed in RESIDENTS, and the plan names the
directory `dirOf` of the grain's id. They agree by convention only. cv 01a14ad0-ae99 (HOSTING-18) derives keybroker's
sockets from the World (the operator's unrevoked allowances' residents) and deletes RESIDENTS.


FILE decisions/kernel.md:3
## KERNEL-3 · ROM cells are content-addressed; a package's identity IS its cell id (2026-10-16)
**Decision.** A born ROM cell's id is `hash(rom-id tag ‖ cellBytes cell)`; any other born cell's id is
`hash(cell-id tag ‖ txId ‖ le32 i)`. `checkCreates` refuses a duplicate born id in one turn (`idTaken`).
LANG-5's package identity becomes the package cell's id: one identity, not "package digest" beside
"cell id". Reinstalling identical bytes is `idTaken`.
**Alternatives.** All ids derived from txId (package found through a name index: a second map); LANG's own
`H(package tag ‖ encode p)` stored in slot 0 and looked up by scan.
**Critique.** The id now depends on CODEC's `cellBytes` of the whole cell (kind + discipline + slots), not on
LANG's package encoding alone: a CODEC change to cell encoding moves every package id (a re-genesis, which
is free). **Meta-critique.** Content addressing is fashionable; the test is that imports must resolve to a
cell the caller pins, and only an id computable from content lets the caller pin it before install lands.
Status: open (LANG to confirm LANG-5 identity = `romIdOf`).


FILE decisions/kernel.md:16
## KERNEL-4 · Receive: one derivation per kind over a pinned view, ending in a sealed Turn (2026-10-16)
**Decision.** `Receive.receive X w c := receiveWith X (pinned w c.unsigned.pins) c`. A receiver sees state
ONLY through `Pinned D := D → Except Refusal (Option Cell)`, which refuses `unpinned` for any id the caller
did not pin. Kinds v0: `install | create | invoke | grant | revoke`; every kind ends in `sealTurn`, which
sets the window and pins from the call, the call bytes as `event`, `charge := weight`, and refuses
`overPrice` above the signed `maxCharge`. LANG plugs in as `Program D` (`checkInstall`, `checkCreate`, `run`),
each reading through the same pinned view. Authority: `Req {agent, principal}`; laws judge by it.
Grants: a grant cell (kind 3, appendOnly, retirable) names target, grantee, issuer, optional parent, and
method slots; `invoke … via g` walks ≤ `grantDepthMax` links (each pinned, live, on target, held by the
next agent, granting the method) and acts for the root issuer; `revoke` retires a grant and every chain
through it dies. Attenuation only narrows (`grantWidens`).
Theorems: `receive_congr` (two Worlds agreeing on the pinned cells derive the same result — consent
determinism), `receive_ok` (signed, `charge = weight ≤ maxCharge`, pins/window/event/txId are the call's),
`sealTurn_ok`, `receiveWith_sealed`.
**Alternatives.** Receivers over the whole World (minidregg: a receiver may read anything, so consent must
replay everything); LANG performing its own state access (two access paths).
**Critique.** Requiring a pin for every read makes clients compute the read set before signing — for an
invoke whose law reads a cell chosen by data, the client must run the method first (it can: `host follow`
is the same code). A grant chain of depth 8 means up to 9 pins per call. Authority by raw signer key is
temporary: members/keys/rotation (`enroll`, `rotate`, `unknownKey`, `notPrecommitted`) are the next commit.
**Meta-critique.** "Reads only pinned cells" is the property that makes HOST-7's `host follow` consent exact;
I may be designing for that elegance and underweighting the client cost of pinning.
Status: open.


FILE decisions/kernel.md:40
## KERNEL-5 · Answers: LANG law refusals, PROTOCOL refusal names (2026-10-16)
- LANG ruling "law results carry a refusal NAME": `Receive.Refusal.lawDenied (law : D) (reason : String)`,
  produced by LANG through `Program`. Laws run in `receive`, never in `World.admit`, so `Kernel.Reject` does
  not grow (a law refusal in the admit path would mean the follower re-runs laws: it does not).
- PROTOCOL §2: `lawDenied` is the constructor above; `noGrant` is the constructor for "the signer is not
  the grantee of the grant it acts through" (renamed from notGrantee); `stale`, `replayed`, `spent` are
  `Kernel.Reject`. `unknownKey` and `notPrecommitted` arrive with members/rotation (next commit): a
  constructor nothing produces would be a no-op shape (CLAUDE.md), so it lands with its producer.
Status: open.


FILE decisions/kernel.md:50
## KERNEL-6 · Members, keys, rotation, rights, data objects, follow grants, audit (2026-10-16)
**Decision.** (a) Identity is a member cell (kind 4: current key, digest of the next key, sponsor). A call
names `member` and the `key` it signs with; `unknownKey` unless it is the member's current key. `rotate`
is signed by the INCOMING key, whose digest must be the precommitted one (`notPrecommitted`): a stolen
current key cannot rotate. `enroll` is signed by a sponsoring member and carries the new key's
possession signature (`badPossession`). (b) Rights are `observe | mutate | invoke m`; a grant carries
rights as slots; attenuation only narrows (`grantWidens`). (c) Objects: slot 0 package (absent = a data
object), 1 owner, 2..15 extra laws, state at 16+. `write`/`law` act on data objects only (`hasMethods`),
by the owner or a grant chain whose root issuer is the owner (`noGrant`); `invoke` acts on programmed
objects only (`noMethods`), principal = signer or root issuer. Every state change is judged by
`Program.judge` with the laws in force in the pre-state (a law change is judged by the old laws:
"deny-all refuses the repair"). (d) Follow grants (kind 5) gate `log`/`read` (`mayFollow`, a live chain
to a founder's root); founders and their root grants ARE the genesis World (`Receive.genesis`).
(e) `audit` re-derives every logged Turn from its decoded event; `audit_ok`: audit accepts ⇔ fold
accepts ∧ the log is `Honest` (every Turn is what its event derives on the World before it).
**Alternatives.** Raw keys as identity (rotation breaks every grant); `write` as a method of a built-in
package (needs LANG for the most basic row); laws judged by the post-state's laws (lets a law change
license itself); a follower that trusts the writer's derivations (minidregg's follower).
**Critique.** Any member may sponsor: the World is open to whoever one member admits, and the sponsor
pays. Laws judged by pre-state laws make "tighten the law" always possible only if the current law
permits it — a deny-all object is frozen forever (the row wants exactly that, and it is a footgun).
`audit` re-runs LANG for every invoke: an audit is as expensive as the writer's work, by design.
**Meta-critique.** Much of this is shaped by PROTOCOL's rows (unknownKey after rotation, deny-all repair):
I am building to the tests. Check: each rule also falls out of "authority is a member's, keys are its
current credential" independent of the rows.
Status: open.


FILE decisions/kernel.md:77
## KERNEL-7 · A retry is `replayed` by transaction id alone; `Receive.submit`; the Sim (2026-10-16)
**Decision.** (a) `txConflict` is deleted. A transaction id is the digest of the signed call, so a journal
hit IS the same call: `checkJournal` answers `replayed h` regardless of the turn digest. (b)
`Receive.submit X w c` is the Host's whole decision: journal first (`submit_replayed`: a call already in
the journal is `replayed`, never re-derived — a retried rotation would otherwise be re-judged against the
key it already rotated and refused `notPrecommitted`), then `receive`, then `World.admit` (`submit_ok`).
(c) `Kernel/Sim.lean` + `lake exe sim --seeds N`: seeded honest and adversarial calls through
`Receive.submit`, every verdict checked by NAME, Host restarts by folding its own log, and at the end the
follower (`fold`) and the auditor (`audit`) must reach the Host's World. 64 seeds: 2749 admitted, 5307
refused as expected, 472 restarts, 0 failing. Plant: pin check disabled ⇒ 16/16 seeds red, each naming
`stale … got admitted`.
**Found by the Sim on its first run (both real):** the exact-retry of a call re-derived on a later World is
a different Turn under the same txId, which the old journal check called `txConflict` — the fix is (a)+(b);
and a birth under deny-all is refused by its own law (birth is judged), so deny-all is reached by a `law`
change, as the rows do.
**Critique.** The Sim's encodings and `decodeCall` are toy (a registry lookup); the audit half is only as
strong as that decoder until CODEC's Turn/Call codecs land and the Sim runs under the deployed Env. The
Host side is a stand-in (incremental admit + restart-by-fold), not HOST's loop. **Meta-critique.** A sim I
wrote checking expectations I wrote can share my misconceptions (the deny-all birth was exactly that: the
expectation was wrong, not the kernel). The plant shows the checks bite; it does not show they are complete.
Status: open.


