//! `nd ARG…` — one member command. The workspace is `$ND_WORKSPACE` (`seed`, `follow.sock`,
//! `host.sock`, `attempts/`). Every argument goes to `host follow` unread; `nd` performs the steps
//! follow returns and prints what follow says to print. The local verbs are custody, not protocol
//! (CLIENT-13): `nd --init` creates a member's keys, the next secret in `$ND_HOLDING` (a 0700
//! directory on removable media or under another account); `nd --init-hosted` creates a hosted
//! resident's one-record keys; `nd --init-observer` an observe-only follower driver's record,
//! which signs read envelopes only (CLIENT-22): a joined `--init-hosted` record sealed by
//! `nd --seal-observer` (a one-way rename); `nd --keys` prints custody's public keys (`key HEX`, `next HEX`)
//! for the operator's `host genesis`. `$ND_HOLDING` is read again only to rotate. `nd --join`: [`join`].
//! `nd --stdio WORKSPACE FOLLOW_SOCK HOST_SOCK` serves a hosted resident's commands on stdin
//! (nd-supervise's child, CLIENT-24: only STDIO_VERBS, no rotation). `$ND_FOLLOW_SOCK` / `$ND_HOST_SOCK` name the two sockets when they are not the workspace's
//! (HOSTING-17 §7: nd, as nd, drives the nd-hosted follower through its socket in /run/nd-follow).

use std::ffi::OsString;
use std::io::Write;
use std::os::unix::ffi::OsStringExt;
use std::path::PathBuf;
use std::process::ExitCode;

use nanodregg_client::custody::Custody;
use nanodregg_client::generated::{FOLLOW_SOCK, HOST_SOCK};
use nanodregg_client::sockets::{FollowSocket, HostSocket};
use nanodregg_client::table;
use nanodregg_client::{join, run, stdio};

fn main() -> ExitCode {
    match go() {
        Ok(code) => ExitCode::from(code),
        Err(e) => {
            nanodregg_sandbox::say("nd", &e);
            // nd's own failure is its own class, never follow's `uncertain` (PROTOCOL-7).
            ExitCode::from(nanodregg_client::generated::command_exit::CLIENT)
        }
    }
}

fn go() -> Result<u8, String> {
    let argv: Vec<OsString> = std::env::args_os().skip(1).collect();
    if let Some(served) = stdio::from_argv(&argv) {
        return served;
    }
    let ws = PathBuf::from(std::env::var_os("ND_WORKSPACE").ok_or("ND_WORKSPACE is not set")?);
    let holding = std::env::var_os("ND_HOLDING").map(PathBuf::from);
    if argv.len() == 1
        && (argv[0] == "--init" || argv[0] == "--init-hosted" || argv[0] == "--init-observer")
    {
        nd_dir(&ws.join("attempts"))?;
        let made = match holding {
            _ if argv[0] == "--init-hosted" => Custody::create_same_record(&ws),
            _ if argv[0] == "--init-observer" => Custody::create_observer(&ws),
            Some(h) => Custody::create_separate(&ws, &h),
            None => return Err("nd --init: ND_HOLDING is not set (CLIENT-13)".into()),
        };
        made.map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;
        return Ok(0);
    }
    if argv.len() == 1 && argv[0] == "--seal-observer" {
        Custody::seal_observer(&ws).map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;
        return Ok(0);
    }
    let custody = Custody::open(&ws, holding.clone()).map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;
    if let Some(done) = join::from_argv(&ws, holding.as_deref(), &custody, &argv) {
        return done;
    }
    if argv.len() == 1 && argv[0] == "--keys" {
        let hex = |b: &[u8]| b.iter().map(|x| format!("{x:02x}")).collect::<String>();
        let key = custody.public_key().map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;
        let next = custody.next_public_key().map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;
        println!("key {}\nnext {}", hex(&key), hex(&next));
        return Ok(0);
    }
    let sock =
        |var: &str, name: &str| std::env::var_os(var).map_or_else(|| ws.join(name), PathBuf::from);
    let mut follow = FollowSocket::connect(&sock("ND_FOLLOW_SOCK", FOLLOW_SOCK), table::REPLY)
        .map_err(|e| format!("{FOLLOW_SOCK}: {e}"))?;
    let mut host = HostSocket::connect(&sock("ND_HOST_SOCK", HOST_SOCK), table::REPLY)
        .map_err(|e| format!("{HOST_SOCK}: {e}"))?;
    let argv = join::kept(&ws, argv)?;
    let argv: Vec<Vec<u8>> = argv.into_iter().map(OsString::into_vec).collect();
    let (exit, stdout, stderr) = run::run(
        &argv,
        &mut follow,
        &mut host,
        &custody,
        &ws.join("attempts"),
        table::RUN,
    )
    .map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;
    let _ = std::io::stdout().write_all(&stdout);
    let _ = std::io::stderr().write_all(&stderr);
    Ok(exit)
}

/// `mkdir -p` at 0700 whatever the umask: the workspace is the member's alone (custody's chain).
fn nd_dir(p: &std::path::Path) -> Result<(), String> {
    std::os::unix::fs::DirBuilderExt::mode(std::fs::DirBuilder::new().recursive(true), 0o700)
        .create(p)
        .map_err(|e| e.to_string())
}
# deploy/testground — the testground's hosted World (DESIGN-TESTGROUND §3.2; CLIENT-43)

The page (`testground/`, published by Ember) is a member's client: its follow runs in the tab. A
hosted World swaps one function: the member's writer frames go over a WebSocket to a real `host serve`.
Three processes on the writer's box, each with a PID file (`run.sh`) or a unit (`units/`):

| process | what | listens |
|---|---|---|
| `nd-supervise … -- host serve STORE` | the writer, under the supervisor's mux (bounds, deadlines, named closes) | `STATE/run/host.sock` (0600) |
| `node testground/guestbook.mjs serve STATE/gb STATE/run/host.sock` | founder 0, a member like any other: signs the invitation (`world.json`), enrols each visitor's join token (`enroll`, `grant world … observe`) with its own follow re-deriving | `STATE/gb/guestbook.sock` |
| `python3 ws-relay.py` | WebSocket ↔ host.sock, bytes only (parses no frame, no op); `GET /world.json`; `POST /enroll` → the guest-book | `127.0.0.1:PORT` |

To turn it on (hbox, MEASURED 10-26 in `wt/testground-b`, PORT 18787 behind an ssh tunnel):

    testground/build/build.sh c WORK && testground/build/build.sh wasm WORK   # kernel.{mjs,wasm} beside guestbook.mjs
    lake build host && cargo build --release -p nanodregg-client --bin nd-supervise
    deploy/testground/run.sh genesis STATE      # the guest-book's seeds (0600) and `host genesis`
    PORT=8787 deploy/testground/run.sh start STATE
    deploy/testground/run.sh stop STATE         # each process by its PID file

Then expose `127.0.0.1:PORT` as `wss://HOST/ws` (TLS and the proxy are the operator's act; nothing
here listens beyond loopback) and open the page as `…/testground/?hosted=wss://HOST/ws&join`. Each tab
joins over the invitation, under the sponsor key `world.json` names (`Invite.accept`), is enrolled,
and is then a member; a second tab is a second member of the same World.

**What a visitor trusts.** Its own follow, checking every reply the writer sends. **The caveat**: the
kernel and the sponsor key come from whoever serves the page and `world.json`; until the kernel's hash
is reproducible from source and the sponsor key travels by a channel the visitor already trusts
(RUNBOOK §2b's two channels), this demonstrates the check, it is not a trust anchor. Once TRUST-SEED's
certified heads land, a visitor can check the writer's certified head instead of one sponsor's word.

**Abuse.** The relay bounds its connections (`--max-conns`); each connection is one host.sock
connection under the mux's bounds. How many visitors the guest-book enrols is the kernel's to bound
(its World grant's enrolments; the meter, KERNEL-68), and a guest-book that ran out refuses by name.

