/-
# Protocol.Command.Verb — the verbs of the one command language, and its one parser

`nd VERB ARG…` is parsed here and nowhere else: not in Rust (law 2), not in the rows (a row's argv
goes through this same `parse`). Every verb is a constructor of `Verb` (the SIZE ratchet counts
them, ceiling 20), and every way an argv can be wrong is a constructor of `ParseRefusal`.

Arguments: a digest (a cell, a member, a grant, a package) is lowercase hex of its canonical
bytes; a number is decimal; a value is taken as its UTF-8 bytes; a package is its TEXT form, read
by LANG's one reader (`Lang.Read.readPackage`) and installed as its canonical bytes; a join token
(what `init` prints) is lowercase hex. A member names objects by id: petnames are not part of the
language (a member's own index of names is a later row, not a guess now).
-/
import Kernel.Receive.Call
import Protocol.Table
import Protocol.Command.Text
import Lang.Read
import Protocol.Thin
import Protocol.Invite

namespace Nanodregg.Protocol.Command

open Nanodregg.Kernel Nanodregg.Kernel.Receive

/-- Every verb. -/
inductive Verb
  /-- `init SPONSOR`: a join token for SPONSOR to enroll (key, next-key digest, possession) -/
  | init
  /-- `enroll TOKEN`: sponsor a new member -/
  | enroll
  /-- `install TEXT`: install the package TEXT reads to (LANG's text form) -/
  | install
  /-- `create LAW…`: birth a data object owned by the signer, judged by the laws;
  `create --pkg PKG STATE LAW…`: birth an object programmed by package PKG, its seed STATE read
  (at the package's state type, by LANG's reader, on follow's World) -/
  | create
  /-- `write OBJ KEY VALUE [--via GRANT]`: set one state slot -/
  | write
  /-- `law OBJ LAW… [--via GRANT]`: replace a data object's laws -/
  | law
  /-- `invoke OBJ METHOD ARGS [--range REL K [PREFIX]] [--pin REL KEY]... [--via GRANT]`: run a
  method; ARGS is read at the method's argument type (LANG's reader, on follow's World); a method
  that reads a relation by range is handed the range `--range` names, signed at follow's listing;
  each `--pin REL KEY` pins the row of relation REL at KEY (LANG text at the relation's key type) at
  follow's World, so a method's POINT read of it is handed that row and, on a `(live)` read, its key's
  cell liveness (CLIENT-35) -/
  | invoke
  /-- `grant TARGET MEMBER RIGHTS [--via PARENT]`: TARGET is an object, a value account
  (`well:ASSET` | `of:OWNER:ASSET`: a `transfer:N` grant over it lends at most N of its balance, a
  `credit:N` grant over a well at most N of its floor, KERNEL-60), or `world` (the World,
  KERNEL-20: `observe` on it is following the log, `enroll:N` is sponsoring N members); MEMBER is a
  member or `anyone` (a grant whose grantee is the World: every enrolled member, including members
  enrolled later, KERNEL-26: a public object); RIGHTS is
  `observe`, `mutate`, `invoke:N`, `enroll:N`, `transfer:N`, `credit:N`, comma-separated; PARENT is the
  grant attenuated -/
  | grant
  /-- `revoke GRANT` -/
  | revoke
  /-- `read OBJ [KEY=VALUE…]`: print the object's state slots; each `KEY=VALUE` must hold.
  `read OBJ ?`: print the object's self-description, its methods with their indices and argument
  types, its laws and its relations (`Command.Describe`, PROTOCOL-44).
  `read OBJ REL K [PREFIX]`: print the rows of relation REL of the object's package whose key
  starts with PREFIX (LANG text of the key's first fields; none: every row), at most K, one per
  line in key-byte order (PROTOCOL-51) -/
  | read
  /-- `rotate`: move the member to custody's precommitted NEXT key, precommitting the one after -/
  | rotate
  /-- `adopt INVITE KEY`: start follow from the writer's base the invitation INVITE pins, accepted only
  if INVITE is signed by KEY, the sponsor key the member trusts out of band (`Invite.accept`), and the
  base's World bytes hash to the pin (thin consent, PROTOCOL-14; PROTOCOL-42) -/
  | adopt
  /-- `sync`: catch up to the writer's tip and print the height and chain reached (a follower driven
  by a timer, HOSTING-17 §7; it signs envelopes only). `sync --to STEPS:CHAIN`: catch up THROUGH
  another member's stated position and compare (PROTOCOL-74): `shared STEPS` when follow's own fold
  holds that chain at that step count, `refused forkedWorld` when it holds another (the page refused),
  `refused ahead` when follow was already past it, `unreached STEPS` when the writer's tip is below it.
  `sync --attest`: catch up, then sign follow's own head (`Protocol.Head`) and print `attest HEX`.
  `sync --check ATTEST`: `--to` at the attested position, with the attestation's signature, World
  and signer checked by name (`badAttestation`, `otherWorld`, `badHeadSignature`, `notMember`,
  `staleKey`); prints `shared STEPS MEMBER`. `sync --post OBJ`: catch up, then post follow's own
  World's head (chain, height, roots, steps) to the member's head object OBJ (`Std.head`, PROTOCOL-75:
  `invoke OBJ post`, planned at the tip; every follow that absorbs it compares) -/
  | sync
  /-- `issue CAP`: bear a new denomination; its WELL is a value account owned by the signer whose
  id is the denomination, with floor `-CAP` (LEDGER-2) -/
  | issue
  /-- `account ASSET [--owner OWNER]`: open the empty account of denomination ASSET owned by OWNER
  (default: the signer), at its keyed id (LEDGER-7) -/
  | account
  /-- `transfer SRC DST AMOUNT [--via GRANT] [--once HEX]`: move AMOUNT from SRC to DST; an
  account is `well:ASSET` or `of:OWNER:ASSET`; `--once` spends a once-only key of SRC -/
  | transfer
  /-- `credit WELL ACCT AMOUNT [--via GRANT]`: extend a credit line of AMOUNT out of WELL's floor to
  ACCT, with the issuer's authority (LEDGER-4) -/
  | credit
  /-- `invite INPUT`: catch up, then sign an invitation to this World: INPUT (the World's origin
  inputs, which must make the member's own World), the pin of the member's World as it stands, and
  the member's id and key (PROTOCOL-42); prints `invite HEX` -/
  | invite
  /-- `receipt CALL`: catch up, then print a RECEIPT for the signed call's transaction id against
  follow's World (its roots and the trie paths that show it committed, refused or absent,
  `Protocol.Receipt`, PROTOCOL-45), the head it was built at, and the member's settlement of the
  call (`Protocol.Settle.ofReceipt`, PROTOCOL-46): `receipt HEX`, `head STEPS HEIGHT ROOTS`,
  `settle VERDICT`. The attempt store's `confirm` and `probe` (`Protocol.Attempt`) -/
  | receipt
  /-- `cancel CALL`: catch up, then sign a cancel of the member's own signed call CALL (the member's
  signature over `domCancel ‖ txId`, `Ctx.cancelMessage`) and send it to the writer as the `cancel`
  op (KERNEL-60's record C1): the writer records the call `refused cancelled`, final, inside the
  call's window, so a loose call (sent, outcome unknown, window still live) can be closed by its
  member without waiting out the window. Prints `cancelled HEX`, the call's transaction id -/
  | cancel
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

namespace Verb

def all : List Verb :=
  [init, enroll, install, create, write, law, invoke, grant, revoke, read, rotate, adopt, sync, issue, account,
   transfer, credit, invite, receipt, cancel]

def word : Verb → String
  | init => "init" | enroll => "enroll" | install => "install"
  | create => "create" | write => "write" | law => "law" | invoke => "invoke"
  | grant => "grant" | revoke => "revoke" | read => "read" | rotate => "rotate" | adopt => "adopt"
  | sync => "sync" | issue => "issue" | account => "account" | transfer => "transfer" | credit => "credit"
  | invite => "invite" | receipt => "receipt" | cancel => "cancel"

def ofWord? (s : String) : Option Verb := all.find? (·.word == s)

end Verb

/-- A join token: what a newcomer hands its sponsor. -/
structure Join (D : Type) where
  key : ByteArray
  next : D
  possession : ByteArray
  deriving DecidableEq

/-- What a grant is over: the World, an object (or any cell) by id, or a value account by its
name (`well:ASSET` | `of:OWNER:ASSET`, resolved to its id when the call is planned). -/
inductive GrantTarget (D : Type)
  | world
  | cell (id : D)
  | account (r : AcctRef D)

/-- What an `invoke` names for its plan to sign, as TEXT read on follow's World when the call is
planned: `range` is `--range REL K [PREFIX]`, the relation, at most K rows and the prefix TEXT of
the one range the method reads (`read OBJ REL K [PREFIX]`'s arguments, in its order); `points` are
the `--pin REL KEY`s, each a relation and the TEXT of one key of it (CLIENT-35). -/
structure InvokeReads where
  range : Option (String × Nat × Option String) := none
  points : List (String × String) := []
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

/-- What `sync` relates follow's fold to (PROTOCOL-74): the writer's tip alone; another member's
stated position (its step count and chain bytes, as a page states them); follow's own head, to
attest; or another member's attestation, its bytes decoded by the machine (`Protocol.Head`). -/
inductive SyncMode
  | tip
  | to (steps : Nat) (chain : ByteArray)
  | attest
  | check (attestation : ByteArray)
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

/-- A parsed command. -/
inductive Cmd (D : Type)
  | init (sponsor : D)
  | enroll (join : Join D)
  | install (pkg : ByteArray)
  /-- `pkg` = the package and the seed state's TEXT -/
  | create (pkg : Option (D × String)) (laws : List D)
  | write (obj : D) (key : Nat) (value : ByteArray) (via : Option D)
  | law (obj : D) (laws : List D) (via : Option D)
  /-- `args` is TEXT, read at the method's argument type when the call is planned; `reads` are the
  reads the plan signs beside the ones the derivation asks for (`InvokeReads`) -/
  | invoke (obj : D) (method : Nat) (args : String) (reads : InvokeReads) (via : Option D)
  /-- `grantee = none` is `anyone` (the World as grantee, KERNEL-26) -/
  | grant (target : GrantTarget D) (grantee : Option D) (rights : List Right) (via : Option D)
  | revoke (grant : D)
  | read (obj : D) (expect : List (Nat × ByteArray))
  /-- `read OBJ ?` -/
  | describe (obj : D)
  /-- `read OBJ REL K [PREFIX]`: `pre` is TEXT, read at the relation's prefix type when the read is
  answered (on follow's World, where the package is) -/
  | rangeRead (obj : D) (rel : String) (most : Nat) (pre : Option String)
  | rotate
  /-- the invitation's bytes and the sponsor key the member trusts -/
  | adopt (invite key : ByteArray)
  | sync (mode : SyncMode)
  /-- `sync --post OBJ` (PROTOCOL-75): `invoke OBJ post` with follow's OWN World's head
  (`Protocol.Head.headText`), planned at the tip -/
  | post (obj : D)
  | issue (cap : Nat)
  /-- `owner = none` is the signer -/
  | account (asset : D) (owner : Option D)
  | transfer (src dst : AcctRef D) (amount : Nat) (via : Option D) (once : Option ByteArray)
  | credit (well : D) (acct : AcctRef D) (amount : Nat) (via : Option D)
  /-- the World's signed-start inputs (`Invite.startInput` bytes) -/
  | invite (input : ByteArray)
  /-- the signed call's bytes (`Ctx.callBytes`), decoded by the machine -/
  | receipt (call : ByteArray)
  /-- the signed call's bytes (`Ctx.callBytes`) of the call to cancel, decoded by the machine -/
  | cancel (call : ByteArray)

/-- Every way an argv is refused. -/
inductive ParseRefusal
  /-- the argv's encoding, `4 + Σ (4 + len)`, is past `Bounds.argvBytesMax` -/
  | argvPastBound (size : Nat)
  | notUtf8 (index : Nat)
  | noVerb
  | unknownVerb (word : String)
  /-- the verb got the wrong arguments; `usage` is its grammar -/
  | usage (verb : String) (usage : String)
  | badDigest (arg : String)
  | badNumber (arg : String)
  | badRight (arg : String)
  | badToken (arg : String)
  | badExpect (arg : String)
  /-- `install`'s text does not read as a package in bounds; LANG's reason -/
  | badPackage (why : String)
  /-- an account is neither `well:ASSET` nor `of:OWNER:ASSET` -/
  | badAccount (arg : String)
  /-- a `--once` key is not hex, or is empty -/
  | badOnce (arg : String)
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

/-- The refusal's name on the wire (law 7): the constructor, without its arguments. Distinct
constructors have distinct names (`ParseRefusal.name_injective`). -/
def ParseRefusal.name : ParseRefusal → String
  | .argvPastBound _ => "argvPastBound" | .notUtf8 _ => "notUtf8" | .noVerb => "noVerb"
  | .unknownVerb _ => "unknownVerb" | .usage _ _ => "usage" | .badDigest _ => "badDigest"
  | .badNumber _ => "badNumber" | .badRight _ => "badRight" | .badToken _ => "badToken"
  | .badExpect _ => "badExpect" | .badPackage _ => "badPackage" | .badAccount _ => "badAccount"
  | .badOnce _ => "badOnce"

/-- The constructor's index, the thing a name must determine. -/
def ParseRefusal.tag : ParseRefusal → Nat
  | .argvPastBound _ => 0 | .notUtf8 _ => 1 | .noVerb => 2 | .unknownVerb _ => 3 | .usage _ _ => 4
  | .badDigest _ => 5 | .badNumber _ => 6 | .badRight _ => 7 | .badToken _ => 8 | .badExpect _ => 9
  | .badPackage _ => 10 | .badAccount _ => 11 | .badOnce _ => 12

def usage : Verb → String
  | .init => "init SPONSOR" | .enroll => "enroll TOKEN"
  | .install => "install TEXT" | .create => "create [--pkg PKG STATE] LAW..."
  | .write => "write OBJ KEY VALUE [--via GRANT]" | .law => "law OBJ LAW... [--via GRANT]"
  | .invoke => "invoke OBJ METHOD ARGS [--range REL K [PREFIX]] [--pin REL KEY]... [--via GRANT]"
  | .grant => "grant TARGET|ACCOUNT|world MEMBER|anyone RIGHTS [--via PARENT]"
  | .revoke => "revoke GRANT" | .read => "read OBJ [KEY=VALUE...] | read OBJ ? | read OBJ REL K [PREFIX]" | .rotate => "rotate"
  | .adopt => "adopt INVITE KEY" | .sync => "sync [--to STEPS:CHAIN | --attest | --check ATTEST | --post OBJ]"
  | .issue => "issue CAP"
  | .account => "account ASSET [--owner OWNER]"
  | .transfer => "transfer SRC DST AMOUNT [--via GRANT] [--once HEX]"
  | .credit => "credit WELL ACCT AMOUNT [--via GRANT]"
  | .invite => "invite INPUT"
  | .receipt => "receipt CALL"
  | .cancel => "cancel CALL"

def argvSize (argv : List ByteArray) : Nat := 4 + (argv.map (4 + ·.size)).sum

variable {D : Type}

/-- The join token's bytes: `key ‖ next ‖ possession`, each `u32le len ‖ bytes`. -/
def Join.bytes (dbytes : D → ByteArray) (j : Join D) : ByteArray :=
  let f (b : ByteArray) := Codec.Stream.u32.bytes b.size.toUInt32 ++ b
  f j.key ++ f (dbytes j.next) ++ f j.possession

def Join.ofBytes (digestOf : ByteArray → Option D) (b : ByteArray) : Option (Join D) :=
  let field (i : Nat) : Option (ByteArray × Nat) :=
    match Codec.Stream.u32.get b i with
    | .ok (n, j) => if j + n.toNat ≤ b.size then some (b.extract j (j + n.toNat), j + n.toNat) else none
    | .error _ => none
  do
    let (key, i) ← field 0
    let (nb, i) ← field i
    let (possession, i) ← field i
    if i = b.size then some { key, next := ← digestOf nb, possession } else none

section
variable (digestOf : ByteArray → Option D)

def digestArg (s : String) : Except ParseRefusal D :=
  match Text.ofHex s >>= digestOf with
  | some d => .ok d
  | none => .error (.badDigest s)

def numArg (s : String) : Except ParseRefusal Nat :=
  match s.toNat? with
  | some n => .ok n
  | none => .error (.badNumber s)

def rightArg (s : String) : Except ParseRefusal Right :=
  if s = "observe" then .ok .observe
  else if s = "mutate" then .ok .mutate
  else match (s.dropPrefix? "invoke:").bind (·.toString.toNat?) with
    | some m => .ok (.invoke m)
    | none => match (s.dropPrefix? "enroll:").bind (·.toString.toNat?) with
      | some n => .ok (.enroll n)
      | none => match (s.dropPrefix? "transfer:").bind (·.toString.toNat?) with
        | some n => .ok (.transfer n)
        | none => match (s.dropPrefix? "credit:").bind (·.toString.toNat?) with
          | some n => .ok (.credit n)
          | none => .error (.badRight s)

def expectArg (s : String) : Except ParseRefusal (Nat × ByteArray) :=
  match s.splitOn "=" with
  | [k, v] => match k.toNat? with
    | some n => .ok (n, v.toUTF8)
    | none => .error (.badExpect s)
  | _ => .error (.badExpect s)

/-- A package's text, read by LANG's reader, as its canonical bytes. -/
def packageArg (t : String) : Except ParseRefusal ByteArray :=
  match Lang.Read.readPackage t with
  | .error e => .error (.badPackage e.text)
  | .ok p => match Lang.Wire.encodePackage p with
    | some b => .ok b
    | none => .error (.badPackage "past the package bounds")

/-- A trailing `--via GRANT`, or none. -/
def viaArg (v : Verb) : List String → Except ParseRefusal (Option D)
  | [] => .ok none
  | ["--via", g] => (digestArg digestOf g).map some
  | _ => .error (.usage v.word (usage v))

/-- An account reference: `well:ASSET` (the well, whose id is its denomination) or
`of:OWNER:ASSET` (OWNER's account of ASSET, resolved by the receiver to `accountId`). -/
def acctArg (s : String) : Except ParseRefusal (AcctRef D) :=
  match s.splitOn ":" with
  | ["well", a] => match Text.ofHex a >>= digestOf with
    | some a => .ok (.well a)
    | none => .error (.badAccount s)
  | ["of", o, a] => match Text.ofHex o >>= digestOf, Text.ofHex a >>= digestOf with
    | some o, some a => .ok (.of o a)
    | _, _ => .error (.badAccount s)
  | _ => .error (.badAccount s)

/-- Trailing `--FLAG VALUE` pairs, each flag at most once and only from `allowed`, in any order. -/
def flagsArg (v : Verb) (allowed : List String) : List String → Except ParseRefusal (List (String × String))
  | [] => .ok []
  | f :: x :: rest =>
      if allowed.contains f then do
        let fs ← flagsArg v allowed rest
        if fs.any (·.1 = f) then throw (.usage v.word (usage v)) else pure ((f, x) :: fs)
      else .error (.usage v.word (usage v))
  | [_] => .error (.usage v.word (usage v))

/-- The value of `--via` among parsed flags, as a grant. -/
def viaFlag (fs : List (String × String)) : Except ParseRefusal (Option D) :=
  match fs.lookup "--via" with
  | some g => (digestArg digestOf g).map some
  | none => .ok none

/-- `invoke`'s `--pin REL KEY`s, in order. -/
def pinsArg (v : Verb) : List String → Except ParseRefusal (List (String × String))
  | [] => .ok []
  | "--pin" :: r :: k :: rest => do pure ((r, k) :: (← pinsArg v rest))
  | _ => .error (.usage v.word (usage v))

/-- `invoke`'s `[--range REL K [PREFIX]] [--pin REL KEY]...`, what is left of its tail once `--via`
is split off. A PREFIX is the word after K unless that word is `--pin` (no LANG text is). -/
def readsArg (v : Verb) : List String → Except ParseRefusal InvokeReads
  | "--range" :: r :: k :: rest => do
      let n ← numArg k
      match rest with
      | [] => pure { range := some (r, n, none) }
      | "--pin" :: _ => pure { range := some (r, n, none), points := ← pinsArg v rest }
      | p :: rest' => pure { range := some (r, n, some p), points := ← pinsArg v rest' }
  | rest => do pure { points := ← pinsArg v rest }

/-- Split a trailing `--via GRANT` off a variable-length tail. -/
def splitVia (args : List String) : List String × List String :=
  match args.reverse with
  | g :: "--via" :: rest => (rest.reverse, ["--via", g])
  | _ => (args, [])

def parseVerb (v : Verb) (args : List String) : Except ParseRefusal (Cmd D) :=
  let bad : Except ParseRefusal (Cmd D) := .error (.usage v.word (usage v))
  match v, args with
  | .init, [s] => .init <$> digestArg digestOf s
  | .enroll, [t] =>
      match Text.ofHex t >>= Join.ofBytes digestOf with
      | some j => .ok (.enroll j)
      | none => .error (.badToken t)
  | .install, [t] => (.install ·) <$> packageArg t
  | .create, "--pkg" :: p :: st :: ls => do
      pure (.create (some (← digestArg digestOf p, st)) (← mapEx (digestArg digestOf) ls))
  | .create, ls => .create none <$> mapEx (digestArg digestOf) ls
  | .write, o :: k :: val :: via => do
      pure (.write (← digestArg digestOf o) (← numArg k) val.toUTF8 (← viaArg digestOf .write via))
  | .law, o :: rest =>
      let (ls, via) := splitVia rest
      do pure (.law (← digestArg digestOf o) (← mapEx (digestArg digestOf) ls) (← viaArg digestOf .law via))
  | .invoke, o :: m :: a :: rest =>
      let (rg, via) := splitVia rest
      do pure (.invoke (← digestArg digestOf o) (← numArg m) a (← readsArg .invoke rg)
        (← viaArg digestOf .invoke via))
  | .grant, t :: m :: rs :: via => do
      let target ← if t = "world" then pure GrantTarget.world
        else if t.startsWith "well:" || t.startsWith "of:" then GrantTarget.account <$> acctArg digestOf t
        else GrantTarget.cell <$> digestArg digestOf t
      let grantee ← if m = "anyone" then pure none else some <$> digestArg digestOf m
      pure (.grant target grantee (← mapEx rightArg (rs.splitOn ","))
        (← viaArg digestOf .grant via))
  | .revoke, [g] => .revoke <$> digestArg digestOf g
  | .read, [o, "?"] => .describe <$> digestArg digestOf o
  -- a first argument with `=` is an expectation; one without is a relation's label
  | .read, o :: r :: k :: p =>
      if r.contains '=' then do pure (.read (← digestArg digestOf o) (← mapEx expectArg (r :: k :: p)))
      else match p with
        | [] => do pure (.rangeRead (← digestArg digestOf o) r (← numArg k) none)
        | [t] => do pure (.rangeRead (← digestArg digestOf o) r (← numArg k) (some t))
        | _ => bad
  | .read, o :: es => do pure (.read (← digestArg digestOf o) (← mapEx expectArg es))
  | .rotate, [] => .ok .rotate
  | .sync, [] => .ok (.sync .tip)
  | .sync, ["--attest"] => .ok (.sync .attest)
  | .sync, ["--post", o] => do pure (.post (← digestArg digestOf o))
  -- `STEPS:CHAIN`: a step count and the chain's hex, as `sync` prints them
  | .sync, ["--to", a] =>
      match a.splitOn ":" with
      | [s, c] =>
          match s.toNat?, Text.ofHex c with
          | some n, some cb => .ok (.sync (.to n cb))
          | none, _ => .error (.badNumber s)
          | _, none => .error (.badToken c)
      | _ => .error (.badToken a)
  | .sync, ["--check", h] =>
      match Text.ofHex h with
      | some b => .ok (.sync (.check b))
      | none => .error (.badToken h)
  | .adopt, [i, k] =>
      match Text.ofHex i, Text.ofHex k with
      | some inv, some key => .ok (.adopt inv key)
      | none, _ => .error (.badToken i)
      | _, none => .error (.badToken k)
  | .receipt, [c] =>
      match Text.ofHex c with
      | some call => .ok (.receipt call)
      | none => .error (.badToken c)
  | .cancel, [c] =>
      match Text.ofHex c with
      | some call => .ok (.cancel call)
      | none => .error (.badToken c)
  | .invite, [i] =>
      match Text.ofHex i with
      | some input => .ok (.invite input)
      | none => .error (.badToken i)
  | .issue, [c] => .issue <$> numArg c
  | .account, a :: fl => do
      let fs ← flagsArg .account ["--owner"] fl
      let owner ← match fs.lookup "--owner" with
        | some o => some <$> digestArg digestOf o
        | none => pure none
      pure (.account (← digestArg digestOf a) owner)
  | .transfer, s :: d :: n :: fl => do
      let fs ← flagsArg .transfer ["--via", "--once"] fl
      let once ← match fs.lookup "--once" with
        | some k => match Text.ofHex k with
          | some b => if b.isEmpty then throw (.badOnce k) else pure (some b)
          | none => throw (.badOnce k)
        | none => pure none
      pure (.transfer (← acctArg digestOf s) (← acctArg digestOf d) (← numArg n) (← viaFlag digestOf fs) once)
  | .credit, w :: a :: n :: fl => do
      let fs ← flagsArg .credit ["--via"] fl
      pure (.credit (← digestArg digestOf w) (← acctArg digestOf a) (← numArg n) (← viaFlag digestOf fs))
  | _, _ => bad

/-- THE parser: argv bytes to a command, or a refusal by name. -/
def parse (argv : List ByteArray) : Except ParseRefusal (Cmd D) := do
  if argvSize argv > Bounds.argvBytesMax then throw (.argvPastBound (argvSize argv))
  let words ← mapEx (fun (b, i) => match String.fromUTF8? b with
    | some s => .ok s
    | none => .error (.notUtf8 i)) argv.zipIdx
  match words with
  | [] => throw .noVerb
  | w :: args => match Verb.ofWord? w with
    | none => throw (.unknownVerb w)
    | some v => parseVerb digestOf v args

end
end Nanodregg.Protocol.Command
/-
# Protocol.Command.Machine — what `host follow` does with a member's argv

`start` parses the argv and answers the first step; `step` takes the continuation `k` and the
result of the step it asked for and answers the next one, with follow's World after absorbing
any log the result carried. A write runs

  sign(envelope) → host(log, envelope) → [absorb: every logged step re-derived, PROTOCOL-4]
  → (page again, from the page's step count, until it is the writer's tip)
  → plan on the caught-up World → sign(callMessage u) → submit(call) → done

and a `read` stops after the catch-up; `init` needs no World (sign twice, print a join token).
The first envelope is valid for follow's own height + window, every later one for the tip the last
page stated + window. A member more than a window behind is refused `expired` with the writer's
height and re-signs ONCE a command for that height + window (`reSign`, PROTOCOL-19); a thin member's
`adopt` envelopes do the same, first for one window from height 0 (a pin names a step count).

`rotate` asks custody to `precommit` after the catch-up, plans the rotation to custody's NEXT key
(precommitting the digest of its PENDING key) and has it signed by `signNext`; custody advances on
its own settlement of the submit (CLIENT-13).

`invite INPUT` (PROTOCOL-42) catches up, checks that INPUT makes the member's own World, pins the
writer's newest base as follow verified it itself (PROTOCOL-47, `pinOf`: follow's own World at the
base's step count, the digest of its bytes; `noBase` / `baseMismatch` otherwise) and has custody
sign the invitation; it prints `invite HEX`.

`adopt INVITE KEY` (thin consent, PROTOCOL-14 A) takes its pin from an invitation `Invite.accept`
accepts under the sponsor key KEY, and pages the writer's newest base with `read`, one
signed envelope per page, `Protocol.Thin.accept` deciding each page; the download so far lives in
follow's state (`Follow.download`, memory only), and an adopted base replaces follow's World and is
handed back as `Persist.adopt` for follow to seal as its own newest base. From there every command
catches up from the base's step count like any other.

The log is a list of STEPS (KERNEL-47): admitted Turns and recorded refusals. Log positions count
steps, and follow binds on (steps, chain), as the kernel's `fold_reaches_host` does. Envelope windows
stay in heights: the page states the writer's tip in both units (PROTOCOL-41).

`sync --to STEPS:CHAIN` / `--attest` / `--check ATTEST` (PROTOCOL-74, `Protocol.Head`): the catch-up
compares follow's own fold with another member's stated position as it passes it (`Head.anchorCheck`
on each page's steps, re-derived from the argv at every step like every plan: `syncAnchor`), and
refuses `forkedWorld STEPS` by name when the chain there is another; `--attest` has custody sign
follow's own head at the tip (`attestTip`: the message is `Head.message` of follow's OWN World,
never a result's) and prints `attest HEX`; `--check` verifies the attestation's signature and World
before anything is sent and its signer's current key at the tip (`Head.signer`).

`cancel CALL` (PROTOCOL-63) catches up, checks on follow's World that the cancel records there, has
custody sign it and relays it; the writer's final answer (`refused cancelled`, or `ok`: the call was
admitted) is its word, so follow catches up AGAIN and ends by its own settlement of the call
(`cancelSettled`, PROTOCOL-71): `cancelled TXID` only when its own World records the cancel.

`k` holds only the argv, the phase and the results so far; every plan is RE-DERIVED from follow's
World at each step and the bytes it signs are compared with the ones custody signed (`planMoved`),
so `k` cannot steer what is signed (HOST-9's `resume_rederives`). A plan is offered for signature
only if follow's own World admits the Turn it derives (consent: `Protocol.Proofs.Command`).
-/
import Kernel.Receive.Rederive
import Protocol.Command.Verb
import Protocol.Envelope
import Protocol.Invite
import Protocol.Head
import Protocol.Command.Describe
import Protocol.Receipt
import Protocol.Settle
import Protocol.Attempt
import Protocol.Command.Exit
import Lang.Program

namespace Nanodregg.Protocol.Command

open Nanodregg.Codec Nanodregg.Kernel Nanodregg.Kernel.Receive

/-- What one `host follow` knows about the member it serves. -/
structure Self (D : Type) where
  /-- the World this member was invited to (its genesis origin, KERNEL-24), from the member's own
  side — its own Store's genesis, built from the invitation — never from the Host it talks to.
  Every call and envelope it signs names it. -/
  world : D
  member : D
  /-- the member's World grant (observe; enroll, if it carries it), which its envelopes name and
  its enrolments spend (KERNEL-16) -/
  grant : D
  /-- how many heights a signed call or envelope stays valid -/
  window : Nat

/-- Why a command ends without its effect. -/
inductive Refusal (D : Type)
  | parse (r : ParseRefusal)
  /-- follow's own derivation refused the call -/
  | receive (r : Receive.Refusal D)
  /-- follow's own World rejects the derived Turn -/
  | reject (r : Reject D)
  /-- the Host refused, by its name -/
  | host (name : String)
  /-- the logged step at this log position does not re-derive on follow's World: an event that does
  not read (`eventOf`), or one `Receive.rederive` refuses `eventMismatch` (a call that does not
  decode, a Turn event that does not derive, a refusal event that `Receive.judge` does not record)
  (a kernel rejection of a derived Turn is refused by the kernel's own name, `reject`; a well-formed
  cancel event that `Receive.cancel` does not record by `receive cancelForged`: `ofVerdict`) -/
  | eventMismatch (step : Nat)
  | badState
  | badResult
  /-- follow's World moved between the plan custody signed and the submit -/
  | planMoved
  /-- read: a `KEY=VALUE` that does not hold -/
  | expect (key : Nat)
  | missingCell (cell : D)
  /-- the writer's stated step count and chain after a log page are not what follow's fold reached -/
  | chainMismatch (steps : Nat)
  /-- a log page carried more steps than `Bounds.pageStepsMax`, more than one follower record holds
  (HOST-41): refused before any of it is absorbed -/
  | pageTooLong (count : Nat)
  /-- the derivation asked for more cells than `Kernel.Bounds.touchesMax` -/
  | tooManyPins
  /-- a base download refused, by `Protocol.Thin`'s name -/
  | thin (r : Thin.Refusal)
  /-- custody refused the step, by its name (`CustodyRefusal`) -/
  | custody (r : CustodyRefusal)
  /-- `read OBJ ?`: some part of the object's description has no text (`Lang.Print`) -/
  | noText
  /-- an invitation refused by `Invite.accept`'s name, or one for another World (PROTOCOL-42) -/
  | invite (r : Invite.Refusal)
  /-- the writer refused the envelope `expired` at `height` after follow's one re-signed envelope
  of this command, or stated a height that a re-signed envelope would not cover (PROTOCOL-19) -/
  | expired (height : Nat)
  /-- a call's bytes that do not read back as the call: `receipt CALL` whose bytes do not decode as
  a signed call, or step 3's signed call whose canonical bytes do not decode to itself (the World
  would judge the call they decode to, not the one follow derived) -/
  | badCall
  /-- `read OBJ REL K`: the object's package declares no relation REL (a data object declares none) -/
  | unknownRelation (rel : String)
  /-- `read OBJ REL K`: K is past the relation's declared `limit` (PROTOCOL-51) -/
  | rangePastLimit (most : Nat)
  /-- `read OBJ REL K PREFIX`: PREFIX reads at none of the relation's prefix types -/
  | badPrefix
  /-- `invoke … --pin REL KEY`: KEY does not read at the relation's key type (CLIENT-35) -/
  | badKey
  /-- `sync --to` / `--check`: follow's own fold reached the anchor's step count with ANOTHER chain
  (`Head.anchorCheck`): this member and the member whose head that is hold two histories of one
  World (PROTOCOL-74; `Proofs.Head.forked_not_on_fold`). The page is refused and nothing of it
  kept, as a `chainMismatch` page is -/
  | forkedWorld (steps : Nat)
  /-- `sync --to` / `--check`: follow was already past the position when the command began. Its
  World holds only the current chain, so the comparison on the way cannot be made: the member
  BEHIND does it, or `host check STORE --anchor` on this member's own Store (PROTOCOL-74) -/
  | ahead (steps : Nat)
  /-- a page carried a member's post of ITS head of this World (`Std.head` with `world` this World's
  origin, `Head.selfPosts`) at a position follow's own fold reached on this page with ANOTHER chain
  (PROTOCOL-75; `Proofs.Head.forkedSelfPost_forked`). The page is KEPT (it is lawful on follow's
  fold), the command ends by name, and the post is the witness: its poster signed that head
  (`Proofs.Head.body_post`) -/
  | forkedPost (steps : Nat) (member : Option D)
  /-- `sync --check`: the attestation refused by `Protocol.Head`'s name -/
  | attest (r : Head.Refusal)

/-- A step answered to the client. -/
inductive Reply
  | done (exit : UInt8) (out err : ByteArray)
  | sign (message k : ByteArray)
  | host (op : Op) (payload k : ByteArray)
  | submit (call k : ByteArray)
  | precommit (k : ByteArray)
  | signNext (message k : ByteArray)
  | signIncoming (message k : ByteArray)
  deriving Inhabited

/-- The continuation a reply carries, if any. -/
def Reply.cont : Reply → Option ByteArray
  | .done .. => none
  | .sign _ k | .host _ _ k | .submit _ k | .precommit k | .signNext _ k | .signIncoming _ k => some k

variable {D : Type} [Ord D] [DecidableEq D]

/-- The refusal's name on the wire (law 7): the constructor, arguments dropped. PROTOCOL's own
constructors are named here; a receive refusal or a kernel rejection carries its owner's name
(`Receive.Refusal.name`, `Reject.name`, each with `name_injective`), and a parse refusal the
parser's. -/
def Refusal.name : Refusal D → String
  | .parse r => r.name
  | .receive r => r.name
  | .reject r => r.name
  | .host n => n
  | .eventMismatch _ => "eventMismatch"
  | .badState => "badState"
  | .badResult => "badResult"
  | .planMoved => "planMoved"
  | .expect _ => "expect"
  | .missingCell _ => "missingCell"
  | .tooManyPins => "tooManyPins"
  | .chainMismatch _ => "chainMismatch"
  | .pageTooLong _ => "pageTooLong"
  | .thin r => r.name
  | .custody r => r.name
  | .invite r => r.name
  | .noText => "noText"
  | .expired _ => "expired"
  | .badCall => "badCall"
  | .unknownRelation _ => "unknownRelation"
  | .rangePastLimit _ => "rangePastLimit"
  | .badPrefix => "badPrefix"
  | .badKey => "badKey"
  | .forkedWorld _ => "forkedWorld"
  | .ahead _ => "ahead"
  | .forkedPost .. => "forkedPost"
  | .attest r => r.name

def refused (r : Refusal D) : Reply :=
  .done CommandExit.refused.code.toUInt8 ByteArray.empty s!"refused {r.name}\n".toUTF8
def printed (out : String) : Reply := .done CommandExit.ok.code.toUInt8 out.toUTF8 ByteArray.empty
/-- The catch-up read `Bounds.pagesPerCommandMax` pages and follow is still at log position
`steps`, the writer at `tip` steps: what was absorbed is kept, and the member runs the command
again. Not a refusal: its own end (`CommandExit.behind`, restart class), so a timer runs it again
(PROTOCOL-38). -/
def behind (steps tip : Nat) : Reply :=
  .done CommandExit.behind.code.toUInt8 ByteArray.empty s!"behind {steps} {tip}\n".toUTF8
/-- The writer refused the call by name and did NOT record it, so it is not final (PROTOCOL-41,
`Status.declined`): the attempt stays open. Printed `declined NAME`. -/
def declined (name : String) : Reply :=
  .done CommandExit.declined.code.toUInt8 ByteArray.empty s!"declined {name}\n".toUTF8
/-- The writer refused a page `pageIo` (HOST-48): its IO failed on that page and it serves on. "Not
now", never "never": the command ends restart-class (`CommandExit.pageIo`), what was absorbed is
kept, and the member runs it again. Printed `pageIo`. -/
def notServed : Reply :=
  .done CommandExit.pageIo.code.toUInt8 ByteArray.empty s!"{pageIoName}\n".toUTF8

/-! ## Follow's state -/
# Spec — what nanodregg is for, as theorems

The top of the tree. Each claim below is a `Prop` over `System` (`Spec/System.lean`: the deployed
entry points as fields). It has a top theorem `top_* : Claim (deployed d)`, a floor
`floor_* : ¬ Claim trivial` refuting a lazy system, and a deployed run that reaches it. Only these
claims owe a vacuity argument; a lemma under a top theorem means what the claim says it means
(TOPLEVEL-SPEC §1.3). Design: `redregg/work/promo/TOPLEVEL-SPEC.md`; decisions: `decisions/spec.md`.

**The trusted reading** is `System.lean` + `Trivial.lean` + `Proofs/Claims.lean` + `Proofs/Floor.lean` (≈ 620 lines).
`Proofs/Bridge.lean`, `Proofs/Member.lean` and `Proofs/Deployed.lean` are proofs: checked, not read.

## The claims

| § | claim | one sentence | top theorem | floor (the system it refuses) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2 A1 | `OneHistory.member` | whatever frames follow is fed, the member's World is an audited fold from a start it consented to | `top_oneHistory_member` (under `keyFaithful`; `Proofs/Member.lean`) | `floor_oneHistory_member`: the follower that takes the World it is told |
| 2 A2 | `OneHistory.pair` | two folds from one World that agree on step count and chain are one World, up to a collision among their link inputs | `top_oneHistory_pair` | `floor_oneHistory_pair`: the kernel whose chain does not move |
| 3 B2 | `Integrity.value` | along any fold, every denomination's balance sum is unchanged, and its floor sum unless the log created its well | `top_integrity_value` | `floor_integrity_value`: the kernel that admits without `checkValue` |
| 3 B3 | `Integrity.law` | along an audited log an object's package never changes and its states are a chain its `NoRef` laws' reference meaning admits | `top_integrity_law` | `floor_integrity_law`: the receiver whose `write` reaches the package slot |
| 4 C(ii) | `Consent.okCommitted` | a command that ends `done ok` at the settle phase means follow's own World journals the call | `top_consent_okCommitted` | `floor_consent_okCommitted`: the follower that says ok on the Host's word |
| 4 C(iii) | `Consent.final` | a settlement `expired`, or `refused` other than `stale`, is never contradicted on any extension | `top_consent_final` | `floor_consent_final`: the settlement that is always final |
| 4 C(ii) | `Consent.cancelOk` | a `cancel CALL` step that ends `done ok` leaves follow at a World that settles the call `refused cancelled` | `top_consent_cancelOk` | `floor_consent_cancelOk`: the follower that says ok on the writer's word |
| 4 C(iii) | `Consent.staleFinal` | a planned call follow settles `refused stale` is admitted on no extension, up to a collision among its pin inputs | `top_consent_staleFinal` | `floor_consent_staleFinal`: the settlement that says stale on its own word |
| 8 G2 | `Shown.onlyFollowers` | the writer defers a frame (answers with a page) only past an `Envelope.check` on its World | `top_shown` | `floor_shown`: the host that serves every frame |

**The apex** is `OneHistory.member ∧ Integrity.*`: the member's World is audited (A1) and audited
means lawful (B2, B3). Neither half excludes the other's trivial system: a kernel that admits
everything makes every log `Decided`, which A1 cannot see and B2/B3 refuse.

## Premises (each a parameter of the claim, named; deleting one is a strengthening)

* **`keyFaithful`** (A1): every continuation that parses as `adopt INVITE KEY` names the sponsor key
  the member trusts. Under an arbitrary relay A1 is FALSE today: a forged continuation makes a fresh
  member adopt any base the forger pinned (SPEC-4). Deleted by keeping the command's state inside
  follow (TOPLEVEL-SPEC §11.2).
* **`NoRef`** (B3): the chain lift exists for laws without holes only (TOPLEVEL-SPEC §11.6).
* **Canonical tries** (B2: `w.Canon`; B3: `CellsCanon`; C(iii): `RefusalsCanon`, `JournalCanon`): true of
  every World a Store opens to (`Kept`), carried as premises because the claims are generic in `D`.

B2's floor starts at the genesis World and ends at a World no deployed fold reaches, reached by the
minting kernel's fold (SPEC-7). B3's floor refutes the package clause; the law-chain clause has no
floor of its own yet (SPEC-7).

## Inhabited

At `deployed storeDigest` (the digest every deployed Store pins by; it does not depend on the Store's
key): `inhabited_okCommitted` (follow, holding the World THE re-deriver reaches on the run's log,
ends the rotate's settle phase `done ok`: C(ii)'s premise) and `inhabited_final` (on that World
follow settles `expired` a call the writer would have admitted at genesis: C(iii)'s premise, by a
call that was not final before), `inhabited_shown` (the writer defers the founder's `log` frame under
its root grant's envelope at the genesis World: G2's premise) and `inhabited_value` (the founder
issues a well, opens an account and transfers 10 into it; THE re-deriver accepts the three calls and
the account holds 10: B2's non-trivial case, value moved).

`deployed_run` / `inhabited_audited` (`Floor.lean`): at the deployed `Ctx`, a genesis World with one
founder and the founder's signed `rotate`, re-derived by THE re-deriver, reaches height 1 — a
`native_decide` fact about a computed run (SPEC-5). It inhabits A1/A2/B2/B3's premise (an audited log
with a Turn); it does not move value or run a law, so the non-trivial cases of B2 and B3 are not yet
reached by a deployed run (B2's is, by `inhabited_value`; B3's — a law judging a step — is not);
nor is a cancel that ends `ok`.

## Not yet claims (TOPLEVEL-SPEC sections, and why)

* B1 authority (§3.1): the `Authorized` inductive over every cell kind; the statement is the work.
* B2's grant cap and escrow (§3.2): exist as theorems (`grant_moves_le_cap`, `escrow_invokes_one_payout`).
* A2′ fork consistency (§2.3): landed as an act (PROTOCOL-74/75); the claim over `Command.step` is new.
* C(i) signing, C(v) at most once (§4).
* DURABILITY (§5), THE BELL (§6), RECEIPTS BETWEEN WORLDS (§9): their models (`Crash`, `RingLife`)
  or the writer's signed heads are §11's items.

