/-
# Lang.Package — a package: a checked core term with its interface, state type, methods and laws

A package is what `install` puts in a ROM cell (DECISIONS LANG-5, LANG-9): its imports (the ids of
installed packages; `ref i` in the body is the `i`-th), its object STATE type, its INTERFACE type
(the type of `body`), and which interface fields are METHODS (`State → Args → Req → State`) and
which are LAWS (`State → State → Req → Verdict`). `checkWith ifaces p` is the whole install check,
given the imports' interfaces; the package's id is KERNEL's `romIdOf` of its cell, so it commits to
the import ids and through them to the whole closure.
-/
import Lang.Wire
import Lang.Check
import Lang.HoleWire

namespace Nanodregg.Lang

/-- A relation an object of the package holds (LANG-49, LANG-44 as ruled): keyed, bounded rows
under the object, in the World's rows trie. A relation is a PLACE, never a value: no term has a
relation type, so no method holds a whole relation (law 5). Its key is fixed-width data, its
fields in DECLARED order (the key's bytes are the fields' data encodings in that order, so a
range's prefix is the first fields; the method sees the key as a record, labels ascending). -/
structure RelDecl where
  key : List (Label × Ty)
  val : Ty
  /-- at most this many rows (`relationFull`, kernel-checked on the current head) -/
  limit : Nat
  /-- the nat field of `val` the head sums (`relationCapped` past `cap`); none: no sum column -/
  sum : Option Label
  cap : Nat
  /-- rows may be retracted; `false`: insert-only by declaration (`retractForbidden`) -/
  retract : Bool
  /-- an interface field judging each edit, `fn S (fn reqTy (fn editTy verdictTy))` -/
  guard : Option Label
  deriving Inhabited, DecidableEq

structure Package where
  /-- ids of installed packages, as the digest bytes the kernel uses -/
  imports : List ByteArray
  state : Ty
  iface : Ty
  body : Term
  methods : List Label
  laws : List Label
  /-- its relations, labels ascending; relation `i` is the `i`-th (LANG-49) -/
  rels : List (Label × RelDecl) := []
  /-- what the package says of itself, one line (LANG-60): `Bounds.docOk` bytes, printed as `(doc "…")`
  and on `read OBJ ?`'s `doc` line; never run, never judged -/
  doc : ByteArray := .empty
  /-- Live contextual declarations and candidate syntax committed by the ROM id. -/
  holes : List HoleROM := []
  deriving Inhabited, DecidableEq

/-- Which act is judged (KERNEL's `Occasion`): a birth (the seed is `old = new`), a write, a law
replacement by the listed law ids (their digest bytes concatenated in order; the state is unchanged),
or an invoke of method `i`. A law cases on it; the checker requires one arm per label, so a law
written with `case` has no default arm. -/
def occasionTy : Ty :=
  .sum [("adopt", .bytes), ("birth", .record []), ("invoke", .nat), ("relaw", .bytes), ("write", .record [])]

/-- What a law and a method see of the call: the signing member, whose authority it uses, the
object judged or run on (`self`: a method names its own accounts by it, LANG-25), the call's
signed window (the Turn lands at a height in `[notBefore, validUntil]`, KERNEL-10), and the act
being judged. -/
def reqTy : Ty :=
  .record [("agent", .digest), ("notBefore", .nat), ("occasion", occasionTy), ("principal", .digest),
    ("self", .digest), ("validUntil", .nat)]

/-- A law's verdict: `ok`, or `refused` with the author's reason. -/
def verdictTy : Ty := .sum [("ok", .record []), ("refused", .bytes)]

def lawTy (S : Ty) : Ty := .fn S (.fn S (.fn reqTy verdictTy))

/-! ## Calls: what a method asks the kernel to do as the object (LANG-23, LANG-33)

The core has no lists, so a method returns its calls in a row of option SLOTS `c0 … c(k-1)`,
`k ≤ Kernel.Bounds.callsMax`. Each slot's type names the kinds of call it may carry: options over
a sub-sum of `callTy`, every arm at exactly its `callTy` type. `callTy` has one arm per kind an
object may exercise (KERNEL's `Body.callable`: account, credit, grant, install, invoke, transfer), its fields
the `Body` constructor's: a digest is `digest`, an `Option` is `optTy`, an `AcctRef` is
`acctRefTy`, a grant's rights the fixed-arity `rightsTy`. So a method's type says how many calls
it may make and of which kinds, and costs bytes only for those: a method that makes none has
`calls : rec {}`. `Lang.Call` reads the kernel's `Body` back out of such data. -/

/-- An optional `T`. -/
def optTy (T : Ty) : Ty := .sum [("none", .record []), ("some", T)]

/-- How a call names a value account (KERNEL's `AcctRef`): a well by its denomination, any other
account by its owner and denomination. -/
def acctRefTy : Ty := .sum [("of", .record [("asset", .digest), ("owner", .digest)]), ("well", .digest)]

/-- A right a granted call carries (KERNEL's `Right`). -/
def rightTy : Ty := .sum [("credit", .nat), ("enroll", .nat), ("invoke", .nat), ("mutate", .record []),
  ("observe", .record []), ("transfer", .nat)]

/-- The rights one granted call may carry. KERNEL's `Right` has six constructors, but a grant carries
at most one COUNTED kind (`enroll`, `transfer`, `credit`: KERNEL-60's `rightTarget` refuses two), so
four slots hold `observe`, `mutate`, an `invoke` and the one counted right; a grant naming one twice
says nothing more. -/
def rightsMax : Nat := 4

/-- The slot labels `r0 …` of `rightsTy` and `c0 …` of a calls row, in slot order. -/
def slotLabels (c : String) (n : Nat) : List Label := (List.range n).map fun i => c ++ toString i

def rightLabels : List Label := slotLabels "r" rightsMax

def rightsTy : Ty := .record (rightLabels.map fun l => (l, optTy rightTy))

/-! ### The call arms (LANG-23, LANG-77)

One arm per callable kind. The `invoke` arm is TYPED: its `args` is a VALUE of the slot's declared
argument type `A`, which the runtime encodes with the one data codec (`Wire.encodeData A`, the codec
the callee's `runSetupWith` inverts at its own argument type) when it reads the method's calls
(`Lang.callsOf`). So a method composes a nested call from values it computed, and its type says
what it sends. Before LANG-77 `args` was `bytes` and a method could only forward bytes it held
(LANG-23 (iii)). -/

def accountArm : Label × Ty := ("account", .record [("asset", .digest), ("owner", .digest)])
def creditArm : Label × Ty :=
  ("credit", .record [("acct", acctRefTy), ("amount", .nat), ("via", optTy .digest), ("well", .digest)])
def grantArm : Label × Ty :=
  ("grant", .record [("grantee", .digest), ("parent", optTy .digest), ("rights", rightsTy), ("target", .digest)])
def transferArm : Label × Ty :=
  ("transfer", .record [("amount", .nat), ("dst", acctRefTy), ("once", optTy .bytes), ("src", acctRefTy),
    ("via", optTy .digest)])
/-- `install`: the object installs the package these bytes encode (LANG-86, KERNEL-75: a generator).
The kernel checks the bytes exactly as a member's `install` (`checkInstall` at the signer's pins) and
bears the ROM cell `romIdOf`; a `bytes` value holds at most `Bounds.bytesMax`, so a package an object
installs is at most that long. -/
def installArm : Label × Ty := ("install", .record [("pkg", .bytes)])

/-- The `invoke` arm's record at argument type `A`. -/
def invokeArm (A : Ty) : Ty :=
  .record [("args", A), ("budget", .nat), ("method", .nat), ("obj", .digest), ("via", optTy .digest)]

/-- The arms a call slot may hold, in canonical order: with an `invoke` arm at argument type `A`
(`some A`), or without one (`none`). -/
def callArms : Option Ty → List (Label × Ty)
  | some A => [accountArm, creditArm, grantArm, installArm, ("invoke", invokeArm A), transferArm]
  | none => [accountArm, creditArm, grantArm, installArm, transferArm]

/-- Any call an object may make, its invokes (if `A` names an argument type) carrying a value of `A`. -/
def callTy (A : Option Ty) : Ty := .sum (callArms A)

/-- A slot of `kinds` other than `invoke`, in canonical order. `invoke` is not an arm here: a slot
that names only it is empty, and install refuses it `badMethod` (`callSlotOk`). -/
def callOf (kinds : List Label) : Ty := .sum ((callArms none).filter fun a => kinds.contains a.1)

/-- A slot that may invoke with an argument of type `A`, and may make the other `kinds`. -/
def invokeOf (A : Ty) (kinds : List Label := []) : Ty :=
  .sum ((callArms (some A)).filter fun a => a.1 == "invoke" || kinds.contains a.1)
/-
# Lang.Claim — a claim a package makes of itself, and the checker of its certifying shape (LANG-83)

A claim is a sentence in a small fixed language that a package carries in its identity and the
kernel refuses to install unless the package's own term bears it: proof-carrying code (Necula 1997)
in the one form nano's install check already has. The type check is such a checker (the claim is
the interface type, the proof is the term, `checkE` decides it in one pass, `Lang.Proofs.Check` is
its soundness); a claim extends the sentences that check the same way. The proof is the term's
SHAPE, the checker is a one-pass function beside `checkE` (unmetered, O(|term|), bounded by the
wire), and its soundness is one Lean theorem, `Lang.Proofs.Claim.guardedBy_sound`. No nano term
ever checks a proof; the kernel does, once, at install, as it types the term.

`keeps inv law`: every state an object of the package holds satisfies the interface field
`inv : fn S bool`, because the law `law` refuses every birth, adoption, write and invoke whose new
state fails it. Its proof is the law's control structure: every `ok` leaf of the law's body is
dominated by the true branch of `ite (app inv new) _ _` (`Term.guardedBy`), the `relaw` arm of a
`case` on `req.occasion` excepted, because the kernel judges a law change over the unchanged state
(`Kernel.Receive.law`; `Lang.Proofs.Chain.LawKeeps`). The `adopt` arm is NOT excepted: an adoption
is a base case like a birth (the incoming law judges the state it is adopted over, KERNEL-27), so a
law in shape refuses to be adopted over a state that fails its invariant.

The checker is sound by construction and incomplete by design: the only former it lets MEAN
`inj "ok" _` is a literal `inj` leaf, so a law that returns a verdict it read from its state, or
through a helper lambda, is not in certifying shape, and its claim is refused by name; the author
writes the law in shape (`Std.keeps` emits one), as a certifying compiler emits checkable code. The
compared `inv` is the closed literal term of the interface field, so no index shifting is needed:
`app inv (var k)` is syntactic equality with `k` the index of `new` at that depth.
Design: `redregg/work/promo/DESIGN-PCC.md`.
-/
import Lang.Subst

namespace Nanodregg.Lang

/-- A claim a package makes of itself, checked at install (LANG-83). -/
inductive Claim
  /-- every state an object of the package holds satisfies the interface field `inv : fn S bool`:
  the law `law` refuses every birth, adoption, write and invoke whose new state fails it -/
  | keeps (inv law : Label)
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr, Inhabited

mutual
/-- Every `ok` leaf of `t` is dominated by the true branch of `ite (app I (var k)) _ _`, or sits in
the `relaw` arm of a `case` on `get (var r) "occasion"`; `k` is the index of `new` and `r` of `req`
at the current depth (`1` and `0` under a law's three binders). Only a literal `inj` leaf may be an
`ok`; every other former is refused, so the walk is sound by construction. -/
def Term.guardedBy (I : Term) : (k r : Nat) → Term → Bool
  | k, r, .ite c a b => (c == .app I (.var k) || Term.guardedBy I k r a) && Term.guardedBy I k r b
  | k, r, .case s arms => Term.guardedArms I (k + 1) (r + 1) (s == .get (.var r) "occasion") arms
  | _, _, .inj _ ℓ _ => ℓ != "ok"
  | _, _, .refuse _ _ => true
  | _, _, _ => false

/-- Each arm's body is guarded, or (`occ`: the case is on the occasion) the arm is `relaw`. -/
def Term.guardedArms (I : Term) (k r : Nat) (occ : Bool) : List (Label × Term) → Bool
  | [] => true
  | (ℓ, body) :: arms =>
      ((occ && ℓ == "relaw") || Term.guardedBy I k r body) && Term.guardedArms I k r occ arms
end

end Nanodregg.Lang
/-
# Lang.Program — the language as KERNEL's `Receive.Program`: install check, laws, methods

`program bytesOf digestOf` instantiates `Kernel.Receive.Program D`. Every read goes through the
caller's `Pinned` view (so `receive_congr` covers LANG's reads): a package and every package in its
closure must be pinned, or the receiver refuses `unpinned`.

* `checkInstall` decodes the package (canonical bytes or a named decode refusal), loads its imports'
  closure exactly as a run does (each import pinned, decoded and RE-CHECKED by `admitContextual` at
  its own holes, depth one below the package's own load), and admits the package by that same
  admission over those interfaces (LANG-35, LANG-78).
* `run` loads the closure (each package once, depth ≤ `closureDepthMax`, bodies relocated to the
  closure's indices), decodes the state at the package's state type and the arguments at the
  method's argument type, runs `method state args req` under the call's tick budget and extracts
  its result (`resultTy` at the method's call slots: the calls, then the new state) under what is left of it; the calls are
  KERNEL `Body`s by `Lang.callsOf`.
* `judge` runs every law in force — the package's own, then each extra law package's — on
  `old → new`, each under `lawTicks`, each reading the slots at ITS state type (LANG-11). `refused r`
  is `lawDenied law (label ++ ": " ++ r)`; running out of ticks is `lawDenied law (label ++ ": exhausted")`.
-/
import Kernel.Receive.Call
import Lang.Law
import Lang.Wire.Data
import Lang.Read
import Lang.Call
import Lang.Rel
import Lang.LiveHoles

namespace Nanodregg.Lang

open Nanodregg.Kernel
open Nanodregg.Kernel.Receive

/-- How deep a closure's import graph may be. -/
def closureDepthMax : Nat := 8
/-- The longest reason a law may give, in bytes. -/
def reasonMax : Nat := Bounds.reasonMax

variable {D : Type} [DecidableEq D]

/-- A loaded closure: package ids in `lib` order, the relocated bodies, the packages. -/
structure Loaded where
  ids : List ByteArray
  lib : List Term
  pkgs : List Package

/-- The interfaces of a closure's packages, in `lib` order: the types `ref` gives them. -/
abbrev Loaded.ifaces (L : Loaded) : List Ty := L.pkgs.map (·.iface)

def reasonText (r : ByteArray) : String :=
  let r := r.extract 0 reasonMax
  (String.fromUTF8? r).getD s!"{r.size} bytes"

def faultText : Fault → String
  | .stuck => "stuck" | .overflow => "overflow" | .underflow => "underflow"
  | .divZero => "divZero" | .tooLarge => "tooLarge" | .ticks => "exhausted" | .stack => "stack"
  | .refused why => s!"refused {reasonText why}"

def decodeText (e : Codec.Refusal) : String := s!"decode {repr e}"

section
variable (bytesOf : D → ByteArray) (digestOf : ByteArray → Option D) (rc : ReceiptCheck)

/-- The package in a pinned package cell. The cell must be ROM (install's birth, `Receive.install`):
what a package id reads is then what it read at install, at every World a fold reaches
(`Kernel.Proofs.Frame.fold_rom`), so a description read at one World and an invoke planned at a
later one read the same package (HOSTING-43). A package cell of another discipline is
`malformedCell`. -/
def readPackage (look : Pinned D) (id : D) : Except (Refusal D) Package := do
  let cell ← readKind look id kindPackage
  if cell.discipline ≠ .rom then throw (.malformedCell id 0)
  match cell.slots.get 0 with
  | none => throw (.malformedCell id 0)
  | some b =>
    match Wire.decodePackage b with
    | .ok p => pure p
    | .error _ => throw (.malformedCell id 0)

def idOf (b : ByteArray) : Except (Refusal D) D :=
  match digestOf b with
  | some d => .ok d
  | none => .error (.program "import is not a digest")

/-- Every digest in `ds` is a digest of the World (LANG-38: an id enters a run only as one). -/
def digestsOk (ds : List ByteArray) : Bool := ds.all fun b => (digestOf b).isSome

/-- Install's and every load's id-literal check: the checked package, or `notDigest` when a
digest literal of its body is not a digest of the World (LANG-38). -/
def checkLits (p : Package) : Except (Refusal D) Unit :=
  if digestsOk digestOf p.body.digestLits then .ok () else .error (.program "notDigest")

def indexIn (b : ByteArray) : List ByteArray → Option Nat
  | [] => none
  | x :: xs => if x = b then some 0 else (indexIn b xs).map (· + 1)

/-- The interfaces of the closure's packages at positions `gs`. -/
def ifacesAt (pkgs : List Package) : List Nat → Option (List Ty)
  | [] => some []
  | g :: gs =>
    match pkgs[g]?, ifacesAt pkgs gs with
    | some P, some r => some (P.iface :: r)
    | _, _ => none

/-- Append package `p` (imports already loaded at `gis`) to the closure, RE-CHECKING it against its
imports' interfaces (decisions/lang.md LANG-16), its body relocated to the closure's indices. -/
def admitPackage (idb : ByteArray) (p : Package) (acc : Loaded) (gis : List Nat) :
    Except (Refusal D) (Loaded × Nat) :=
  match ifacesAt acc.pkgs gis with
  | none => .error (.program "closure")
  | some ifs =>
    match p.checkWith ifs with
    | .error e => .error (.program e.wire)
    | .ok () =>
      match checkLits digestOf p with
      | .error e => .error e
      | .ok () =>
      .ok ({ ids := acc.ids ++ [idb], lib := acc.lib ++ [p.body.relocate fun i => gis.getD i 0],
             pkgs := acc.pkgs ++ [p] }, acc.ids.length)

mutual
/-- Load package `idb` and its closure into `acc` (each package once); its index in the closure. -/
def loadUsing (admit : ByteArray → Package → Loaded → List Nat → Except (Refusal D) (Loaded × Nat)) (look : Pinned D) (fuel : Nat) (idb : ByteArray) (acc : Loaded) :
    Except (Refusal D) (Loaded × Nat) :=
  match indexIn idb acc.ids with
  | some i => .ok (acc, i)
  | none =>
    match fuel with
    | 0 => .error (.program "imports too deep")
    | fuel + 1 =>
      match idOf digestOf idb with
      | .error e => .error e
      | .ok id =>
        match readPackage look id with
        | .error e => .error e
        | .ok p =>
          match loadAllUsing admit look fuel p.imports acc with
          | .error e => .error e
          | .ok (acc', gis) => admit idb p acc' gis
termination_by (fuel, 0)

def loadAllUsing (admit : ByteArray → Package → Loaded → List Nat → Except (Refusal D) (Loaded × Nat)) (look : Pinned D) (fuel : Nat) (ds : List ByteArray) (acc : Loaded) :
    Except (Refusal D) (Loaded × List Nat) :=
  match ds with
  | [] => .ok (acc, [])
  | d :: ds =>
    match loadUsing admit look fuel d acc with
    | .error e => .error e
    | .ok (acc', g) =>
      match loadAllUsing admit look fuel ds acc' with
      | .error e => .error e
      | .ok (acc'', gs) => .ok (acc'', g :: gs)
termination_by (fuel, ds.length + 1)
end

/-- The closed specialization of the shared closure traversal. -/
`rowsRun` reached with what they give back (so `admitImpl` commits them without running the rows
twice). The one source of the check sequence: `check` is its first component. -/
def checkWith (E : Env D) (w : World D) (t : Turn D) : Except (Reject D) (List (Post D) × (Rows D × Nat)) := do
  checkBounds t
  checkWindow w t
  checkJournal E w t
  checkShape t
  let posts := (← mapEx (touchCheck E w) t.touches).reduceOption
  checkValue E t posts
  checkNullifiers E w t
  checkRowsRemain E w t
  checkRanges E w t
  let rr ← rowsRun E w t
  checkCharge w t (credit posts + rr.2)
  checkCreates E w t
  checkTicks E w t
  checkSteps w
  pure (posts, rr)

/-- Every check, against the pre-state only; on success, what the touches leave behind. -/
def check (E : Env D) (w : World D) (t : Turn D) : Except (Reject D) (List (Post D)) :=
  (checkWith E w t).map Prod.fst

def bornEntries (t : Turn D) : List (D × Entry) :=
  t.creates.map fun p => (p.1, .live p.2 0)

/-- Write cell entries into the map, each key in turn, one path each: the proofs' shape of
`commitTrie` (`Kernel.Proofs.Admit.commitTrie_eq`), and the genesis founders' write. -/
def insertAll (E : Env D) (m : Cells D) (ps : List (D × Entry)) : Cells D :=
  ps.foldl (fun m p => m.insert (trieSpec E) (.cell p.1) (.entry p.2)) m

/-- Mark nullifiers spent, each in turn, one path each: the proofs' shape of `commitTrie`. -/
def spendAll (E : Env D) (m : Cells D) (ns : List D) : Cells D :=
  ns.foldl (fun m n => m.insert (trieSpec E) (.nullifier n) .spent) m

/-- The map after a checked turn: its touches' entries, its born cells, its nullifiers, ONE batch on
/-
# Lang.Journey — the first law end to end: install → create → invoke through KERNEL's receivers

`lake exe lang-journey` drives the REAL receive path (`Receive.submit` = `receive` then
`World.admit`) with `Lang.program` as the kernel's `Program`, under the Sim's toy parameters
(UInt64 digest, FNV hash, toy signatures: memory "toy parameters in the real pipeline"). The
package `Owned` keeps `{owner : digest, total : nat}`; its method `set n` writes `total := n`; its
laws are `bounded` (`new.total ≤ 100`, else "over 100") and `ownerOnly` (the SIGNING member must be
the owner and the owner never changes). Rows:

* L0 the text form of `Owned` (`Lang.Read`, what a resident writes) reads to the same bytes.
* L0c `Lang.Print` gives `Owned` its text, which reads back to the same bytes; a non-atom label
  has no text; bytes that are not quote-free UTF-8 print as `#hex` and read back.
* L0d the reader's fuel counts nesting only: an allowlist at `Std.idsMax` ids prints and reads
  back; a list past `Read.listMax` is refused `tooLong`, a form past `Read.depthMax` `tooDeep`.
* L0e a binder named like a literal (`true`, `5`, `#ab`), a head word or a primitive is refused
  `reservedBinder`.
* L0f (LANG-31) one label rule, and the reader two levels past the wire: a package written as text
  with its body and interface nested to the wire's depth bound reads, checks, encodes, prints and
  reads back to the same bytes; one level deeper the reader refuses it `tooDeep`; a label with a
  space has no bytes.
* L1 founder A installs `Owned` (a ROM cell, id `romIdOf`) — admitted.
* LV (LANG-60) the package wire's version: `Owned`'s LANG-59 bytes (a fixture, no version byte) and
  its current bytes at the previous version are refused `lang/package-version/N` (N = `Bounds.packageWireVersion`) at install and at decode.
* L2 A creates an object owned by A, seed `{owner = A, total = 0}`, judged by both laws — admitted.
* L3 A invokes `set 50` — admitted; the object's `total` slot decodes to 50.
* L4 A invokes `set 101` — refused `lawDenied` by `bounded`, nothing written.
* L5 B invokes `set 7` through A's grant for method 0 (so the principal is A, the grant's root
  issuer) — refused `lawDenied` by `ownerOnly`, which checks the SIGNER: a law can be stricter than
  the principal.
* L6 a package whose body does not check at its interface — refused `program` at install.
* LI (LANG-35) install loads the import closure: `Mid` imports `Owned` and installs; `Top` imports
  `Mid`, and its install pinning only `Mid` is refused `unpinned` naming `Owned` (LI2: the check loads
  `Mid`'s own closure, so `Owned` must be pinned too); pinning both, it is admitted (LI3).
* L8 (ID-1) `Tally`'s law `fresh` cases on the occasion: a birth seeded with approvals is refused,
  a zero seed admitted.
* L8d/e the invoke's method index reaches the law: `fresh` closes method `b`, `a` passes.
* L9 (ID-1) `Frozen`, an extra law on a data object, refuses its own replacement; writes pass.
* L10 the relaw carries the incoming law ids: `OnlyTo [Frozen]` refuses a replacement by `[Tally]`
  and admits one by `[Frozen]`.
* L11 (KERNEL-27 `adopt`) `BirthOnly` admits a birth and refuses its adoption: an object may be
  born under it (L11b), and adding it to an existing object is refused (L11c) by the incoming law
  itself, which a birth-conflated occasion could not say. L11d: a relaw that KEEPS BirthOnly and adds
  Frozen is admitted, since only ADDED laws judge adopt (KERNEL-27).
* LD (LANG-38, a digest is a type) LD1: a Relay whose call names its callee by a bytes literal is
  refused `illTyped payload` at install; LD2: an allowlist naming a 3-byte id (not a Sim digest) is
  refused `notDigest` at install; LD3: a Relay seeded with `next = some #78797a` is refused at its
  birth, `state field next: not a digest`; LD4: an invoke whose argument `via` is not a digest is
  refused `arguments: not a digest`.
* LD5 (LANG-41, a run's ids came from where ids enter) `Caller`, holding and taking nothing, calls the
  signing member as an object: the kernel refuses it `wrongKind` (a member is not an object), so
  `callsOf` read the call.
* LT (LANG-45, NANO-VS-MINI §5) Mini's CappedTally ported by hand, as the text a resident writes:
  LT0 it reads (its size printed); LT1 install; LT2a a start at 1001 refused by `cap`; LT2 a start at 0
  with decider B; LT3 the grant; LT4 B replies 600 (required steps and ticks printed); LT5 B replies
  500, refused by `cap`; LT6 the owner replies, refused by `decider`; LT7 two answers signed over one
  view: the first admitted, the second `stale` (Mini's `add` deltas keep both); LT8 a tally that
  closes before the answer's window ends refuses it `closed` (Mini's `timedOut`, as a height).
* LS (LANG-47 → LANG-49) Mini's CoAuthor over relations (`proposals`, `authors`, `ballots`,
  `chapters`; a method that succeeds leaves the state unchanged, the law `react` refuses by `why`):
  LS0 it reads (sizes printed beside the list port's); LS1–LS3 install, start, grant; LS4/LS5 two
  proposals, LS5b a second by one author `keyTaken` (Mini's alreadyProposed); LS6 a vote (required
  steps, ticks and logged bytes printed), LS6b `keyTaken` (alreadyVoted), LS6c `unknown`; LS7
  `notAuthor`, LS7b the author's rewrite; LS8 a seal in the window `early`; LS9 the seal after it,
  over one signed range, makes the rewritten passage chapter 1; LS10 two proposals signed over one
  view both land, in both orders, to the same World; LS11 a vote and an edit of one proposal over one
  view: the second is `stale` (its pinned row moved). LS12 (LANG-59) a layer's `steal`, an upsert of a
  proposal giving it another author, is refused by the guard `authored` (it sees the old row); LS12i a
  layer's `forge`, an insert under another author's name, is refused by it too; LS12' both on a
  CoAuthor whose guard admits any author are admitted. Plant skip-judge: LS6c is
  then ADMITTED.
* L7 the fold (`World.fold` from genesis) and the follower's re-derivation (`Receive.rederiveLog`) reach the Host's World.
* PLANT skip-judge: the same rows against a program whose `judge` admits everything: L4 must be
  ADMITTED there (the row flips), proving the refusal in L4 came from the law, not from elsewhere. LT5 too: its
  refusal came from `cap`.
* PLANT erase-occasion: the same rows against a program whose judge is told every act is a write
  (the judge before ID-1): L8b, L8e, L9d, L10c and L11c must be ADMITTED there, so their refusals came
  from the occasion.
* PLANT raw-install: the install check before LANG-35, each import's interface read raw from its
  cell and its closure never loaded: LI2 must be ADMITTED there, so its refusal came from the loader.
  That mode stops after LI2: the plant runs the rows before LI and LI2, not the rows after.
* PLANT bytes-ids: the install check reads every bytes literal as an id (ids were bytes before
  LANG-38): LD1 must be ADMITTED there, so its refusal came from the type. PLANT skip-lits: the install
  check without its id-literal check: LD2 must be ADMITTED. PLANT total-digest: run and judge with a
  `digestOf` that accepts any bytes: LD3 and LD4b must be ADMITTED, so their refusals came from the
  boundary's digest check. Each of the three runs only the call rows up to its own.
* PLANT unlawful-codec: the method run by a program whose decoder refuses the founder's own id inside
  a lawful `Ctx` (`Lang.Proofs.Provenance.program_run_past_extraction`'s `hp` false there): LD5c must be refused by `callsOf`, by name,
  `calls: c0 obj: not a digest`. A request's ids are the one source no boundary check reads, so this
  is the only way to reach that refusal, and the second premise of
  `program_run_past_extraction` (the program decodes with the `Ctx`'s own codec) is not decoration.
* PLANT drop-range-row: the run is handed each signed range without its last row: LS9's seal is
  still ADMITTED (the kernel re-checks the signer's range pin, never what Lang was given) and chapter
  1 is the wrong passage (the dropped row was the winner), so the honest LS9 says the rows a method
  receives are exactly the keys under the pinned prefix. That mode runs only the LS rows.
* PLANT erase-payload: the occasion's payload is lost (a relaw names no laws, every invoke is
  method 0): L8e must be admitted and L10d refused, so the payload reaches the law.
-/
