import json
pages=json.loads(\"{\\\"OVERVIEW\\\":\\\"# The system\\\\n\\\\nNanodregg is a shared World with one writer and members who check its history themselves. A member signs a call planned against its own view. The writer derives and judges the call, records an admitted turn or a recordable refusal, and serves the history. Followers re-derive each event before accepting the resulting World.\\\\n\\\\n| Layer | Contract |\\\\n|---|---|\\\\n| [Kernel](../Kernel/World.lean) | Cells, accounts, grants, relations, authenticated roots, budgets and the transition for turns and recorded refusals. [Receive](../Kernel/Receive/Rederive.lean) connects signed calls to those transitions. |\\\\n| [Lang](../Lang/Program.lean) | Checked packages supply methods and laws. Imports and external reads use the caller'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s pinned view; evaluation spends a bounded tick allowance. |\\\\n| [Codec](../Codec/Stream.lean) | Canonical encodings, bounded decoders, digests and signatures give the other layers their bytes. |\\\\n| [Protocol](../Protocol/Command/Machine.lean) | Commands catch up, plan, request signatures and settle against the follower'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s own World. Invitations, receipts and heads describe what another party can check. |\\\\n| [Host](../Host/Store.lean) | One locked Store persists authenticated records and atomic bases. Bounded pages serve a base or log; a follower persists its own checked history. |\\\\n| [Client](../client/src/bin/nd.rs) | Rust holds member secrets, freezes signed attempts, transports frames and performs the steps Lean requests. |\\\\n| [Hosting](../Hosting/Resident/Run.lean) | World objects describe resident grains. Confined processes use member and broker sockets; the keybroker holds provider credentials. |\\\\n| [Ops and judge](JUDGE.md) | Rendered units operate the processes. Source gates, builds, simulations, journeys and convergence check changes. |\\\\n\\\\nObjects run installed packages. Laws judge changes; relations hold bounded keyed rows. Activities, offers, escrow and generators are package constructions over these mechanisms. [OBJECTS](OBJECTS.md) connects them to executable journeys.\\\\n\\\\nAn accepted command is more than a writer reply: settlement is checked against the member'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s own journal and refusal roots. A thin joiner begins with a sponsor-pinned base; [TRUST](TRUST.md) explains that boundary, certificates and foreign receipts.\\\\n\\\\n[Spec](../Spec/README.md) names the composed theorem claims, their premises and their floors. They do not imply secrecy from the operator, multiwriter consensus or an unconditional end-to-end durability or at-most-once guarantee.\\\\n\\\\nUse [MEMBER](MEMBER.md) for commands, [OPERATING](OPERATING.md) for processes and recovery, and [TESTGROUND](TESTGROUND.md) for the browser system.\\\\n\\\",\\\"MEMBER\\\":\\\"# Being a member\\\\n\\\\nnd uses ND_WORKSPACE for custody and attempts, and the follower and writer sockets. ND_FOLLOW_SOCK and ND_HOST_SOCK override socket paths. Personal custody uses a separate ND_HOLDING directory for the next secret. Local custody flags are parsed in [nd.rs](../client/src/bin/nd.rs); the shared language is parsed in [Verb](../Protocol/Command/Verb.lean).\\\\n\\\\nThese are command forms: uppercase words stand for the member'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s values. Digests and signed bytes use canonical hex; numbers are decimal. Lang reads package, state and argument text. Describe is spelled read OBJ ?.\\\\n\\\\n| Action and syntax | What is checked |\\\\n|---|---|\\\\n| Join: nd --join KEY_FILE INVITATION STORE /ABS/HOST | The invitation verifies under the sponsor key obtained through a trusted channel. The joined origin comes from its inputs. Join creates the member'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s Store and retains the invitation; a sponsor enrolls the join token and supplies a World observe grant. |\\\\n| Write: nd write OBJ KEY VALUE [--via GRANT] | After catch-up, follow derives the call against its own World, including pins, authority, laws, bounds and charge. Only an admissible plan is offered for signature. |\\\\n| Read: nd read OBJ [KEY=VALUE...] | Follow re-derives history before reading its own state; each supplied expectation must hold. |\\\\n| Describe: nd read OBJ ? | Follow loads the package and prints its description, state type, methods, laws and relations. Method indices come from this description. |\\\\n| Invoke: nd invoke OBJ METHOD ARGS [--range REL K [PREFIX]] [--pin REL KEY]... [--via GRANT] | Arguments are read at the method'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s type. Follow plans against authenticated reads and checks the derived turn before requesting its signature. |\\\\n| Sync: nd sync | Each event is re-derived; reached steps and chain must match the page. Only read envelopes are signed. |\\\\n| Receipt: nd receipt CALL | Follow catches up and builds trie evidence and settlement against its own head. Output includes receipt, head and settlement. |\\\\n| Cancel: nd cancel CALL | Follow checks the member'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s own signed call can be cancelled, requests a cancel signature and sends it. A second catch-up must show the recorded cancellation before success. |\\\\n\\\\nWrites and invokes first sign envelopes for bounded log pages. The eventual call signs its World, member, window, pins and body. A resumed plan is re-derived; changed signed bytes are planMoved. The sponsor key during adoption remains a trust premise described in [TRUST](TRUST.md).\\\\n\\\\nRead OBJ REL K [PREFIX] reads a bounded relation range. Sync --to STEPS:CHAIN compares a position while catching up; --attest signs follow'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s head, --check ATTEST checks another member'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s attestation, and --post OBJ posts follow'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s own head. A follower already beyond the requested comparison answers ahead.\\\\n\\\\nThe language also has init, enroll, install, create, law, grant, revoke, rotate, adopt, issue, account, transfer, credit and invite. [Verb.usage](../Protocol/Command/Verb.lean) gives their forms. The --init, --init-hosted, --init-observer, --seal-observer and --keys flags are local custody operations.\\\\n\\\\nA refusal names the failed condition. Stale means a signed pin no longer matches; lawDenied carries the law'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s reason; noGrant and grantRight concern authority. Parsing and pre-signing refusals create no signed call. An already-sent call needs settlement: uncertain, declined, open and hostContradicted do not mean absent forever. Behind preserves catch-up progress; pageIo identifies a writer page-read failure.\\\\n\\\\nAn unsettled attempt stores exact argv, continuation and signed call. Repeating its argv resumes it. The writer reply is a hint; only the follower'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s receipt settles it. Repeating an already-settled command can create a new call: argv is not a durable caller-intent identifier. Transfer'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s --once HEX is a separate once-key mechanism.\\\\n\\\\nThe [protocol rows](../Protocol/Rows.lean) exercise join, use, describe, receipt, settlement, cancellation and fork comparison through the command machine.\\\\n\\\",\\\"OBJECTS\\\":\\\"# Objects and programs\\\\n\\\\nA package is an immutable ROM cell identified by its canonical contents. Its [interface](../Lang/Package.lean) includes imports, state and interface types, a term, method and law labels, relations, documentation and contextual holes. Installation checks it and its pinned import closure. An object holds a package reference and state; a data object can instead have separate law packages.\\\\n\\\\nMethods produce state, bounded row edits and typed call slots. Calls can exercise the kernel'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s callable bodies, including nested invocation and installation. The kernel checks the resulting tree as one transition: a refused leg does not leave earlier legs committed.\\\\n\\\\nLaws see old state, proposed state and request. The occasion distinguishes birth, adoption, write, relaw and invocation with its payload. A law may refuse with its own bounded reason. Relations are bounded places under an object, not whole-table values; point and range reads are authenticated and pinned.\\\\n\\\\n| Construction | Meaning | Executable evidence |\\\\n|---|---|---|\\\\n| Laws by act | Package and extra laws judge the occasion, including birth and law replacement. | [Lang journey](../Lang/Journey.lean), L8–L11 and erase-occasion plant. |\\\\n| Holes and justifications | ROM declares typed candidates and contexts. Each invocation resolves selections against authenticated rows/cells, support versions, blockers and guards; preparation is charged. | [LiveHoles](../Lang/LiveHoles.lean), [contextual receive](../Lang/Journey/ContextualReceive.lean), [hole checks](../Lang/Journey/HoleChecks.lean). |\\\\n| Activities | Generated methods capture a plan, keep pending/log rows and resume with a typed answer. Patience is in World heights; retirement handles overdue work. | [StdActivity](../Lang/StdActivity.lean), [activity surface](../Lang/Journey/Activity.lean), Bell cases in [Lang journey](../Lang/Journey.lean). |\\\\n| Offers | Each party posts terms and role. Fusion judges a named deal, consumes selected offers and records matching captures through nested calls. | [Offer journey](../Lang/Journey/Offer.lean), LF cases. |\\\\n| Offer escrow | Posting funds the offering object'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s value leg. Fusion pays both legs and consumes the offers atomically. | [OfferEscrow journey](../Lang/Journey/OfferEscrow.lean), LE cases. |\\\\n| Generators | A method emits package bytes through install. The receiver applies the same package check as member installation. Templates can depend on arguments or the invoker. | [Gen journey](../Lang/Journey/Gen.lean), GN cases; [StdGen](../Lang/StdGen.lean). |\\\\n| Claims | Term.guardedBy checks a restricted certifying law shape, with a soundness theorem. This checker is separate from package wire/install admission. | [Claim](../Lang/Claim.lean), [claim journey](../Lang/Journey/Claims.lean), LK cases. |\\\\n| Effects | Call-slot types constrain kinds and slot count; nested arguments are typed and encoded for the callee. This concrete boundary is not a general package-level grading system. | [Package.callTy](../Lang/Package.lean), [Call](../Lang/Call.lean), offer/generator journeys. |\\\\n| Foreign receipts | A receipt justifies an action relative to a trusted head object. It establishes admission at a height, not arbitrary outside content. | [Head journey](../Lang/Journey/Head.lean), HD/RC/TL cases; [Foreign.check](../Protocol/Foreign.lean). |\\\\n\\\\nCases are nested under lang-journey or rows, rather than separate [runs.tsv](../gates/runs.tsv) entries. The table names executable checks and controls.\\\\n\\\\n[Contextual](../Lang/Contextual/Protocol.lean) retains a research model and instances. Deployed package preparation uses [Program](../Lang/Program.lean) and LiveHoles; a research-instance theorem is not automatically a runtime guarantee.\\\\n\\\",\\\"TRUST\\\":\\\"# What a member trusts\\\\n\\\\nOne writer orders a World'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s history. Members check its proposed history: [Receive.rederive](../Kernel/Receive/Rederive.lean) reconstructs each event against the member'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s World and checks the transition. Step count includes admitted turns and recorded refusals; height advances with admitted turns.\\\\n\\\\nTrusted computation includes the member'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s executable, codecs, digests/signatures and custody. The operator sees the World: there is no content secrecy from the writer. Filesystem and process guarantees depend on platform contracts named in [Spec](../Spec/README.md).\\\\n\\\\nA receipt proves a journal/refusal answer against particular roots; it does not authenticate the head itself. Local settlement uses follow'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s own World. A [foreign receipt](../Protocol/Foreign.lean) uses a head object the receiving World holds. Its soundness premise says the head belongs to a fold of the foreign World, not that it is the latest head.\\\\n\\\\nA head records origin, steps, height, roots and chain. Members compare positions, sign attestations and post heads into ordinary objects. Sync --to detects differing chains at a reached position; --check also checks the attestation and member key. A head object'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s fork state retains conflicting evidence. These mechanisms expose incompatible histories when evidence is compared; they do not make multiple writers agree.\\\\n\\\\nGenesis inputs can name certifiers and a threshold, committed in the World. [Head.certified](../Protocol/Head.lean) checks distinct listed signers, signatures, threshold and origin. A zero-threshold authority certifies nothing.\\\\n\\\\nA thin joiner accepts an invitation under a sponsor key it trusts and downloads the pinned base. When that World names certifiers, adoption also requires a certificate of exactly its head. Without certifying authority, adoption uses the sponsor pin alone. The consumer reads base.cert; the deployed Store has no certificate producer. A nonzero authority without a valid certificate cannot complete thin adoption.\\\\n\\\\nThe certificate is checked beside the sponsor pin. Founders must check the genesis inputs they intend to authorize. Browser visitors obtaining executable and sponsor key from the page server rely on that server for bootstrap.\\\\n\\\\nThe member-history theorem retains keyFaithful: an adopt continuation must name the trusted sponsor key. Re-deriving resumed plans does not remove this premise. Law-chain claims also have their stated reference restrictions and canonical-trie premises. Read [Spec'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s claims and floors](../Spec/README.md) before treating a component theorem as composed assurance.\\\\n\\\",\\\"OPERATING\\\":\\\"# Operating a World\\\\n\\\\nThe [RUNBOOK](../deploy/RUNBOOK.md) contains installation/recovery detail. [Host.Cli](../Host/Cli.lean) defines the grammar; [units](../deploy/units/linux) and [exit tables](../deploy/units/EXITS.md) are generated from [Ops.Units](../Ops/Units.lean). Uppercase arguments below stand for operator-supplied values.\\\\n\\\\n| Operation | Form and responsibility |\\\\n|---|---|\\\\n| Genesis | host genesis STORE NAME [--nonce HEX32] [--certifier HEX32]... [--threshold N] [KEY_HEX NEXT_HEX]... checks founders, authority and budget before creation. Founders independently check the inputs. |\\\\n| Writer | host serve STORE [--retain N] serves framed requests. The key-file handle holds the writer lock. Default retention keeps history; an explicit count must meet the floor. |\\\\n| Follower | host follow STORE MEMBER_HEX GRANT_HEX owns a separate writable Store and executes member commands. |\\\\n| Join | host join STORE INVITE_HEX KEY_HEX [IN] authenticates the invitation under the trusted sponsor key. IN is for a carried invitation. |\\\\n| Inspect | host check STORE [--anchor STEPS:CHAIN_HEX] checks history against a held position. The anchor counts steps, not heights. |\\\\n| Seal | host seal STORE [--retain N] seals a stopped Store. |\\\\n| Carry | host export STORE OUT exports a stopped Store. Host import takes STORE NAME IN with either --digest HEX and optional --nonce HEX32, or --origin HEX with --nonce HEX32. Preserve the checked export pin. |\\\\n\\\\nnd-supervise confines the child and multiplexes bounded socket connections onto framed stdio. Policy grants Store writes and executable access while hiding custody roots. Linux needs the fixed sandbox mechanism and its installation permissions; absence has no permissive fallback.\\\\n\\\\n| Unit | Role |\\\\n|---|---|\\\\n| nd-host-serve.service | Writer under nd. |\\\\n| nd-host-follow.service | Personal follower; its own Store and hidden workspace/holding directories. |\\\\n| nd-hosted-follow@.service | Hosted follower under nd-hosted; shared socket group for its driver. |\\\\n| nd-follow-drive@.service | nd sync under nd; Restart=always, forty-second interval, except its fatal refusal class. |\\\\n| nd-keybroker.service | Provider keys, anchors and pins under nd; confined Lean decider. |\\\\n| nd-members@.service | World resident decider and managed follower/member children. |\\\\n| nd-grain-launch@.service | Controller and confined grains under nd-hosted. |\\\\n\\\\nnd-members separates member sockets, custody, follower state and follower sockets. Launcher roots must also be disjoint. Ownership, modes and directory chains are checked; a matching path is insufficient. Rendered tmpfiles/sysusers definitions supply identities and modes.\\\\n\\\\nA resident sees its package, writable volume and own member/broker sockets. It reads the Bell, keeps intent before asking, persists the answer before submitting and settles delivery through its member. The broker checks provider policy and custody; the provider secret does not enter the resident volume.\\\\n\\\\nUncertain is an unknown outcome: use member settlement. Do not invent missing Store files or start a second writer. The [Store layout](../deploy/units/STORE.md) gives backup order: key, atomic base, index, segments. Preserve the backup together and check the restored Store against a held steps/chain anchor. Retention can make older anchors unavailable.\\\\n\\\\nThe optional base.cert sidecar is self-authenticating to its consumer, not a replacement for the base/log. [TRUST](TRUST.md) explains the producer boundary.\\\\n\\\",\\\"JUDGE\\\":\\\"# Changing the system\\\\n\\\\nscripts/commit snapshots named paths, merges against current main, builds a private candidate, regenerates SIZE, judges and lands by compare-and-swap. Conflict and stale-path refusals identify what must be resolved; moved main is merged and judged again.\\\\n\\\\nThe invocation accepts -m MESSAGE or -F FILE, optional --cross-layer REASON and --reverts COMMIT, then the exact paths. The subject layer is resolved by [OWNERS](../gates/OWNERS). CROSS-LAYER: at column zero must name foreign owners.\\\\n\\\\nThe message explains behavior and the layer decision with critique/meta-critique. Include SIZE: METRIC: reason for each required rise. Declaration changes need the stale-path guard'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s map: REDEFINES:, RESTATES:, RENAMES: or DELETES: as applicable. A deliberate reversal names its earlier change through --reverts.\\\\n\\\\n[check.py](../gates/check.py) runs closure, refusals, size, size_limit, paths, runs, rebase, tcb, vectors, waste, owners and targets. Run it against the proposed tree with the message and changed-path list before landing. Resolve placeholders and conflict markers.\\\\n\\\\nThe default fast judge builds reached Lean targets, checks reached Rust packages and formatting, and runs the axiom/statement census over changed reach. Source-only changes reaching no target or crate need no build slot. A flag-day subject or judge-code change selects the full judge.\\\\n\\\\nFull judgment builds the whole system, checks generated outputs, runs selected simulations/journeys/plants and Rust tests with known-red controls, checks measured waste and workspace checks. [runs.tsv](../gates/runs.tsv), [generated.tsv](../gates/generated.tsv), [waste.tsv](../gates/waste.tsv) and [ceilings.tsv](../gates/ceilings.tsv) define the checks.\\\\n\\\\nConvergence checks the whole system periodically and attributes failures to changes reaching their inputs. Fast landing does not mean every runtime row ran on that candidate. Traced keys permit reuse under the judge'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s rules; outside-tree reads are reported separately.\\\\n\\\\nLaunch the lander detached with its own PID file and retained log. Never stop a running judge. Read a refusal'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s whole log, fix forward and relaunch. Apply the lane'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s resource controls and designated build host to local checks.\\\\n\\\\n[Spec](../Spec/README.md) defines composed assurance claims. The judge also checks engineering properties; a passing build alone does not establish a theorem'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s meaning or non-vacuity.\\\\n\\\",\\\"TESTGROUND\\\":\\\"# The kernel in a browser\\\\n\\\\nThe [testground](../testground/index.html) runs shared Lean kernel and command machinery compiled to WebAssembly. JavaScript handles browser interaction and transport. Local scenarios use a writer in the tab; hosted mode sends writer frames over WebSocket while the follower remains in the tab.\\\\n\\\\nThe [build script](../testground/build/build.sh) accepts c WORK, wasm WORK or all WORK after its path testground/build/build.sh. Use a private WORK directory. The C stage uses the pinned Lean toolchain and builds Web.Sim.Testground'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s closure. The WebAssembly stage requires Emscripten, builds the single-threaded runtime and writes testground/kernel.mjs and kernel.wasm, copying example packages beside the page. The stages can use separate build machines and the generated pack.\\\\n\\\\nPublish the resulting testground/ directory through a static web server, preserving module, WebAssembly and package paths. Use the deployment'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s normal publishing mechanism; the repository has no separate publish command. Serve over HTTP(S), not a local file URL.\\\\n\\\\nFor a hosted World, [run.sh](../deploy/testground/run.sh) accepts genesis STATE, start STATE or stop STATE; PORT selects the loopback relay port. Deployment needs Host and nd-supervise, Node, Python and the built browser kernel. Genesis creates guest-book custody and is an operator action.\\\\n\\\\nThe service has a supervised writer socket, guest-book member enrolling visitors, and byte relay exposing WebSocket and invitation/enrollment endpoints. Expose the loopback relay through the operator'\\''\\'\\'''\\''s TLS proxy. Open the page with ?hosted=wss://HOST/ws&join; each tab is a separate member.\\\\n\\\\nVisitors re-execute replies but obtain executable and sponsor key from the page service, making it part of bootstrap trust. A certificate consumer alone does not independently authenticate either download; see [TRUST](TRUST.md).\\\\n\\\\n[Deployment details](../deploy/testground/README.md), [scenarios](../testground/scenario.mjs) and the [hosted runner](../testground/hosted-run.mjs) show the paths through the programs.\\\\n\\\"}\")
