# nanodregg — the charter (2026-10-16)

Ember: "rebuild whatever shape you think will be better. Go hardcore. ONLY GOOD CODE AND BEST
PRACTICES. All decisions get critically and metacritically examined."

nanodregg is the same end-computing system as minidregg (main 2c27b08b) rebuilt from its spec,
not ported from its code. The spec is: the journey ROWS, the deterministic SIMULATION, THE BAR
(two friends + hosted Hermes over ssh; 1000 records, write ≤ 5 s, cold reopen ≤ 60 s), and the
THEOREM STATEMENTS that mean something (not the vacuous ones; see the discovery dossiers). The
target shape is minidregg's `docs/HP-DESIGN.md` taken literally, and the reorientation documents
in `docs/record/`: `ARCHITECTURE-20261016.md`, `CYCLE-TIME-20261016.md`,
`RECOVERY-20261016.md`, and each `DELETE-<layer>-20261016.md`.

## Scope (Ember, 2026-10-21)

Ember: "we agree that nano has nothing to do with proof systems, host-hiding, or multi-node
agreement." nanodregg is an object of study more than the seed of a prototype: a side exploration,
for diversity and focus, while breadstuffs (the whole system built before it) waits to be resumed
the hybrid exists for.
**Meta-critique.** I lean hybrid because minidregg did it and because "post-quantum" is the fashionable
answer; the honest decider is whether anyone ever follows a log signed today after a CRQC exists. The
option ordering (FFI to HACL* first) is chosen to avoid porting ML-DSA to Lean, the hard part; that
puts unverified-by-us C (but machine-verified upstream) in the TCB.
Status: CLOSED: pure Ed25519 is deployed; native custody uses its Rust signer and Codec.Ed25519 verifies. The hybrid alternative was not selected.
ROOT assigns who owns the Lean verifier. CLIENT implements custody's half the day it is named.

## CLIENT-12 friends over ssh: the remote host.sock (2026-10-16, DESIGN ONLY: waits for PROTOCOL's row)
**The need (THE BAR).** Two friends and a hosted Hermes on one Store. Each friend runs `nd` and their
OWN `host follow` (consent is their own fold, HOST-7) on their own machine; the one writer `host serve`
runs on the operator's box. A friend's nd must reach the remote host.sock, and the friend's follow must
get the log (`log` op, gated in Lean by a follow grant, HOST-11).
(5) The source store (7) is the half of §3.1 a reader of someone else's object needs, and it is not in this build.
**Meta-critique.** I measured the surface against texts that were written by generators, so the drafts benefit from
regularities a hand-written package may not have (Lifecycle is 277 chars because it IS one Std clause). The honest
tests are the two hand ports (the tally, CoAuthor), and the first resident package written in it, which I cannot
measure. I also chose to keep the Lean builders, which keeps my layer's proofs where they are; the reason given (the
meaning theorems are about the builders) is real, and the cost is a text and a builder that a row must keep equal.
Status: SUPERSEDED PROPOSAL: Lang.Surface implements the authoring reader. Source-identity and executable-documentation follow-ons are separate obligations, not asserted complete.
(core, its vacuity read) → `Lang/Surface.lean` + `Read.locate` + `ob` → the three `.ob` texts and rows → tally/
CoAuthor re-cut → theorems 1–3 with teeth and the vacuity read.

**LANG-51 · Built (2026-10-18; LANG-15; ROOT APPROVED both, with conditions).** `Term.refuse (ty : Ty) (why : ByteArray)`
(wire tag 17, `why` the payload, `ty` the one child), `Fault.refused why`, `Prim.lenLe` (code 11; `bytes, nat → bool`,
`a.size ≤ n`: `ByteArray.size`, O(1)). One amendment to the proposal, from the vacuity read: the reason is a LITERAL
(not an evaluated term) and the checker requires `Bounds.reasonOk` (at most `reasonMax` = 256 bytes, UTF-8, no
control character; `badReason` otherwise). A refusal is a name (law 7), so the program's data never chooses it, and
"""gates/fast.py — what one landing is judged by (GATES-22, Ember 10-21: "we don't need to focus on landings as much,
as long as every now and then we make sure the whole system still builds and passes its tests, and do convergences at
those points instead of EVERY LANDING").

  fast.py mode --message FILE --changed FILE     prints `fast` or `full WHY`
  fast.py targets TREE --changed FILE            the lake targets the change reaches, one line (`` = none)
  fast.py crates TREE --changed FILE             `workspace`, the packages to check (one line), or `` = none

THE MODE. A landing is judged in FULL (scripts/pre-push: every target, the census, the selected rows, the waste, the
generated files, the whole Rust workspace) when its subject is a flag day (`kernel!:`) or it changes the JUDGE'S CODE:
gates/*.py, anything under scripts/, or the census's Lean under Census/ (the self-judge rule: main's code judges a judge
change, and a judge change is judged in full). Everything else lands FAST: scripts/commit's stale-path guard
(GATES-20b/20b'), every source gate (gates/check.py, the candidate's and the parent's: owners, closure, refusals, size,
paths, the runs table, tcb, vectors, the waste ratchet, targets), `lake build` of exactly the targets the change reaches
(and the census modules), the declaration diff (gates/decls.py), the axiom and statement census over the reach
(gates/census.py --changed, GATES-20v (c)), `cargo check` and `cargo fmt --check` of the packages it reaches, and
nothing else (no plant: a plant is never a landing gate). A gates/*.tsv change is DATA (20h (b)): it is judged by the source gates
(the ratchets of ceilings.tsv and waste.tsv, runs.tsv's classification) at the landing, and by every row it feeds at
the next convergence.

THE TARGETS. A changed module, every module that imports it (gates/tree.py dependents), and every module that imports
a module this change deleted (its import now dangles): the lean_libs holding one of them and the lean_exes whose import
closure holds one (an exe links: an extern or a missing `main` fails only there). A non-module build input (lakefile.lean,
lean-toolchain, lake-manifest.json, a C source or header) builds every target. A lib target is built whole: lake's traces
rebuild only what the slot's .lake lacks.

THE CRATES. A path under a workspace member reaches that member's package and every member that depends on it by a
path dependency (a changed API breaks its importers); the root Cargo.toml or Cargo.lock, or a manifest that
