# Operating a World

The [RUNBOOK](../deploy/RUNBOOK.md) contains installation/recovery detail. [Host.Cli](../Host/Cli.lean) defines the grammar; [units](../deploy/units/linux) and [exit tables](../deploy/units/EXITS.md) are generated from [Ops.Units](../Ops/Units.lean). Uppercase arguments below stand for operator-supplied values.

| Operation | Form and responsibility |
|---|---|
| Genesis | host genesis STORE NAME [--nonce HEX32] [--certifier HEX32]... [--threshold N] [KEY_HEX NEXT_HEX]... checks founders, authority and budget before creation. Founders independently check the inputs. |
| Writer | host serve STORE [--retain N] serves framed requests. The key-file handle holds the writer lock. Default retention keeps history; an explicit count must meet the floor. |
| Follower | host follow STORE MEMBER_HEX GRANT_HEX owns a separate writable Store and executes member commands. |
| Join | host join STORE INVITE_HEX KEY_HEX [IN] authenticates the invitation under the trusted sponsor key. IN is for a carried invitation. |
| Inspect | host check STORE [--anchor STEPS:CHAIN_HEX] checks history against a held position. The anchor counts steps, not heights. |
| Seal | host seal STORE [--retain N] seals a stopped Store. |
| Carry | host export STORE OUT exports a stopped Store. Host import takes STORE NAME IN with either --digest HEX and optional --nonce HEX32, or --origin HEX with --nonce HEX32. Preserve the checked export pin. |

nd-supervise confines the child and multiplexes bounded socket connections onto framed stdio. Policy grants Store writes and executable access while hiding custody roots. Linux needs the fixed sandbox mechanism and its installation permissions; absence has no permissive fallback.

| Unit | Role |
|---|---|
| nd-host-serve.service | Writer under nd. |
| nd-host-follow.service | Personal follower; its own Store and hidden workspace/holding directories. |
| nd-hosted-follow@.service | Hosted follower under nd-hosted; shared socket group for its driver. |
| nd-follow-drive@.service | nd sync under nd; Restart=always, forty-second interval, except its fatal refusal class. |
| nd-keybroker.service | Provider keys, anchors and pins under nd; confined Lean decider. |
| nd-members@.service | World resident decider and managed follower/member children. |
| nd-grain-launch@.service | Controller and confined grains under nd-hosted. |

nd-members separates member sockets, custody, follower state and follower sockets. Launcher roots must also be disjoint. Ownership, modes and directory chains are checked; a matching path is insufficient. Rendered tmpfiles/sysusers definitions supply identities and modes.

A resident sees its package, writable volume and own member/broker sockets. It reads the Bell, keeps intent before asking, persists the answer before submitting and settles delivery through its member. The broker checks provider policy and custody; the provider secret does not enter the resident volume.

Uncertain is an unknown outcome: use member settlement. Do not invent missing Store files or start a second writer. The [Store layout](../deploy/units/STORE.md) gives backup order: key, atomic base, index, segments. Preserve the backup together and check the restored Store against a held steps/chain anchor. Retention can make older anchors unavailable.

The optional base.cert sidecar is self-authenticating to its consumer, not a replacement for the base/log. [TRUST](TRUST.md) explains the producer boundary.
# Changing the system

scripts/commit snapshots named paths, merges against current main, builds a private candidate, regenerates SIZE, judges and lands by compare-and-swap. Conflict and stale-path refusals identify what must be resolved; moved main is merged and judged again.

The invocation accepts -m MESSAGE or -F FILE, optional --cross-layer REASON and --reverts COMMIT, then the exact paths. The subject layer is resolved by [OWNERS](../gates/OWNERS). CROSS-LAYER: at column zero must name foreign owners.

The message explains behavior and the layer decision with critique/meta-critique. Include SIZE: METRIC: reason for each required rise. Declaration changes need the stale-path guard's map: RESTATES-MEANING:, RESTATES:, RENAMES:, DELETES: or DELETES-MODULE: as applicable. A deliberate reversal names its earlier change through --reverts.

[check.py](../gates/check.py) runs closure, refusals, size, size_limit, paths, runs, rebase, tcb, vectors, waste, owners and targets. Run it against the proposed tree with the message and changed-path list before landing. Resolve placeholders and conflict markers.

The default fast judge builds reached Lean targets, checks reached Rust packages and formatting, and runs the axiom/statement census over changed reach. Source-only changes reaching no target or crate need no build slot. A flag-day subject or judge-code change selects the full judge.

Full judgment builds the whole system, checks generated outputs, runs selected simulations/journeys/plants and Rust tests with known-red controls, checks measured waste and workspace checks. [runs.tsv](../gates/runs.tsv), [generated.tsv](../gates/generated.tsv), [waste.tsv](../gates/waste.tsv) and [ceilings.tsv](../gates/ceilings.tsv) define the checks.

Convergence checks the whole system periodically and attributes failures to changes reaching their inputs. Fast landing does not mean every runtime row ran on that candidate. Traced keys permit reuse under the judge's rules; outside-tree reads are reported separately.

Launch the lander detached with its own PID file and retained log. Never stop a running judge. Read a refusal's whole log, fix forward and relaunch. Apply the lane's resource controls and designated build host to local checks.

[Spec](../Spec/README.md) defines composed assurance claims. The judge also checks engineering properties; a passing build alone does not establish a theorem's meaning or non-vacuity.
# The kernel in a browser

The [testground](../testground/index.html) runs shared Lean kernel and command machinery compiled to WebAssembly. JavaScript handles browser interaction and transport. Local scenarios use a writer in the tab; hosted mode sends writer frames over WebSocket while the follower remains in the tab.

The [build script](../testground/build/build.sh) accepts c WORK, wasm WORK or all WORK after its path testground/build/build.sh. Use a private WORK directory. The C stage uses the pinned Lean toolchain and builds Web.Sim.Testground's closure. The WebAssembly stage requires Emscripten, builds the single-threaded runtime and writes testground/kernel.mjs and kernel.wasm, copying example packages beside the page. The stages can use separate build machines and the generated pack.

Publish the resulting testground/ directory through a static web server, preserving module, WebAssembly and package paths. Use the deployment's normal publishing mechanism; the repository has no separate publish command. Serve over HTTP(S), not a local file URL.

For a hosted World, [run.sh](../deploy/testground/run.sh) accepts genesis STATE, start STATE or stop STATE; PORT selects the loopback relay port. Deployment needs Host and nd-supervise, Node, Python and the built browser kernel. Genesis creates guest-book custody and is an operator action.

The service has a supervised writer socket, guest-book member enrolling visitors, and byte relay exposing WebSocket and invitation/enrollment endpoints. Expose the loopback relay through the operator's TLS proxy. Open the page with ?hosted=wss://HOST/ws&join; each tab is a separate member.

Visitors re-execute replies but obtain executable and sponsor key from the page service, making it part of bootstrap trust. A certificate consumer alone does not independently authenticate either download; see [TRUST](TRUST.md).

[Deployment details](../deploy/testground/README.md), [scenarios](../testground/scenario.mjs) and the [hosted runner](../testground/hosted-run.mjs) show the paths through the programs.

