### client/src/mux.rs
1: //! The supervisor's core: many Unix-socket connections onto one Lean child's stdio.
2: //!
3: //! Client side, per connection: frames `u32le len ‖ op ‖ payload` (see [`crate::frame`]).
4: //! Child side: the same frame whose body is `u32le conn ‖ op ‖ payload` toward the child and
5: //! `u32le conn ‖ status ‖ payload` back. The mux parses no payload and decides nothing about a
6: //! request: a reply for a connection that is gone is dropped (the client's recovery is lookup),
7: //! and when the child closes its stdout every connection is closed `unreachable` and [`serve`]
8: //! returns: the supervisor exits and the service manager restarts the pair (one restart path).
9: //!
10: //! What the mux does decide is when a CONNECTION ends, with Lean's numbers and Lean's names
11: //! (`Protocol.Bounds`, `Protocol.CloseReason`, emitted into [`crate::generated`]):
12: //! - each connection has its own writer thread fed one reply at a time, so a peer that stops
13: //!   reading is closed `replyBacklog` and never stalls the routing of anyone else's replies;
14: //! - a connection is `idle` only when it is silent at a frame boundary with no request in flight;
15: //!   a request in flight longer than `REPLY_MS_MAX` is `replyTimeout` (a Host that loses a reply
16: //!   must not wedge the connection forever); a started frame must finish within `FRAME_MS_MAX`;
17: //! - a connection is one fd; `end` takes its writer's sender, so the writer drains at most a reply
18: //!   and the close (each write within `reply_write`) and returns; the connection counts against
19: //!   `connections_max` until its fd closes, so [`MuxLimits::fds_max`] bounds the process (CLIENT-28:
20: //!   the writer once held its own sender and outlived every connection, two fds and a thread each);
21: //! - every close is named to the peer: one frame `status::CLOSED ‖ CloseReason::name()`, written
22: //!   best-effort by the connection's writer (one writer per stream, so frames never interleave),
23: //!   then the stream is shut down. The over-the-bound connection is answered `full` the same way,
24: //!   and a `closed` reply from the child (`nd-attach` relaying a remote close) ends the connection.
25: //!
26: //! `conn` is a counter, never an identity.
27: 
