### launcher/src/bin/keybroker.rs
1: //! `keybroker KEYS ANCHORS PINS SOCKDIR PIDS UPSTREAM STORE OPERATOR PEER DECIDER`
2: //!
3: //! The provider-key broker's byte mover (HOSTING-12). It holds the operator's provider keys (the
4: //! file KEYS, read once under the custody checks of `nanodregg_sandbox::custody`) and the TLS trust
5: //! anchors (the PEM file ANCHORS, the operator's data: in production the installed copy of
6: //! `deploy/tls/anchors.pem`, read once under `custody::read_public`), the leaf-key pins (the file
7: //! PINS, the operator's data shaped like ANCHORS: `HOST SHA256-HEX` lines, the SHA-256 of the
8: //! provider's leaf certificate's SubjectPublicKeyInfo; in production the installed copy of
9: //! `deploy/tls/pins`, emitted from Lean's `Hosting.Pins.pins`, HOSTING-28), and serves one Unix
10: //! socket per resident, `SOCKDIR/<resident>/broker.sock` (HOSTING-17), for exactly the residents the
11: //! World holds: it asks its decider `residents` at start and every `TICK`, binds a socket for each
12: //! name newly answered (a resident grain's birth) and closes and removes the socket of each name no
13: //! longer answered (its removal) (HOSTING-26; no operator list). SOCKDIR is the operator's (nd:nd-hosted, mode 02710, every directory above
14: //! it StrictModes-clean); keybroker makes each resident's directory in it (02710, SOCKDIR's group by
15: //! the setgid bit), puts nothing there but the socket, and sets the socket 0660 (0600 when PEER is
16: //! keybroker's own uid: a one-uid box, where no group is needed and the sandbox's connect rule
17: //! accepts exactly 0600; HOSTING-17 §5, HOSTING-21). Anything else at
18: //! those paths ends it `sockDir` before any socket opens. On every accept the peer's credentials must
19: //! be uid PEER (the hosted uid) and SOCKDIR's group, or the connection is answered `peerRefused`.
20: //! Identity is the socket: keybroker puts a `decide` frame naming that socket's resident in front
21: //! of each ask, unread, and hands both to DECIDER (the Lean `hosting-broker`, started confined as
22: //! `DECIDER T0 SLOTS STORE OPERATOR`: the operator clock, the comma-separated key slots the key file
23: //! holds (so Lean refuses `keyMissing` before it charges anything), and the World it follows for
24: //! the allowances OPERATOR owns, which it may read and nothing else). A `grant` names a key slot and the
25: //! upstream request Lean printed; keybroker dials UPSTREAM (`addr:port`, the operator's dial
26: //! address), speaks TLS 1.3 (rustls with the ring provider, HOSTING-15) with SNI and certificate
27: //! verification for the HOST the grant names (Lean's provider table, never the dial address), then
28: //! checks that the verified leaf's key is one PINS names for that host (`pinMismatch` otherwise,
29: //! before any request byte, a host with no pin included), sends the request (HTTP/1.0, `Host:`
30: //! Lean's host) with the slot's key in the header Lean named, and hands the upstream's status and
31: //! body back to the decider (`reply`), which answers the reply TEXT Lean read from the provider's
32: //! JSON, the status, or `replyMalformed` (HOSTING-29): the resident never receives the provider's
33: //! body. A response holding the key is `keyEchoed` before Lean sees it, and a reply text holding it
34: //! is `keyEchoed` before the resident does (an escape can spell the key without its bytes). A TLS
35: //! failure is `tlsCertificate`, `tlsHostname` or `tlsProtocol`, never the upstream's text. It
36: //! decides nothing: every verdict is the decider's, and every way it ends is a name on stderr. One
37: //! ask at a time per resident (`busy`).
38: 
