### client/src/attempt.rs
//! The life of one signed call, frozen on disk so a crash cannot change what was sent.
//!
//! An attempt is a directory. `call` holds the exact signed bytes and is written once, after
//! `argv` (the command it was signed for, as the generated `Command` encodes it) and `cont`
//! (follow's continuation at the submit step), so a directory with `call` is complete. Before
//! each send a marker `sent.<n>` is written. The settlement is one file, `settled`, holding the
//! three lines the member's OWN follow printed for `receipt CALL` (PROTOCOL-46): the receipt, the
//! head it was checked against, and the `settle` verdict. Never the Host's reply: the Host's status
//! byte only steers the loop (when to look up, when to resend the same bytes). The state is which
//! files exist; any other file refuses to load:
//!
//! | files (beside `argv`, `cont`) | state                 |
//! |-------------------------------|-----------------------|
//! | `call`                        | [`State::Signed`]     |
//! | `call`, `sent.*`              | [`State::Uncertain`]  |
//! | `call`, `sent.*`, `settled`   | [`State::Settled`]    |
//! | no `call` (a crashed freeze)  | none: never sent      |
//!
//! WHAT to do next is `Protocol.Attempt.next`, emitted as [`crate::generated::attempt::next`];
//! this module performs the action it names and keeps the files. A Host's claimed final (`ok`,
//! `refused`) is `confirm`ed, and a lookup's `absent` with no send left is `probe`d, both by the
//! member's follow; only its `committed` or `refused` writes `settled`. Every other end (declined,
//! open, contradicted, uncertain, badReply) leaves the attempt open on disk, and the next run of the
//! SAME argv continues it ([`Attempt::open_for`], CLIENT-37): it drives the frozen call from where
//! the disk says it stands and hands follow the attempt's own continuation, so the command's end is
//! that call's, and no second call is signed beside it. Which verdict a `settle` line names is the
//! generated `settle_seen`, not this crate.

### client/src/custody.rs
//! Custody of one member's keys (CLIENT-13, ruled by Ember 2026-10-16): the CURRENT key signs every
//! call; the precommitted NEXT key signs the `rotate` that makes it current (KERNEL-6) and, once that
//! rotate is signed and armed, read envelopes until the rotation settles (CLIENT-27). The
//! next key is fresh OS randomness born at precommit, never derivable from the current one, held
//! where a theft of the workspace does not reach it:
//! - [`Separate`] (a member): `ws/seed` (current secret), `ws/next.pub` (next PUBLIC key), and the
//!   next secret in `HOLDING/next` (`HOLDING/pending` mid-rotation), on removable media or under
//!   another account, needed only to rotate.
//! - [`SameRecord`] (a hosted resident, which has no offline): `ws/keys = current ‖ next
//!   [‖ pending]`, replaced atomically; a thief of it has both keys, so for it the `rotate` row
//!   holds only against a leak of the current key alone.
//!
//! Every custody file is read under the custody checks and written durably (`fsx`). Custody signs
//! the bytes it is handed (what is signed is follow's decision) and advances ONLY on its own
//! settlement of the rotate call (`settle`), never on a follow verb; each advance step is guarded by
//! the rotation's public keys, so a crash anywhere is finished by the next `settle`.

### client/src/frame.rs
//! One frame: a `u32` little-endian body length, then the body, which is one operation byte
//! followed by the payload. The body length counts the operation byte, so it is at least 1.
//!
//! The bound is the codec table's `frameBytesMax`, passed in as [`Limits`]. A length past the
//! bound is refused before any allocation; a stream that ends inside a frame is `Truncated`,
//! never a short frame. Time is bounded per REPLY, not per syscall, by [`Deadline`].
//!
//! The layout is Lean's (CLIENT-40): a frame is `Protocol.socketFrame` (`Stream.bytesOf`), its
//! length prefix `FRAME_LEN_BYTES` wide, its body read by `Protocol.bodyParts?`; the child wire's
//! body is `Host.Wire.decodeBody`'s `u32le conn ‖ byte ‖ payload` ([`child_parts`]). Each refusal
//! renders as Lean's verdict on the same bytes (`client/tests/vectors.rs`, the codec vectors'
//! `wire/frame` and `wire/child` rows).

### client/src/fsx.rs
//! Durable files. Every workspace write goes through here, and a crash leaves each file either
//! absent or complete-and-durable: its bytes are written whole and synced in `.NAME.tmp` (mode 0600,
//! `O_EXCL`), and only then given its name, by a hard link for `create_once` (which, unlike a
//! rename, refuses an existing name) or a rename for `replace`; the directory is synced. A `.NAME.tmp`
//! is fsx's own residue of a crash, incomplete by construction: the next write of NAME removes it,
//! and a reader of the directory passes over it ([`is_residue`]). Custody files are read by
//! `nanodregg_sandbox::custody`, the one read every key holder shares.

