### client/src/join.rs
//! A member's own Store from an invitation its sponsor signed (CLIENT-26, PROTOCOL-42):
//! `nd --join KEY_FILE INVITATION STORE /ABS/HOST`. The sponsor's public key arrives as a FILE
//! (the trust root, read under custody's chain rule); the invitation as a hex ARGUMENT (untrusted
//! until Lean accepts it). nd decides nothing: `host join` runs `Invite.accept` under the key and
//! writes STORE only if it passed (`join_genesis_iff`); `init SPONSOR` is follow's. nd keeps two
//! non-secret files: `invitation` (read by `nd adopt KEY_FILE`) and `input`, the World's genesis
//! inputs as host printed them (read by bare `nd invite`; a founder keeps its own with `nd --input
//! HEX`).

### client/src/lib.rs
//! The member's side of nanodregg: custody (the seed, the frozen attempts), transport (frames
//! over a socket) and the workspace on disk, plus the supervisor that puts a Lean child behind a
//! Unix socket.
//!
//! This crate decides nothing. Its only protocol knowledge is [`generated`], emitted from
//! `Protocol/Table.lean`; the library functions take the values they need as parameters and
//! [`table`] builds those parameters from the generated file, nowhere else.

### client/src/mux.rs
//! The supervisor's core: many Unix-socket connections onto one Lean child's stdio.
//!
//! Client side, per connection: frames `u32le len ‖ op ‖ payload` (see [`crate::frame`]).
//! Child side: the same frame whose body is `u32le conn ‖ op ‖ payload` toward the child and
//! `u32le conn ‖ status ‖ payload` back. The mux parses no payload and decides nothing about a
//! request: a reply for a connection that is gone is dropped (the client's recovery is lookup),
//! and when the child closes its stdout every connection is closed `unreachable` and [`serve`]
//! returns: the supervisor exits and the service manager restarts the pair (one restart path).
//!
//! What the mux does decide is when a CONNECTION ends, with Lean's numbers and Lean's names
//! (`Protocol.Bounds`, `Protocol.CloseReason`, emitted into [`crate::generated`]):
//! - each connection has its own writer thread fed one reply at a time, so a peer that stops
//!   reading is closed `replyBacklog` and never stalls the routing of anyone else's replies;
//! - a connection is `idle` only when it is silent at a frame boundary with no request in flight;
//!   a request in flight longer than `REPLY_MS_MAX` is `replyTimeout` (a Host that loses a reply
//!   must not wedge the connection forever); a started frame must finish within `FRAME_MS_MAX`;
//! - a connection is one fd; `end` takes its writer's sender, so the writer drains at most a reply
//!   and the close (each write within `reply_write`) and returns; the connection counts against
//!   `connections_max` until its fd closes, so [`MuxLimits::fds_max`] bounds the process (CLIENT-28:
//!   the writer once held its own sender and outlived every connection, two fds and a thread each);
//! - every close is named to the peer: one frame `status::CLOSED ‖ CloseReason::name()`, written
//!   best-effort by the connection's writer (one writer per stream, so frames never interleave),
//!   then the stream is shut down. The over-the-bound connection is answered `full` the same way,
//!   and a `closed` reply from the child (`nd-attach` relaying a remote close) ends the connection.
//!
//! `conn` is a counter, never an identity.

### client/src/run.rs
//! One command: execute the steps `host follow` asks for until it says done (CLIENT-2, HOST-9).
//!
//! The order of steps, what is signed and what is printed are follow's. This loop only performs a
//! step and hands its result back with the continuation, under two bounds from the table: steps
//! per command and sends per frozen call. A step is the generated codec's value
//! ([`crate::generated::layout::Step`], byte for byte `Protocol.Frames`); this module never
//! sees step bytes. A command whose argv has an attempt left open by an earlier run IS that
//! attempt (CLIENT-37, [`run`]).

### client/src/sockets.rs
//! The two sockets of a workspace as the step loop's [`Follow`] and [`Host`].

### client/src/stdio.rs
//! `nd --stdio` (HOSTING-20, CLIENT-24): nd-supervise's child serving one hosted resident over the
//! mux's child wire. Each request is one nd command (`follow_op::COMMAND ‖ layout::Command`), each
//! reply its done step (`status::OK ‖ layout::CommandReply`). The surface is this mode's property,
//! never a peer's choice: a verb not in `STDIO_VERBS` (flags and custody words included) is refused
//! `notOnSurface` before follow sees it; a request that does not decode is `undecodable`; custody
//! is opened rotation-refusing (`noRotation`); the workspace and the two sockets are nd's argv at
//! start (`nd --stdio WORKSPACE FOLLOW_SOCK HOST_SOCK`; the sandbox clears the environment). No argument is ever resolved against the file system (nothing in nd or follow reads an
//! argv path).

