### Codec/Deployed.lean
/-!
# Codec.Deployed — the one deployed `Env` and `Ctx`

The kernel is generic in its digest and hash. This module fixes them for deployment: digests are
`Codec.Digest` (32 bytes), the hash is cSHAKE256 with the domain as customization (`Hash.digest`),
turns, cells and calls are encoded by `Codec.Kernel`, and signatures are checked by RFC 8032
Ed25519 (`Ed25519.verify`). LANG supplies the `Program`. The facts the kernel's theorems take as
premises are proved for this instance in `Codec.Proofs.Deployed`.
-/

### Codec/Digest.lean
/-! # Digest — the one digest type

A digest is exactly 32 bytes. It is never a natural number: ordering, trie index
bits and equality are read off the bytes. Hash functions produce it
(`Codec.Hash`); codecs carry it as 32 raw bytes (`Codec.Stream.digest`). -/

### Codec/Ed25519.lean
/-!
# Ed25519 — signature verification (RFC 8032 §5.1.7), cofactored

Field elements are ten unboxed `UInt64` limbs (radix 2^25.5). The product is generated
schoolbook arithmetic, folded by `2^255 ≡ 19`, and no intermediate exceeds 2^63. Points are
extended twisted-Edwards coordinates (RFC 8032 §5.1.4). Verification computes `[S]B - [k]A` in one
double-and-add pass (Shamir's trick) and checks `[8]([S]B - [k]A) = [8]R`. It refuses a
non-canonical `S` (≥ L), a point encoding with `y ≥ p`, and a point that does not decompress. Every
loop is structural on a bit count. RFC 8032 vectors: `Codec.Proofs.Ed25519.{Test1, Test2, Tampered}`.
-/

### Codec/Hash.lean
/-!
# Hash — Keccak-f[1600], SHA3-256, cSHAKE256 and KMAC256 (FIPS 202, NIST SP 800-185)

The one hash family. `Hash.digest domain msg` is cSHAKE256 with `domain` as the customization
string: every digest the system forms is domain-separated by construction, because SP 800-185
encodes the customization with its length (`encodeString`), so no domain is a prefix of another.
`Hash.mac key domain msg` is KMAC256 with a 32-byte key: the Host's log and base seal.

The permutation works on 25 unboxed `UInt64` lanes of a structure, one round unrolled (generated from
the FIPS 202 ρ and π tables): no array access can fail, no lane is boxed, and there is no `partial def`. Every loop is structural on a block count. The conformance
vectors (FIPS 202 and SP 800-185 samples) are theorems in `Codec.Proofs.Hash`.
-/

### Codec/Kernel.lean
/-!
# Codec.Kernel — the canonical bytes of KERNEL's values

One codec per kernel type, built from the one Stream family. Each is generic in the digest's own
codec `dc : Stream D` (the deployed 32-byte digest, or a simulation's toy digest). Kernel types carry
plain `Nat`, `List` and `ByteArray` with bounds checked in `World.admit`. So each codec is lawful on
the values within those bounds (`Codec.Proofs.Kernel`): every admitted Turn has exactly one spelling,
and every decoded value is within bounds.

The definitions below are the layout authority: `turn`, `unsigned` and `body` include
all fields of their kernel types. Fixed-width fields and canonical varints coexist.
Rows have distinct storage and pin encodings: `rowLeaf` uses compact counters, while
`rowLeafHashed` spells the pin preimage. Their agreement is proved in `Codec.Proofs.Rows`.
-/

### Codec/Layout.lean
/-! # Layout — a frame's fields, as data

A `Layout` is a list of named fields. It is data, so the protocol table can carry it, the Rust
emitter can print a reader and writer for it, and `Layout.codec` turns it into the one `Stream`
that Lean speaks. Every byte-string field shares one bound `max`, the frame bound: a field cannot
be longer than the frame that carries it. -/

### Codec/Path.lean
/-!
# Codec.Path — a sandbox path: clean components, joined by `/`

A path relative to an operator's root is a list of components; on the wire it is ONE byte string,
the components joined by `/` (47), so the Rust side hands exactly those bytes to the sandbox and
assembles nothing (law 2). A component is CLEAN: nonempty, at most `componentMax` bytes, not `.`
or `..`, and free of the `forbidden` bytes (`/` and NUL). Over clean components the join is
injective and `split` inverts it (`Codec.Proofs.Path`), so the wire spelling is canonical.

`clean`, `join` and `split` index the bytes directly (no `List UInt8`, law 3) and are structural on
the size, so they reduce in the kernel: a `decide` over a literal path checks. The Rust emitter
prints the same rule from `componentMax`, `forbidden` and `reserved`.
-/

### Codec/Prime.lean
/-!
# Codec.Prime — modular exponentiation, for primality certificates (CODEC-23)

`powMod b e m n` is `b ^ e % m` for `e < 2 ^ n`, by square-and-multiply. It is structural on `n`, so the
kernel reduces it on literals: a Pratt certificate for the Ed25519 field prime `2^255 − 19` checks by
`decide +kernel` (`Codec.Proofs.Prime`).
-/

### Codec/Raw.lean
/-!
# Raw — codecs over unbounded types, canonical on a domain

KERNEL's types carry plain `Nat`, `List` and `ByteArray` and check their bounds in `World.admit`
(`checkBounds`), not in the types. These codecs write any value, but read only values in their
domain: `nat64` reads naturals below 2^64, `rawBytes max` byte strings of at most `max` bytes, and
`rawList max c` lists of at most `max` elements of `c`'s domain. They are lawful ON that domain
(`Codec.Proofs.Raw`, `LawfulOn`): every value in the domain round-trips, and every value read is in the
domain and was spelled exactly one way. Outside the domain, `put` truncates, and nothing ever reads
such a value back.
-/

### Codec/Refusal.lean
/-! # Codec refusals

Every way a decoder refuses, by name, with the byte offset at which it refused.
A refusal is never a value: a decoder returns `Except Refusal`, and the layer that
answers a request embeds `Refusal` in its own named refusal. -/

### Codec/Sha512.lean
/-!
# Sha512 — SHA-512 (FIPS 180-4), for Ed25519 (RFC 8032)

Eight unboxed `UInt64` working variables in a structure; the 80-word schedule of one block is a
`Vector`; every loop is structural on a block or round count. Vectors: `Codec.Proofs.Sha512`.
-/

### Codec/Stream.lean
/-! # Stream — the one codec family

A `Stream α` writes a value by appending its bytes to a buffer and reads one
value at an offset of a `ByteArray`, returning the offset after it. It never
decodes through a `List UInt8` and does not re-encode to check itself. Byte-string
readers extract their payload; scalar/composite readers advance an offset.
Canonicity (`Codec.Stream.Lawful`, in `Codec.Proofs.Stream`) is a
theorem about each combinator, proved once.

Every codec carries its `Shape`. A tag is the hash of a label and a shape
(`Codec.Tag`), so a codec whose structure changes gets a new tag without anyone
editing a literal.

The family includes fixed-width little-endian integers, big-endian ordering encodings and
canonical varints. Bounded combinators carry limits in their value types (`Bytes max`,
`Bounded max α`); `Codec.Raw` supplies codecs lawful on explicit bounds for raw values.
A reader refuses a declared length past its bound before reading that payload. -/

### Codec/Tag.lean
/-!
# Tag — content-addressed codec identities

A codec's tag is the digest of a label and the codec's `Shape`: `Tag.of label c =
Hash.digest "nanodregg/codec/tag/v1" (label ‖ shape bytes)`. Change a codec's structure and its
tag changes with it. Nobody edits a version literal, no census lists the tags, and no fingerprint is
pinned. A Store stamped with the tags of the codecs that wrote it refuses to open under codecs
with other tags: an old shape refuses, it is never reinterpreted. A `refine`'s name is the one part
the shape takes on trust: the shape cannot see inside a function. Its accepted set is pinned by its
lawfulness theorem (the refine census) and its meaning by the codec vectors, which go red when a
meaning changes under an unchanged tag (CODEC-28).
-/

### Codec/Tree.lean
/-!
# Tree — the one recursive codec

A recursive type (LANG's terms and types) is encoded as a `Tree`: a node is a tag byte, a payload
written with the ordinary codecs, and its children. The recursion lives here, once, depth-bounded:
a reader nests at most `depth` levels, and anything deeper is refused (`tooDeep`) before it is
read. A type becomes a `Stream` with `Stream.viaTree`, a refinement of `tree`. Its author proves
only that `toTree` and `ofTree` are inverse (`refine_lawful`); the byte-level canonicity is
`tree_lawful`, proved here.

Wire: `u8 tag ‖ varint |payload| ‖ payload ‖ varint |children| ‖ child*` (CODEC-13: LEB128, shortest form,
so a small node's framing is three bytes).
-/

### Codec/Varint.lean
/-!
# Varint — LEB128 naturals, shortest form only

A natural as base-128 digits, least significant first, each byte's top bit set when another byte
follows: 0–127 is one byte, below 2^14 two. Two widths share one reader (`getVarint width limit`):
`varint`, below 2^32 in at most five bytes (lengths, counts), and `varint64`, below 2^64 in at most
ten (KERNEL-54's per-cell versions and rows floors on the World wire). A decoder refuses an overlong
spelling (a last byte of 0 after a continuation, which a shorter spelling would have omitted), a byte
past the width, and a value at or past the limit. So every value has exactly one spelling
(`Codec.Proofs.Varint`).
-/

### Codec/World.lean
/-!
# Codec.World — the canonical bytes of a whole World (the checkpoint, HOST-14's base)

`trie ‖ journal ‖ refusals ‖ rows ‖ height u64 ‖ steps u64 ‖ chain ‖ allowance u64 ‖ origin`. The World's four maps are tries
(KERNEL-23 cells and spent nullifiers; KERNEL-41 the journal, under `journalSpec`; KERNEL-44 the
refusals, under `refusalSpec`; KERNEL-48 the rows, under `rowSpec`), and each is written
as its listing (`Trie.T.toList`, left to right by key bits) and read back by `Trie.build` over that
listing (`listingAccept`): every node hash is recomputed, none is ever read from the wire (CODEC-19), and
a listing is refused by name unless it is exactly the rebuilt trie's own (no key twice in a leaf).
The cells' listing is refused `kernel/leaf-fit` unless every slot holds the leaf of its kind, and
`kernel/trie-order` unless it is the canonical trie's; the journal's is refused
`kernel/journal-order` unless it is the canonical journal trie's (at the deployed digest: strictly
ascending by transaction id, `Codec.Deployed.journalOrder_iff`); the refusals' `kernel/refusal-order`
likewise. So every map reads back EXACTLY
(`build_toList`), decode ∘ bytes is the identity (`world_lawfulOn`), and the bytes are a function of
the contents. The World is refused `kernel/journal` unless its journal is the window's (KERNEL-39's
`journalFits`, every entry at or below the height and inside the window, one entry per height), so a
decoded base carries `World.JournalOk` (`world_decode_journalOk`); `kernel/refusals` unless its refusals
are the window's (KERNEL-47's `refusalsFits`), so it carries `World.RefusalsOk`
(`world_decode_refusalsOk`); `kernel/steps` unless its height is at most its step count; and
`kernel/rows` / `kernel/rows-headed` / `kernel/rows-parented` unless its rows fit, every head counts its
rows and every relation's parent is live (KERNEL-48).
`world.size w` is the encoded length, computed from the entries without encoding (HOST's
`worldBytesMax`).
-/

### Codec/WorldSize.lean
/-!
# Codec.WorldSize — the measures a World's encoded size is stated in (cv 01a150a3-2523)

The named numbers HOST reads for its `worldBytesMax` (`entrySlack`, `worldFixed`, `journalBytes`,
`refusalsBytes`) and the per-entry counts the exact size names; their theorems are
`Codec.Proofs.WorldSize`'s (`world_size_eq`, `world_size_le`). Specification measures, O(World), never
computed on the request path.
-/

### Hosting/Resident/Args.lean
/-
# Hosting.Resident.Args — a grain's argv is its plan, joined onto the operator's roots (HOSTING-27)

grain-launch starts every grain with its launch frame's paths as argv, one field after another
in `Plan` order (`launcher::plan_argv`): the program, then for each of read, write, exec, broker and
member a count followed by that many absolute paths. Hidden paths are not passed, since they name
what the grain must not see. The sandbox clears the environment, and `IO.appPath` is absent under
bwrap (no /proc), so argv is the only way a confined grain can learn where it is.

The resident trusts none of it until it is its plan. `place` reads the label, the grain id and the
entry out of the program path (`…/worlds/<l>/grains/<id>/pkg/<entry>`), computes Lean's own
`plan l g` for that resident grain, takes the three roots off the front of the paths, and accepts
only if the argv is EXACTLY that plan joined onto those roots, field for field
(`Proofs.Resident.place_render`). A reordered field (member sockets where broker sockets belong),
a dropped path or a stray one is refused `argvNotPlan`.
-/

### Hosting/Resident/Bell.lean
/-
# Hosting.Resident.Bell — what the resident decides, as pure functions (HOSTING-27, HOSTING-31)

The resident serves ONE bell (`world/resident/Bell.ob`, LANG-58: a queue of rings) through its
member socket:
- It reads the bell's description once (`read OBJ ?`, PROTOCOL-44). Its `core` line, the package
  cell's bytes, must decode to the Bell (`bellCore`: `Lang.Bell.shapeOk`, HOSTING-45), or the
  resident ends `bellShape notBell` before any read or ask: the object the config names is the
  program the theorems speak of. From it come the index of the
  method labelled `answer`, whose argument must be `answerArgTy`, and the relation `pending`, whose
  key must be `(notBefore nat) (agent digest)` and whose value holds the captured `ask` plan. No index,
  bound or relation layout is written into the program; the read's K is `pending`'s declared limit.
- Each tick it makes ONE read, `read OBJ pending K` (PROTOCOL-51's range read): every unanswered
  ring, one `Lang.Print.data` line per row, read with `Lang.Read.readData` at the relation's row
  type (`Lang.rowTy`). The range read refuses past K and never truncates, so with K the declared
  limit the lines are ALL the pending rings (`Protocol.Proofs.Command.rangeLines_reads`), in
  ascending key order, which is ring order (`Lang.Proofs.KeyOrder.range_keys_ascend_read`).
- It answers the lowest pending ring: `invoke OBJ <answer> ARG`, ARG `Lang.Print.data` of
  `(record (notBefore N) (ringText X) (ringer #H) (text T) (to A))`, X the ring's text and A its
  optional AIM (HOSTING-53: `(none)`, or `(some (record (cand #C) (method M) (target #O) (via #G)))`) as
  the pending row holds them. The bell (LANG-59) upserts `rings[k]` to an `answered (some (ask T))`
  with immutable `captured (ask (record (text X) (to A)))` and retracts `pending[k]`; its `rings`
  guard refuses the upsert unless the ring is unanswered and the capture echoed is the ring's own
  (`rung: answered`, `rung: text`), so exactly-once is a check on the row as it is, and an aim cannot
  be dropped or altered by the resident. For an aimed ring the bell's `answer` also makes ONE nested
  call, the forward of T to the aim's target (`Lang.Proofs.Bell.answer_run`): answered is delivered,
  in the same Turn. The resident neither reads nor pins the target: the aim it echoes is judged by
  the guard, the forward by the kernel's authority on the Bell object's grant (`via`).

Everything it reads and writes is Lang's one reader and one printer. It holds no copy of either.

**Exactly one ask per ring** (each ask costs the meter). A ring is its key `(notBefore, ringer)`
and its text. An ask is SPENT once its frame has been sent in full. From then on the ring's answer
text is decided, whatever comes back: the reply's text, or a short text naming what came instead.
That text is kept in the resident's volume, with the ring, until the ring leaves `pending`, so
neither a failed invoke nor a restart asks again. A ring that arrives BELOW the kept one (a ringer
may backdate its notBefore by up to the kernel's window) waits until the kept ring is delivered: the
kept ring is answered first wherever it stands, and only then is the lowest asked about
(`Proofs.Resident.decide_*`, over `decideTick`).
-/

### Hosting/Resident/Run.lean
/-
# Hosting.Resident.Run — `resident`: THE BAR's hosted Hermes (HOSTING-27, HOSTING-31)

    resident <the plan's paths, as grain-launch passes them>

A resident grain's program (`pkg/resident`), confined by grain-launch like any grain. It can see its
package (read), its volume (read and write), its own broker socket and its own member socket, and
nothing else. Its argv must be its plan (`Args.place`), or it ends `argvNotPlan` before reading
anything. It reads `pkg/resident.conf`, ONE Lang data value at `Bell.confTy`. Then, every
`tickMs`, for at most `lifetimeTicks` ticks:
1. Until it has the bell's shape, it describes the bell (`read OBJ ?` → `Shape.read`); a package
   that is not the Bell ends it `bellShape notBell` (HOSTING-45).
2. It reads every pending ring in ONE read (`read OBJ pending K`, K = `pending`'s declared limit →
   `Ring.readAll`), lowest first.
3. It acts on `decideTick`:
   - idle: forget a kept answer whose ring is no longer pending;
   - ask (the lowest pending ring, when no kept ring is pending): first keep the intent
     (`(unanswered restart)`) for the ring, keyed by its `(notBefore, ringer)` and its text, then
     one ask on the broker socket; the answer text replaces the intent in `vol/kept-<bell>` before
     the bell sees it (every keep is synced, renamed, directory-synced and read back);
   - answer (the kept ring, still pending): invoke `answer (notBefore, ringText, ringer, text)`
     with the ring's text and the kept answer. The client pins `pending[k]` and `rings[k]` itself
     (`derivePins`); the resident names no pin.
4. The bell taking the answer forgets it. A refusal is judged by the bell's rows: if the ring is
   no longer pending (answered already: the `rings` guard refuses `rung: answered`), the answer is
   forgotten; if it is still pending, the resident ends `answerRefused NAME` (`program` for a
   method's check such as `notResident`, `grantRight` for a grant without the right). The kept
   text stays, so a restart answers again but never asks again.
A transport failure on either socket, and every member reply that did not settle (a describe or read
that did not answer `ok`; an answer that came back neither `ok` nor `refused`: `uncertain`, `behind`,
`declined`, `client`, `open`, `hostContradicted`, `pageIo`; a refusal whose re-read failed), is logged
and retried at the next tick: a not-sent ask costs nothing, a spent one is never repeated. Retries
are bounded: `retriesMax` ticks in a row that ended on one, and the resident ends `retryExhausted`
naming the last (HOSTING-48, `Proofs.ResidentRun.no_refusal_repeats_forever`); any tick that
settles starts the count again. Every line on stderr starts `resident: `.

The tick is a tree of effects (`Prog`, HOSTING-36): `tickP` builds it, `Prog.interp` performs it one
node at a time (HOSTING-37: `interp_runs`). The order above is a theorem over every path of that tree, for every reply the
sockets and the volume could give (`Proofs.ResidentRun.tick_order`, `tick_counts`): at most one
describe, first, and only without a shape; one read; at most one ask, right after the intent was
kept for its ring; at most one invoke, of the kept answer (no ask on that path) or of this tick's
answer right after it replaced the intent.

Limits, named: a ring is its key `(notBefore, ringer)` and its text. Keys are unique among the
bell's rows, but a ringer may re-use its own key once the ring is answered AND retired, by
backdating within the kernel's window; if that happens while the old answer is still kept (its
delivery unsettled) and with the same text, the new ring is answered with the kept text, never
asked twice. A ring that lands below the kept one waits for it (`decide_kept_first`): the queue
order the resident keeps is "lowest first, the ring already asked about before any other", and a
ringer's backdate by up to windowMax heights is the order's slack the resident trusts. A late
landing of an unsettled answer cannot take a re-rung ring at the same key with another text: the
answer carries the ring's text and the `rings` guard refuses it unless that is the ring's own
(`rung: text`, LANG-59); with the same text it is the same-text case above.
`answerRefused` stops the whole queue, not one ring: a restart re-delivers the kept ring first and
stops again until the operator acts. That is right for every refusal known to reach it (the
resident is not the bell's, the grant lacks `invoke:0`, `rings` is full until the owner
retires), each of which refuses every ring alike; a refusal that singled out one ring would block
the rest behind it, and would need a refused marker to skip it. A ring RETIRED between the read and the
answer (LANG-76: anyone may abandon an unanswered ring past its patience) is not one: its answer is
refused, the re-read no longer lists it, and the answer is forgotten and the queue goes on
(`Proofs.ResidentRun.deliver_gone_continues`; resident_bell Q7 runs it). K = `pending`'s limit is complete:
the kernel holds at most `limit` rows (`relationFull`), and a range read past K is refused, never
truncated, so a missed ring would be a failed read (logged, retried), never a silent gap. A not-sent ask whose intent cannot be
forgotten ends `volume`, and the restart answers that ring `(unanswered restart)` without having
asked: one wasted answer, no meter charge.

Provider access uses the installed leaf-SPKI pins and keybroker's provider key file. The repository
ships provider pins in `deploy/tls/pins`; the operator supplies credentials and maintains the
installed policy. A pin or credential failure is answered by name before a successful provider call.
-/

### Hosting/Resident/Sockets.lean
/-
# Hosting.Resident.Sockets — the resident's two sockets, through `Host.Sock` (HOSTING-27)

- The member socket (`nd --stdio` behind nd-supervise, HOSTING-20/25): one command per connection.
  The request frame is `u32le len ‖ Op.command ‖ commandLayout(argv)`, where `len` counts the op byte
  (the client's frame). The reply frame is `u32le len ‖ status ‖ commandReplyLayout`, the `done`
  step's exit, stdout and stderr. Every layout is `Protocol.Table`'s, through the one codec.
- The broker socket (keybroker, HOSTING-12): one ask per connection, `u32le len ‖ ask`, answered
  by `u32le len ‖ answer`. Encoded by `Broker.Wire.encodeAsk` and read by
  `Broker.Wire.decodeAnswer`, the one codec (HOSTING-29: the answer carries text, never a provider
  body).

Every call is bounded by `Host.Sock`'s per-call deadline. Every failure comes back as a value naming
it, never as a thrown error, so the loop decides what each one means.
-/

### Hosting/Resident/Trace.lean
/-
# Hosting.Resident.Trace — the vocabulary of a tick's traces (HOSTING-36)

The effects a tick performs, each with the result it had (`Ev`), and the readings of a trace the
order theorems state (`Proofs.ResidentRun`): the effects on the world (`core`: stderr dropped), a
read's rings, a description's shape, the text an ask's end leaves to keep, the asks, invokes and
describes a trace holds, and the kept answer its keeps and forgets leave. Nothing here performs
anything: the deployed exe never imports it.
-/

### Hosting/Resident/Volume.lean
/-
# Hosting.Resident.Volume — the volume's contract, as a model (HOSTING-37)

What `keep` and `forget` trust of the filesystem, written down: one directory of names over inodes.
It is the CONTRACT, not a proof of the kernel's: the theorems of `Proofs.ResidentVolume` hold for
every filesystem that meets it, over exactly the steps `keepOps` and `forgetOps` perform.

- `create` makes a fresh inode under the name, empty; `write` sets the open inode's contents;
  both leave it unsynced. `sync` (`fsync`) marks the open inode's contents durable.
- `rename a b`, `removeIfExists`: change the live entries. Each value an entry takes is remembered
  (`hist`) until the next `syncDir` (`fsync` on the directory), which makes the live entries the
  only ones a crash can come back with.
- A crash is a RELATION (`Proofs.ResidentVolume.Crash`), not a function: each entry comes back as ANY value it held
  since the last directory sync, independently of the others (a rename's two halves too); a synced
  inode comes back with its contents; an unsynced one with ANY contents, or unreadable. Nothing
  else is assumed: no ordering between a file's data and its entry, no atomicity of rename across
